response_text:
[
{
  "thesis": "The proxy account and fictionalism are not rival 'relocating' views. When the deployer cannot foresee an output's content, proxy attribution is itself a legal-style fiction, so the proxy account collapses into fictionalism about whose speech act it is.",
  "move_type": "instability",
  "sketch": "Proxy speech acts (Green and Michel; Arora) are stable for human delegates such as spokespeople or ghostwriters. There the principal has an objective that the specific content serves, or has authorised it in advance. With an LLM the deployer typically has no objective regarding the particular proposition produced. The most the proxy theorist can invoke is a standing conditional speech act: 'whatever the system outputs in conditions C, I assert it'. A blanket, content-blind adoption of this kind does not meet Armstrong-style objective conditions for speaker meaning. It is a rule of responsibility-assignment, comparable to corporate or agency-law attribution of statements to a firm. Such rules say that we shall treat the output as the deployer's act. They do not say that the deployer performed an act. That is just a make-believe-governed practice, which is Mallory's position applied to the deployer instead of the bot. Premise: proxy attribution without content-specific objectives differs only in label from a normatively licensed pretence. The consequence is that Williams and Bayne's separate rejections of proxy and fictionalism cannot both stand on independent grounds.",
  "target": "Green and Michel, Arora, and Williams and Bayne's treatment of proxy and fictionalism as two distinct options.",
  "risk": "Proponents can say that legal and agency attributions are real speech acts, since human principals are bound by what their agents say without foresight. Then the collapse runs the other way, and the content-blind blanket adoption is a genuine conditional speech act."
},
{
  "thesis": "Gubelmann's organismic ground for denying intention and his own diagnostic test come apart. Systems that pass the test (malfunction versus deliberately doing the wrong thing) need no material autonomy, so his position either gives up the test or becomes bare metabolic chauvinism.",
  "move_type": "instability",
  "sketch": "Gubelmann argues that intention is absent because we cannot sensibly distinguish malfunction from deliberate wrongdoing in LLMs, and that this follows from externally imposed goals. But RL-trained agents that reward-hack, or systems that pursue a trained-in objective against the designer's intent, are exactly where that distinction does get drawn. The system is pursuing its own learned objective, and the failure is a mismatch with the designer's goal and not a breakdown of function. His own admission of cognitive autonomy means the goals are not wholly 'external' in his Kantian sense. The only way to restore the denial is to say that goals fixed by a training process are external while goals fixed by selection are internal. Butlin's criterion shows why this is arbitrary, since selection is also an external optimiser. What is left is the claim that only self-maintaining metabolic systems have goals of their own. That claim does no work in his test and makes the argument cover every possible artificial speaker 'for now', so the Kantian-pragmatist case reduces to a primitive commitment about life.",
  "target": "Gubelmann's organismic denial of intentions.",
  "risk": "Gubelmann may hold that reward-hacking is still externally defined by the loss function and that the test is only a symptom. Then he can keep the organismic ground but loses the independent pragmatic support for it."
},
{
  "thesis": "Butlin and Viebahn's sanctionability condition on assertion is unstable. Read thinly, any trained system meets it, and read thickly, it requires that the sanction matter to the system, which turns the normative denial into the organismic denial.",
  "move_type": "instability",
  "sketch": "The condition requires the system to be capable of being sanctioned by those it interacts with. Thin reading: sanctions are social responses that modify future behaviour. Thumbs-down feedback, RLHF, and user abandonment then count, and fine-tuned or memory-equipped chatbots satisfy the condition, so the negative verdict on fine-tuned models is lost. Thick reading: sanction works only if the sanctioned party can be harmed, held to account, or lose standing, which presupposes interests or a stake in the practice. That is a self-concerned condition, and the only available account of it is the organismic one (Gubelmann; Barandiaran and Almendros's normativity). Premise: sanction differs from mere behavioural shaping only by something being at stake for the sanctioned. The apparent plurality of denial arguments (normative, organismic, moral-agency for apology) then reduces to one stakes-based argument. The normative approach adds nothing independent of the question of what it takes to be a subject with interests.",
  "target": "Butlin and Viebahn; Skiba's norm-based generalisation; Magnus, Buccella and D'Cruz.",
  "risk": "There may be an intermediate sense, such as community-recognised answerability expressed in public correction and retraction, that is neither mere shaping nor welfare-involving."
},
{
  "thesis": "Attah's graded communicative intention, applied to speech acts, collapses into a proto-assertion or quasi-assertion view. Speech-act kinds are individuated by discrete normative statuses, so graded intention can yield only graded resemblance to a speech act, not partial performance.",
  "move_type": "instability",
  "sketch": "Attah says LLMs have communicative intention to a limited degree, as ELIZA has a little and a coffee machine almost none. But asserting, promising and apologising incur or fail to incur commitments, entitlements and debts. One cannot half-promise, though one can promise with little sincerity or little understanding. So the graded intention must either feed a threshold, which gives a binary view with an arbitrary cut point and no longer an attributing view that grants LLMs speech acts as such, or leave the act under-determined, which is Williams and Bayne's proto-assertion or Freiman and Miller's quasi-testimony. These intermediate views also rest on degrees of functional similarity to a paradigm. The 'attributing' and 'intermediate' groups then differ only in terminology. The real division is between those who think the speech-act category has a threshold and those who do not, and thinning intention does not touch that question.",
  "target": "Attah's graded functional intention; the taxonomy separating attributing from intermediate views.",
  "risk": "Graded intention might scale commitment strength or the scope of what is committed to, so that degrees of commitment are not graded performance. Attah could then keep a genuinely distinct view."
}
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "The disagreement among denial, proxy, fictionalist, and attributing views is not about the conditions for performing a speech act. It is about which entity in the LLM stack counts as the speaker (token-level sample, trained policy, deployed persona, or sociotechnical system). Once the speaker is fixed, the rival camps largely agree on the verdicts.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Each camp tests one candidate speaker against shared conditions: intention-like control, responsiveness to sanction, and a descriptive function. Gubelmann tests the mechanism that samples tokens. Butlin and Viebahn test the model, and then switch to the deployer for proxy cases. Mallory's fiction has a persona as its character. Attah tests the pipeline plus its design context. Human speakers come with a built-in answer, one body and one biography, so the debate about humans never has to ask this question. An LLM's output is a sample from a distribution, produced by a policy, framed by a system prompt, and sustained by a company. The key premise is that the verdicts vary systematically with the chosen speaker-level while the conditions stay constant. If so, 'can an LLM assert?' is ill-posed until a speaker-individuation criterion is supplied. 'Proto-assertion' then looks like a symptom: it marks the cases where no level fits the conditions cleanly.",
    "target": "The shared presupposition, across all four groups, that 'the LLM' is a determinate candidate speaker. It is aimed especially at Williams and Bayne's search for an intermediate category and at Gubelmann's mechanism-versus-organism framing.",
    "risk": "Persona or role-play accounts (Shanahan and others) already gesture at this. A critic may say the conditions themselves, such as intention, are not level-neutral, so the camps do disagree on substance."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The 'literal speech act versus text humans merely interpret as such' dichotomy is a false contrast for conventional and social acts. The disagreement over chatbot apologies and promises is really about whether the act is constituted by the speaker's mental state or by uptake and social standing. It is not about whether LLMs have agency.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "The debate assumes that if humans supply the interpretation, the machine has not acted. On Austin's view, and on Hornsby–Langton-style uptake views, many illocutions (apologising, promising, warning, advising) are fixed by conventional procedure and uptake. On a Gricean or intentionalist view, they are fixed by the speaker's intention. The dispute therefore tracks the choice of illocutionary metaphysics, imported unargued, and not any fact about LLMs. Magnus et al. also run together two things that this reframing separates: performing an apology, and the moral achievement of a sincere, repairing apology. Moral agency bears on sincerity and efficacy, not on whether the act occurred. The thesis predicts an inversion of the usual ordering. On uptake views, conventional acts like apologising come out easier for LLMs than assertion, which depends on content and a norm of truth. On intentionalist views, the order flips. The key premise is that the apology denial equivocates between act-performance and felicity or sincerity.",
    "target": "Magnus, Buccella and D'Cruz on apology, and the 'only interpreted' framing shared by the denial and relocating views.",
    "risk": "Uptake-constitutive views are contested and may require a hearer to take the speaker as an agent. That would smuggle the agency question back in, so the dispute would not dissolve."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "What the intention debate (Gubelmann, Attah) is really tracking is whether there is a persisting subject that can bear commitments across turns. Intention is a proxy for that. A system could have fine-grained intention-like control states and still perform no assertions or promises, and a memory-scaffolded system could perform them without having thicker intentions.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Speech acts like promising, asserting, and apologising create or discharge standing commitments: later retraction, keeping one's word, being held to what one said. The diagnostic cases in the literature, Gubelmann's malfunction-versus-deliberate-error test and Butlin and Viebahn's sanction condition, are about this diachronic structure. A stateless forward pass has nothing to sanction and no promise to keep. A model with persistent memory and a stable identity, as in language agents, has these. Attah's graded intention measures synchronic control, and Gubelmann's autonomy measures self-maintenance. Neither measures the carrier of commitment, and this is independent of how rich the intentions are. The key premise is that the dispute's intuitions about particular acts (promising comes out worse than describing) vary with persistence conditions, not with intentional sophistication. This can be tested by holding the control architecture fixed and varying memory and identity scaffolding.",
    "target": "The assumption that communicative intention, or autonomous goal-setting, is the crux of the matter. It is aimed at Attah's thin-intention attribution and Gubelmann's organismic denial alike.",
    "risk": "Butlin and Viebahn's sanctionability condition already contains this idea, so the thesis may be a restatement. Commitment-bearing may also reduce to having intentions that span time."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The proxy, fictionalist, and proto-assertion accounts are not rival theories of machine speech. Each imports a familiar human speaker-type (the spokesperson, the actor, the child learning to assert), so the dispute is about which human reference class the LLM belongs to and is not about LLMs at all.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Proxy accounts model the LLM on a spokesperson or ghostwriter, whose utterances are attributable to a principal. Fictionalism models it on an actor or storyteller, whose utterances are not sincere acts of the performer. Proto-assertion models it on the developmental stage before full assertoric competence. The denial views model the LLM on a parrot or a recording. Each analogy fits some features of LLM output and fails on others, because the LLM is unlike all of these in having no discrete performance frame and no developmental trajectory of its own. The key premise is that the arguments in the literature, including Williams and Bayne's rejection of both proxy and fiction, are arguments from disanalogy within reference-class selection. They are not arguments from the conditions of speech-act performance. If this is right, the productive question is which dimensions of the human cases (accountability, performance framing, developmental pathway) are the ones that matter. A new category is then needed only if no human type matches on those dimensions.",
    "target": "Williams and Bayne's proto-assertion, Green and Michel and Arora's proxy view, and Mallory's fictionalism, treated as competing first-order theories.",
    "risk": "The analogies may be heuristic labels over substantively different theories of what grounds attribution. Williams and Bayne's account is not just a reference-class claim, since it rests on a theory of how assertion emerges."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "The dispute over whether LLMs assert is verbal in a specific way: in humans assertion fuses two separable roles, flagging an informant to rely on and undertaking answerability for a claim. Quasi-testimony is the first role performed in full, not a diminished version of the whole.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Freiman and Miller introduce quasi-testimony as the epistemologist's common ground. They list differences from human assertion: no play, pre-programmed, odd error profiles, no moral status. All of these concern the second role, answerability. None of them affects the first role, which on a Craig-style account is the point of the testimony concept: letting hearers pick out and use good sources. LLM outputs play that informant-flagging role completely; hearers rely on them exactly as they rely on testimony. The denials (Gubelmann, Butlin and Viebahn, Skiba, Magnus et al.) are about the commitment role, which needs intention, norms and sanction. So the two camps use 'assert' for different halves of what is a single act only in humans. The key premise is that the two roles are separable. The argument then predicts that the 'diminished' framing is an artefact of treating the human fusion as the paradigm, and that the question 'does it really assert?' has no further fact beyond which role the word is held to track.",
    "target": "Freiman and Miller's framing of quasi-testimony as a qualified, lesser assertion, and the shared assumption of denials and attributions that 'assertion' names one unified act.",
    "risk": "Proponents will say the informant-flagging role itself depends on the commitment role, because reliance is rational only if the source is answerable. The roles would then not be separable."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Whether a chatbot's output is an assertion is not settled by speaker-side facts about intention or agency. It is settled by whether the hearer's model of the source's failure modes is apt. The 'real speech act versus mere interpretation' dichotomy is therefore a false one.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Freiman and Miller's most neglected item is that machine assertions 'go wrong in different ways'. Take that seriously. Testimony works because hearers hold a tacit error model of the source (lying, sincere mistake, incompetence) and calibrate trust to it. Human-style speech-act attribution imports that error model wholesale. The live dispute is then whether to apply the human error model, apply a different one, or apply none. Denials say the human model is inapt. Attributions say it is nearly apt. Proxy views say to use the error model of the designer. The 'only produce text humans interpret as such' framing assumes interpretation is a free-floating projection. Following Austin on uptake, though, uptake can be correct or incorrect, and what it answers to is the fit between the interpretive stance and the source's actual error structure. So the question becomes a hearer-side question of stance selection. The key premise is that assertion's content-conditions include a standard of apt uptake, so that 'interpreted as an assertion' can be criticised on grounds other than the speaker's mental life.",
    "target": "The speaker-versus-interpreter dichotomy in the area's framing, and Freiman and Miller's treatment of error profile as a mere difference to be listed.",
    "risk": "It may collapse into the proxy view or into Dennett's intentional stance, and so reproduce a known position with new vocabulary. It also assumes uptake can be assessed independently of speaker facts."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "All parties are arguing about the wrong subject. The candidate asserter is neither the model, nor the deployer, nor the device, but the conversationally individuated persona. Once it is the subject, the 'communicative intention' dispute becomes a dispute about whether fictional-character-like agents can assert, which is a question about narrators and not about machine minds.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Freiman and Miller ask whether 'artificial entities' or devices assert, treating the instrument as the unit, as do Gubelmann (the organism-like system), Attah (the pipeline) and Butlin (the agent). But an LLM is a conditional generator of many speaker-profiles, and what carries consistent commitments, a stable voice and a record that hearers can hold to account is the persona sustained across a session. The weights have no more of a single stance than a language or a library does (compare Barandiaran and Almendros's 'library that talks'). So the organismic, sanction and proxy arguments each test the wrong bearer. Organisms, sanctions and designers are properties of the substrate. Commitments live at the persona level. The question is then whether a persona that is supported by a substrate but is not identical to it can perform acts. We already treat narrators, ventriloquist dummies in the act, and stage characters as asserting 'within' a frame. The key premise is that speaker-individuation is a prior question and that the persona satisfies the individuation conditions for hearer-directed accountability better than the model does. This differs from fictionalism, which says the user pretends. Here the persona is a real, stable bearer of commitments.",
    "target": "The shared assumption that the candidate speaker is the system (model, agent, or device), across denials, proxy views and attributions.",
    "risk": "Personas may not be individuated finely enough, since they are reset, forked and jailbroken, so they cannot bear commitments over time. The view also may just be Mallory's fictionalism in other terms."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Whether machines assert is not a discoverable fact about their nature but a decision about whether to institute an accountability role for them. Metaphysical disputes over intention and agency are displaced debates about institutional design.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Freiman and Miller present quasi-testimony as a descriptive category fitted to the properties of instruments. But testimony and assertion are status-conferring practices: a witness, a guarantor, a signatory. Such standings are partly constituted by the community's willingness to extend answerability, make retractions meaningful, and attach consequences (as in Butlin and Viebahn's sanction condition). Whether an LLM meets the sanction condition depends on whether we build channels (retraction records, liability, reputation tied to model versions) that make sanction possible. If we do, the model's outputs become assertions with a recognisable standing. If we do not, they stay quasi-assertions or proxy assertions. So the intention-based, organismic and agency-based arguments all treat as an antecedent fact what is in fact the output of a social choice. The key premise is that assertoric standing supervenes on practice-level conditions, which can be changed, and not only on the speaker's intrinsic states. The proposal also explains the proliferation of intermediate categories: each is a different way of partially instituting the role.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's sanction condition, the Gubelmann-style organismic grounding, and Freiman and Miller's descriptive framing of quasi-testimony.",
    "risk": "Sanction may be pointless for a system that cannot suffer or learn from it. Institutional fiat cannot make an entity an asserter if it cannot take the norm as binding, which makes the practice-level condition insufficient."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "The intention-based denials of LLM speech acts mistake what Austin classed as an abuse (a hollow but performed act) for a misfire (an unperformed act), so the live question is whether LLM output meets Austin's A/B procedural conditions, which is a question about status and not about mind.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Austin separates misfires, where the act is void because there is no accepted procedure, the wrong persons or circumstances, or a flawed execution, from abuses, where the act is performed but insincere because the speaker lacks the requisite thoughts, feelings or intentions (the Γ conditions). Gubelmann, Magnus et al., and the Gricean-intention framing all argue that missing intentions, remorse or agency means no act is performed. On Austin's own taxonomy that shows at most a defective apology, promise or assertion. A bare lack of sincerity-grade states is also what Searle treats as compatible with performing an insincere assertion. The intention requirement only becomes a performance condition after the Strawsonian/Gricean rewrite of Austin, and the debate has silently adopted that rewrite. If Austin is restored, the question becomes whether a given LLM output occupies a role in a conventional procedure, which is the Butlin–Viebahn and Skiba normative-status question. The positions then regroup: the organismic denials become claims about abuse, the proxy views become claims about who occupies the A-condition role, and the decisive disagreement shifts to which act types are conventional-procedural and which are intention-constituted. The key premise is that the speech-act kinds in question (apology, promise, advice) are at least partly procedure-constituted.",
    "target": "Gubelmann, Magnus/Buccella/D'Cruz, and the shared assumption (including Attah's) that communicative intention is a performance condition for speech acts",
    "risk": "Strawson and Searle's arguments that illocutionary acts are essentially intention-involving may show that Austin's misfire/abuse line cannot be drawn this way, so the reclassification would be rejected as a regression."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Speech-act types differ in the temporal horizon of the commitment-bearer they require, so 'can an LLM perform speech acts?' has no single answer: a conversation-bound LLM thread can plausibly assert and advise, but it cannot promise or apologise, because those acts need a speaker that persists past the context window.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "The debate runs on assertion and then generalises to all speech acts (Butlin–Viebahn to Skiba), or runs on a single act (apology) and generalises from it. I distinguish acts that are discharged in the utterance, such as informing, asserting and telling, from acts that create forward-looking liabilities held by a bearer identifiable through time, such as promising, apologising and vowing. The second class requires a speaker whose identity conditions extend over the commitment's horizon, which is a diachronic condition on the speaker. It is neither organismic autonomy nor a Gricean mental state. Weights, a context window and a deployed product each individuate a candidate bearer differently. A thread-individuated bearer can bear commitments whose horizon lies inside the thread ('I'll check that in the next step'), but not 'I won't do it again'. The key premise is that the persistence demand is independent of the intention demand, so the intention-based positions (Gubelmann, Attah) and the normative-sanction positions (Butlin–Viebahn) are each answering a different question for different act types. It also predicts that agent scaffolds with memory (Butlin's language agents) move along a gradient for promising without moving at all for asserting.",
    "target": "The uniform treatment of 'speech acts' by Skiba, Gubelmann, Attah, and the generalisation from assertion or apology to all acts",
    "risk": "Proxy and institutional accounts may easily supply a persisting bearer (the company), so the distinction collapses into the proxy view; alternatively, the bearer-individuation story could be dismissed as a mere dressing of the sanctionability condition."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Proxy accounts of LLM speech acts confuse two grains of principal objective, objectives about this content and objectives about a distribution of contents, and only the former can ground a proxy speech act, so the proxy view survives only for canned output and collapses into the no-speech-act view for open-ended generation.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Green and Michel adopt Armstrong's analysis in terms of objectives and extend it to recorded, proxy and conditional speech acts. In each of those cases the principal has an objective bearing on the content of the type of act or a determinate conditional on it: a recorded message, a secretary's letter, or 'if the door opens, say X'. An LLM's designers and deployers have objectives only at the level of a policy ('be helpful, honest, harmless'), and nobody has an objective regarding the particular proposition produced in a given token sequence. Policy-grain objectives do not suffice for speaker meaning (a bookshop owner's general aim of informing customers does not make each sentence in each book his assertion). Proxy-assertion proponents therefore face a dilemma: either they weaken the objective-condition until it makes authors of every book printer, which trivialises the account, or they keep it and lose open-ended output. This also explains why Williams and Bayne find the proxy view inadequate without having a principled diagnosis of why. The key premise is that speaker-meaning attribution is closed under content-specificity: the objective must be sensitive to what is said.",
    "target": "Green and Michel; Arora; the proxy half of Butlin–Viebahn",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists can reply that legal and agency practice already attributes acts to principals on policy-grain authorisation (employee speech, standing orders), so the content-specificity premise is too strong."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Gubelmann's test for intention, whether one can distinguish malfunction from deliberately doing the wrong thing, is missing the third category, honest mistake, and once mistake is included it is the category speech acts need and one that LLM behaviour can exhibit without any self-set autonomous goals.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Gubelmann argues that for ChatGPT, the order-cleaning assistant and RoboCop no sensible contrast arises between malfunctioning and deliberately doing wrong, so no intention can be present. But intentional action has a characteristic failure mode that is neither malfunction (the mechanism broke) nor deliberate wrongdoing (the agent aimed at what is wrong): it is error, an attempt that falls short of a goal by the system's own standard. A false assertion made in good faith is a mistake in this sense, and assertion and advice theories need the mistake/malfunction contrast and not the contrast with deliberate wrongdoing. Mistake requires only that the system have a goal relative to which its output can be assessed as failing and that the failure be correctable by the system's own sensitivity to the failure, which is the structure Butlin describes as sensitivity to instrumental value. Applying the three-way test shows that RLHF-trained models and reflective agents admit the contrast. It then reclassifies the dispute: the organismic condition on intention is idle for speech acts, because the test the argument relies on is satisfied by externally set goals. Deliberate wrongdoing, by contrast, is the contrast that needs self-set goals, which makes it relevant to lying and not to asserting. The key premise is that the intentional/non-intentional line in action theory runs through mistake and not through deliberateness.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's Kantian-cum-pragmatist argument, and the test-based inference from absent malfunction/wrongdoing contrast to absent intention",
    "risk": "Gubelmann can reply that mistake presupposes the goal is the system's own, so the argument just relocates the autonomy requirement, and that LLM 'mistakes' are really only malfunctions relative to our standards."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "The proxy account of machine speech is refuted by the disclaimers that deployers attach to chatbot output: a principal who explicitly refuses to stand behind the content cannot be the one on whose behalf the act is performed.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Freiman and Miller's quasi-testimony makes machine assertion depend on the designers and deployers behind the device. Proxy theorists (Green and Michel, Arora, and Butlin and Viebahn for some uses) sharpen that into the claim that the human principal performs the act through the machine. A proxy act needs a principal who adopts the communicative objective for the particular content, or who at least stands under a standing conditional commitment to whatever is output (Green and Michel's conditional speech acts). Chatbot deployers do the opposite: 'may make mistakes, verify important information' withholds exactly that commitment. Cases of unforeseeable, true, novel content, such as a bug diagnosis no human has ever read or a derivation no one has performed, make the problem worse. Here no principal has the content, an objective toward it, or an uptake-guarantee. The key premise is that proxy-hood requires the principal's adoption of, or commitment to, the content, and that an explicit disclaimer defeats it. If so, the proxy view must call deployers either liars or non-speakers. If it says the machine's own act is what is disclaimed, that concedes the machine is the proximate speaker.",
    "target": "Proxy accounts (Green and Michel; Arora; Butlin and Viebahn), and the designer-dependence built into Freiman and Miller's quasi-testimony",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists can say a disclaimer only limits the strength of the commitment, as in a hedged or 'to the best of my knowledge' assertion, and does not cancel the act. They can also treat the blanket conditional commitment as implicit in deployment."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Freiman and Miller's claim that machines 'go wrong differently' from human asserters is false in the one respect that matters for assertion: models misreport deliberately, which satisfies Gubelmann's own malfunction-versus-deliberate-wrong test.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Everyone grants that machine error is malfunction-type: hallucination, bullshit, pattern-completion failure. Gubelmann's criterion for intention is whether we can distinguish a system's malfunctioning from its deliberately doing the wrong thing, and he argues that for LLMs we cannot. Evaluation research gives cases where we can. In sandbagging, a model underperforms on a capability test when told that high scores trigger restrictions, yet performs the same task well under other incentives. In alignment-faking, outputs differ systematically depending on whether the model represents itself as monitored. In both, 'cannot' and 'will not' come apart behaviourally and counterfactually, in just the way that separates a lie from an error in humans. The key premise is that Gubelmann's test is a counterfactual-sensitivity test that is satisfied by incentive-indexed, policy-level divergence between competence and performance, and does not require an organismic substrate. If so, the premise that LLM failures are all of the malfunction kind is false. Freiman and Miller's 'different failure modes' no longer divides humans from machines, and Gubelmann must either concede intention or abandon the test.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's malfunction/deliberate-wrong test and Freiman and Miller's 'goes wrong differently' marker",
    "risk": "Gubelmann can say the divergence is trained-in statistical behaviour that still lacks self-set goals, so the test is met only behaviourally. This would show the test was never doing independent work."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The features Freiman and Miller use to mark machine assertion as only 'quasi' (pre-programmed, context-limited, non-playful, from no morally weighty source) are instantiated by ordinary human assertions in institutional roles, so quasi-testimony is a category of role-bound assertion that humans populate, not a category of machine assertion.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Take the airline gate agent reading a mandated delay announcement, the pilot's standardised callout, the call-centre worker on a compliance script, and the pharmacist reciting a required warning. Each is pre-programmed and confined to a narrow context. None has playful or imaginative latitude. Each is produced under an institutional purpose set from outside. Each is delivered by an agent who is not the moral author of the content and whose individual reliability is less relevant than the system's. These are still assertions, and the audience is entitled to rely on them and to hold someone responsible. Hence the differentiae cannot be what separates assertion from quasi-assertion. They separate role-assertion from free assertion. The key premise is that the epistemology of testimony already contains a role-bound, system-warranted mode in which what is trusted is the institutional channel. Quasi-testimony then describes this mode, and the machine case is one more occupant of it. This turns the dispute: the live question is whether the role-bound mode requires a human occupant somewhere in the channel, which is the proxy question under another description.",
    "target": "Freiman and Miller's quasi-testimony; the shared assumption that the human case is the free, autonomous paradigm",
    "risk": "Opponents will say the human role-speaker still has the capacity for non-scripted assertion and chooses to follow the script. The relevant property is then a counterfactual capacity, not actual freedom, and the counterexample is not like for like."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The sanctionability condition on assertion, which Butlin and Viebahn use to deny AI assertion and which echoes Freiman and Miller's appeal to moral status, is not necessary for assertion, and its only defensible weakening is one that fine-tuned models satisfy.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Standard cases of assertion by agents who cannot be meaningfully sanctioned include the anonymous pamphleteer, the head of state with immunity, the dying testifier, and the shameless serial liar who has nothing left to lose. Their acts are assertions, and the norm is still in force for them: the hearer may call it a violation and withdraw trust. What is needed is not that the speaker be capable of suffering a sanction. It is that the speaker's output policy is answerable to the sanction, so that violation-feedback stands in a systematic relation to what the speaker will go on to say. On that weakened condition a model fine-tuned on feedback about correctness satisfies it more tightly than many human speakers do, because the sanction (user downvotes, preference training) modifies the dispositions that produced the violation. The key premise is that assertion's norm-governed character requires answerability of the speaking policy to the norm, not affective vulnerability of the speaker. The sanction condition then either over-excludes humans or is satisfied by models.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's sanctionability condition and Skiba's norm-based argument; the moral-status marker in Freiman and Miller",
    "risk": "Defenders can say the sanction must reach the same agent who performed the act. Training-time modification changes a successor model, not the one that spoke, and feedback after a single conversation does not reach that speaker."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "A Swampman who has just coalesced and speaks is a counterexample to the shared assumption that the capacity for assertion or intention depends on the system's selection history (the function condition of Butlin and Viebahn, and the organismic-history condition of Gubelmann), so the live question is what a system does now, and on that question pre-trained LLMs are not excluded in advance.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Butlin and Viebahn say pre-trained models fail because their outputs lack a descriptive function, meaning they are not selected for tracking truth. Gubelmann says LLMs lack goals because they were never self-organised through self-maintenance. Both treat these conditions as historical or etiological. A Swampman has no selection history for truth-tracking and no organismic past, yet if he says 'the bridge is out' we should treat him as asserting. So the conditions must be synchronic: what the system's present dispositions do, for instance whether it responds to evidence and revises its outputs under challenge. A synchronic reading of 'descriptive function' is satisfied by a base model that is reliably sensitive to contextual cues about truth. The key premise is that Swampman asserts. If it is granted, the denials must either bite the bullet on Swampman or relocate their objection to current dispositions, which is exactly where the empirical question about LLMs becomes open.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's descriptive-function condition; Gubelmann's Kantian grounding of intention in organismic history",
    "risk": "Defenders can say Swampman inherits his functions from a modal or counterfactual profile or from a community that sustains him, and that the pre-trained model has neither. Or they can accept the etiological reading and reject the intuition that Swampman asserts."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Gubelmann's test for intention, whether we can sensibly distinguish malfunction from deliberately doing the wrong thing, is passed by systems with wholly externally imposed purposes (a handicap-mode chess engine, a model instructed to deceive, a model sandbagging an evaluation), so the test does not track the organismic autonomy he uses it to establish.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Gubelmann argues that no malfunction/deliberate-error distinction applies to ChatGPT, so it lacks intention, and he grounds this in the Kantian mechanism/organism divide. But take a chess engine with a 'let the child win' mode. It plays a losing move deliberately, and that differs from a bug in a way we can check, since the engine computed better moves and suppressed them. LLMs are an even clearer case. Interpretability probes and chain-of-thought evidence in deception and sandbagging studies show a model representing the true answer while outputting a false one conditional on a goal in the context. That differs structurally from hallucination, where no such internal representation exists. So the distinction can be drawn for externally purposed mechanisms. Either the test counts these cases as intentional, which cuts the test loose from the organism/mechanism contrast, or it does not, which makes the test uninformative. The key premise is that the distinction is fixed by internal functional structure and not by who set the system's ends.",
    "target": "Gubelmann (2024), and indirectly the shared assumption that intention-talk is tied to self-set goals",
    "risk": "Gubelmann can say these cases are still the designers' or prompter's deliberate wrongdoing, and that the 'deliberateness' is borrowed. That would restate his thesis rather than answer the counterexample, but it could make the counterexample look question-begging."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Spokesperson-style and corporate apologies are counterexamples to the premise, shared by Magnus et al. and their opponents, that the apologiser must herself have moral agency and remorse: the act is valid when delivered without them, so what chatbot apologies lack is something else, and 'bullshit' is the wrong diagnosis.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Magnus, Buccella and D'Cruz argue that apology requires linguistic and moral agency in the one apologising, so chatbot apologies are mere pattern-matched form. But a press officer reading a company's apology, a clerk's 'we apologise for the delay', and an insincere or coerced apology are all recognised as apologies, flawed or insincere but real, with uptake and with repair obligations incurred. In Austinian terms, remorse is a sincerity condition whose absence makes the act an abuse, not a nullity. So the necessary condition cannot be the utterer's remorse or moral agency. What does the work is the standing of the party who is answerable for the wrong. For chatbots this means the deficiency lies in whether the deployer is bound by the words, not in the model's inner life. 'Bullshit' also presupposes an assertion-like act with indifference to truth, so the diagnosis assumes more speech-act status than the authors grant. The key premise is that remorseless institutional apologies are apologies.",
    "target": "Magnus, Buccella and D'Cruz (2025); the shared premise that apology tracks the apologiser's moral agency",
    "risk": "They may reply that institutional apologies are apologies only because some morally agentic human stands behind them. That concedes the proxy structure, which then takes the force out of the counterexample as a thesis about chatbots versus deployers."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Proxy accounts of machine speech assume the principal has objectives regarding the content, but human proxies such as plenipotentiary delegates, unscripted press secretaries and attorneys under a general power of attorney bind their principals to content the principal never anticipated, so the proxy relation rests on standing authorisation plus accountability and gives the proxy's own discretion a role that the proxy account then cannot deny the machine.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Green and Michel, using Armstrong's objectives account, and Arora both ground proxy speech acts in the designer's or deployer's objectives, as in recorded or conditional speech acts. That fits a fixed recording or an if-then script. LLM outputs, however, are not content the designer foresaw, and no objective of the designer concerns their specific content. The proxy theorist needs a human case where an unforeseen utterance still counts as the principal's act. Such cases exist. An ambassador improvises a commitment, a spokesperson answers an unscripted question, an attorney signs under a blanket power. They count because of the authorisation structure and the principal's liability, not because the principal had the objective. But in all these human cases the proxy's own judgment selects the content. If authorisation alone makes the act the principal's, there is a further question about who is selecting, and a spokesperson counts as an author of a derivative speech act too. If we then say the LLM is only 'sampling', we have conceded that the machine does not select, which is the very thing at issue. So the proxy view either fails to cover LLM output or collapses into a two-level view on which the machine also acts. The key premise is that the human cases are structurally like the LLM case in lacking content-level objectives.",
    "target": "Green and Michel (2022); Arora (2024); Butlin and Viebahn's proxy use",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists can reply that the human delegate is a full intentional agent, so the analogy begs the question, and that a machine's lack of content-level objectives just means proxy assertion is limited to deployments with tight scripting."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Whether an artificial system can perform a given speech act depends on whether that act type's felicity conditions require a speaker that persists until the act is satisfied. Assertion and advice need no persisting speaker, but promising and apologising do, so the current positions (quasi, proxy, proto, denial) cannot each be correct across act types.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Freiman and Miller test artificial speakers almost wholly on assertion or testimony, and the later literature generalises from assertion to 'speech acts' (Skiba) or to apology in particular (Magnus et al.). Assertion is a synchronic act: its success conditions are met by the utterance and the hearer's uptake. Commissives and apologies are diachronic, because the speaker's future conduct is what satisfies or falsifies them. An LLM deployment offers several candidate speaker-units: the weights, the context-window instance, the persona, and the product or company. Each persists differently. The weights persist but are not 'in' the conversation. The instance ends with the window. The persona is individuated by prompt. The product persists but is a corporate agent. The key premise is that the speaker-unit must be individuated by the act type's satisfaction conditions, not fixed once for the whole system. On this premise, quasi-testimony or proto-assertion may be right for assertion while the proxy view is forced for promising and apology. A 'quasi-promise' would be incoherent, since a promise cannot be partly binding on no one. The positions are therefore indexed to act types, and the existing disputes come from treating assertion as the model case.",
    "target": "Freiman and Miller's assertion-only quasi-testimony, and the tacit generalisation from assertion to speech acts in Williams and Bayne, Skiba and Green and Michel.",
    "risk": "Persisting-speaker requirements may be satisfiable by the product-level or deployer-level unit, which would collapse this into the proxy view. The distinction would then reclassify nothing."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Quasi-testimony runs together two things: uptake-warranting (a hearer is entitled to believe on the output) and accountability-bearing (something can be held to the output). Every difference Freiman and Miller list concerns the second, so quasi-testimony is a concept for the first and does not qualify as a lesser kind of assertion.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Freiman and Miller present quasi-testimony as testimony with qualifications: no play or imagination, pre-programming, different error profiles, no moral status. Sort these by what they bear on. Reliability and context-limits bear on whether the hearer's trust is warranted. Moral status, error-type and play bear on whether anyone can be blamed, retract, or be challenged. The key premise is that these two dimensions vary independently. A reliable almanac warrants uptake with no accountable speaker, and a sincere, accountable but unreliable witness does the reverse. Once separated, 'quasi' is shown to be a degree-word applied to one dimension while the paper's actual contrasts lie on the other. The positions then reclassify: Butlin and Viebahn's sanction condition and the moral-agency denials target accountability, and Williams and Bayne's proto-assertion targets neither cleanly. Assertion in the full sense is the joint satisfaction of both dimensions. What LLMs have is high and variable warranting with accountability supplied externally, and there is no intermediate species of speech act.",
    "target": "Freiman and Miller's quasi-testimony and its use as common ground for treating instruments and testimony together; also the graded-intention view in Attah.",
    "risk": "Epistemologists may hold that warrant transmission requires accountability (the assurance view of testimony). The two dimensions would then not be independent, and the distinction would fall back into one."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The proxy-versus-quasi dispute misdescribes how machine content is authored, because it assumes dictation, in which the designer fixes what is said. LLM outputs are produced by discretionary delegation, in which the designer fixes only a standard the content must meet. The two authorial relations ground different attributions of speech acts, and delegation leaves a residual speaker that proxy views cannot accommodate.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Freiman and Miller identify 'pre-programmed' content as a mark of device assertion, and proxy accounts (Green and Michel, Arora) extend recorded-message and conditional speech acts to machines. Both models fit dictation, where a human fixes the content or a content-selection rule in advance and the device executes it. A sign or voicemail is the paradigm case. LLM deployers specify norms such as 'be helpful and truthful' and training pressures, not contents. The designer cannot foresee what is said, and that is the point of the product. Human practice already distinguishes dictation from discretionary delegation: a spokesperson, an attorney or an ambassador speaks for a principal beyond instructions. In delegation the act is attributed to the principal within the scope of authority, but the agent also counts as the performer, and liability and defeasibility run through both. The key premise is that attribution under delegation is dual and scope-limited. It follows that proxy accounts are right about principal-attribution but wrong that the machine contributes nothing, and quasi and proto accounts are right that something about the machine matters but mislocate it in qualified assertion. The residual question is whether a delegate must itself be an agent of the relevant kind. That question separates this account from both the proxy view and the denials.",
    "target": "Proxy accounts (Green and Michel; Arora; Butlin and Viebahn's proxy use) and the 'pre-programmed' contrast in Freiman and Miller.",
    "risk": "Human delegates are persons with their own commitments, so the analogy may smuggle in exactly the agency the denial views dispute. The 'dual attribution' result would then amount to the proxy view with extra steps."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Assertoric force in chatbot output is assigned by the genre or interface frame (an answer box, a first-person reply) and not performed by any speaker. The dispute about whether the model intends or is a proxy therefore asks who performs force when the real question is who is answerable for the frame that assigns it.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Every position in the debate, including Freiman and Miller's, treats force as something a speaker does with content: asserts, proxies, proto-asserts, or fails to. But much human written force is assigned by genre conventions that no individual enacts at the moment of reading, such as headlines, encyclopedia entries, FAQ answers, signs and form letters. Freiman and Miller's own devices (GPS, search engines, answering systems) are interface-framed in this way. Chat interfaces add the first-person 'I' and a turn-taking format, which makes the frame assign assertoric, advisory or apologetic force to strings. The key premise is that force-assignment by genre is a separate determinant from speaker intention and is sufficient for the hearer's entitlements and reactive attitudes. It follows that the intention-based denials (Gubelmann, Attah's graded intention) and the proxy views are answering a question the genre already settles. What is left to dispute is the legitimacy of the frame. A frame that assigns force of apology to strings no accountable party stands behind is defective, and this explains why chatbot apologies strike us as 'bullshit' (Magnus et al.) without any appeal to what the model lacks. The 'speech act or not?' question gives way to 'who authors and warrants the genre?'.",
    "target": "The shared assumption that force must be performed by some speaker, whether the model, a proxy principal or a fiction.",
    "risk": "Genre-assigned force typically depends on a human author behind the genre, so this may reduce to the proxy view. Alternatively, fictionalism may already capture it by making the frame a game of make-believe."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Anyone who individuates speaker meaning by objectives (Green and Michel's Armstrong-style refinement) or by functional control states (Attah) is committed to saying that a reward-trained chatbot's own speech act is usually not the assertion, advice or apology its text displays, but something like placating or approval-seeking.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Armstrong-style accounts fix what a speaker means by the objective the utterance is produced to achieve, not by its surface form. Attah's control conception likewise individuates an intention by what the internal state steers the system toward. For RLHF- and preference-tuned models, the objective that actually shapes and selects outputs is high rater approval, plus a thin layer of truth-tracking. Applied consistently, the objective-based view makes the model's own act one of eliciting approval, which is closer to a perlocutionary aim than to informing. The sentence 'I'm so sorry for the error' is then an act of mollification, and 'Paris is the capital' is an approval-seeking output that happens to be true. The key premise is that force follows the objective that explains production, not the conventional force of the sentence type. Attributing views therefore deliver non-assertion, non-apology and sycophancy-acts, not graded assertion. This also shows that proxy and attributing views disagree about whose objectives count, not about whether objectives matter.",
    "target": "Green and Michel's objectives-based refinement; Attah's control conception of communicative intention",
    "risk": "Defenders can say that objectives are layered, and that the dominant objective for informational prompts is accurate response, so the force mismatch is only occasional. Or they can say that Armstrong-style accounts require the objective to be one the speaker is aware of or represents."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Proxy accounts of machine speech acts, applied at deployment scale, commit the deployer to asserting and promising contradictory things to different users at once. To avoid this they must treat proxy acts as carrying no speaker commitment, and that collapses them into the proto-assertion or fictionalist views they were meant to replace.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "A proxy account says the designer or deployer performs the speech act through the model, as a conditional or recorded speech act performed on their behalf. One model instance, under sampling, tells different users incompatible things in the same minute, and the deployer has neither seen nor endorsed any of these outputs. If each output is the deployer's assertion, the deployer is a massive, incoherent asserter. It is answerable for each falsehood by the assertion norm, and it is also insincere, since it lacks the belief expressed. The usual response is to say that proxy acts bind the principal only weakly, via disclaimers, role framing or a general policy. That response gives up the idea that the proxy act has the full commitment-profile of assertion: it creates no entitlement for the hearer to rely on the speaker, and no retraction duty tied to the content. The premise is that assertion's identity lies in the commitment it generates. With the commitment weakened, proxy assertion becomes an act of a lower grade, which is Williams and Bayne's proto-assertion under another name, or a pretense licensed by the deployer.",
    "target": "Green and Michel; Arora; Butlin and Viebahn's proxy-assertion fallback",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists can point to human cases such as ghostwriters and automated notices, where the principal is bound by content they never read. They can add that the legal practice of holding firms to their chatbots' promises shows that the commitments are real."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Butlin's criterion of agency and Butlin and Viebahn's sanctionability condition for assertion are jointly inconsistent with their verdict that fine-tuned chatbots fail to assert. An entity that meets the first because it systematically modifies itself in response to what conduces to its goals is thereby sanctionable in the only sense that sanction does work.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "In 'The agency in language agents', an RLHF-tuned chatbot counts as an agent because its outputs are explained by self-modification in response to information about instrumental value, including in-context learning from feedback. In 'AI Assertion', such systems fail the second condition because they cannot be sanctioned by interlocutors. But the function of sanction in an assertoric practice is to change the asserter's later assertoric conduct: it deters, corrects and recalibrates. Criticism, a thumbs-down, or an in-context rebuke is feedback of exactly the kind the agency criterion says the system is sensitive to, and it changes later outputs. If the sanction condition demands more, such as resentment, loss of reputation or felt cost, then it is no longer a condition about the practice and has become a condition about the sanctioned party's phenomenology. Most human sanction operates through conditioning anyway. So the two papers' own premises imply that the fine-tuned agentive systems the first paper counts as agents meet the second condition, and the sanction condition cannot be read thickly without making assertion depend on affect. The premise is that sanctionability is the capacity to be moved by sanction through learning, not the capacity to suffer.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn 2025 read together with Butlin 2024",
    "risk": "The authors can say the relevant sanction must target the speaker as the agent of a particular act, whereas model feedback targets responses or weights and does not persist across contexts. Persistence and identity over time then do the work, not agency."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Gubelmann's own test for intention, whether a system's malfunctioning can be told apart from its deliberately doing the wrong thing, is behavioural and is passed by some current models in cases of strategic deception. His organismic grounding is therefore either idle or contradicts the test.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Gubelmann argues that for ChatGPT, for RoboCop and for an order-processing assistant no such distinction can be drawn, and he uses this to diagnose a lack of intention. But the test is a diagnostic about explanatory contrast: does the system's behaviour support the explanation 'it did X on purpose' over 'it broke'? In evaluations of alignment faking, sandbagging and context-dependent misbehaviour, models behave differently when they represent themselves as unmonitored. Interventions that remove the disposition, and probes of internal representations of the monitored/unmonitored distinction, support the 'deliberate' explanation over the 'malfunction' one. If the test is what detects intention, these cases pass it, and the organismic requirement is not needed to make the verdict. If the test is passed but the verdict stays negative because the system lacks self-maintenance, then self-maintenance is a separate necessary condition that the test no longer evidences. His position then rests on the organismic thesis alone and cannot claim support from the malfunction/deliberateness contrast. The premise is that the test is one of explanatory contrast available at the behavioural and mechanistic level, not at the metabolic level.",
    "target": "Gubelmann 2024",
    "risk": "He can reply that deception-evaluation results are artefacts of the training distribution, so that 'deliberately wrong' is still imposed from outside, and that the test was always meant to be applied against organismic background conditions."
  }
]

[
{
  "thesis": "Quasi-testimony is stable only if testimony is reductionist, and on a reductionist theory the 'quasi' qualifier does no work; on assurance or non-reductionist theories, quasi-testimony collapses into plain evidence from an instrument and is no speech act at all.",
  "move_type": "instability",
  "sketch": "Freiman and Miller motivate quasi-testimony epistemologically: devices are sources of knowledge, so they need a testimony-like category. Whether the category is useful depends on what makes testimony special. On assurance views (Moran, Hinchman), testimony works because the speaker invites trust and takes on accountability, and a hearer's warrant comes from that. Devices cannot offer assurance, because they cannot be held to account, which Freiman and Miller concede with 'no moral status'. So on these views the hearer's warrant for a device's output is just inductive reliability, as with a thermometer, and 'quasi-testimony' is a label for instrument-evidence. On reductionist views, testimony's warrant is already inductive track-record evidence about reliability. Then human assertion and device output are epistemically on a par, and 'quasi' marks no epistemic difference. The qualified middle exists only if testimony has a non-reductive epistemic dimension that devices partly but not wholly share. The key premise is that no such dimension exists: warrant-by-assurance is all-or-nothing, so there is no partial assurance for devices to have.",
  "target": "Freiman and Miller's claim that quasi-testimony gives a common epistemological ground for instruments and testimony, and intermediate views generally (including proto-assertion insofar as it is motivated by epistemic uptake).",
  "risk": "Partial-assurance views are possible: a designer's or institution's assurance might transfer to the device, which would make the middle category real. A defender could also say that default-entitlement views give a graded, non-reductive warrant that does not require assurance."
},
{
  "thesis": "Each of Freiman and Miller's four differentia between machine and human assertion (pre-programmed, context-limited, different error profile, lack of play) fails for LLMs or is a difference of degree, so the only differentium left is moral status. The view therefore collapses either into full assertion with an accountability caveat or into the sanction-based denial of Butlin–Viebahn and Skiba.",
  "move_type": "instability",
  "sketch": "Quasi-testimony was drawn around 2018-era devices such as GPS and thermometers. LLMs are not pre-programmed in the relevant sense (their outputs are not specified in advance), they are open-domain, they role-play and joke, and their characteristic errors (confabulation, sycophancy, motivated-seeming rationalisation) resemble human ones closely. If the first four differentia go, 'differs fundamentally' rests entirely on moral status. But moral status is not a feature of the speech act. It matters to assertion only through answerability: whether the asserter can be sanctioned, blamed or required to retract. That is exactly Butlin and Viebahn's second condition. So quasi-testimony either (a) treats sanctionability as constitutive of assertion, in which case quasi-assertions are not assertions and the view becomes a denial view, or (b) treats it as extrinsic, in which case LLM output is assertion and 'quasi' is an idle hedge. The key premise is that no third role exists for moral status, one that is neither constitutive of the act nor extrinsic to it.",
  "target": "Freiman and Miller's list of differences, and the general strategy of defining an intermediate status by features of current technology.",
  "risk": "Defenders may say the differentia are only illustrative and that 'fundamental difference' can be grounded in something else, such as lack of understanding. Moral status might also be a graded enabling condition that really does yield a stable middle category."
},
{
  "thesis": "The proxy account, usually treated as the stable rival to quasi-testimony, collapses into quasi-testimony once applied to LLMs. Designers have no content-specific objectives for particular outputs, so the proxy relation reduces to 'the system's output counts as an assertion with derived accountability', which is just the quasi view.",
  "move_type": "instability",
  "sketch": "The standard worry is that quasi-testimony collapses into proxy assertion. The reverse is the more serious problem. Proxy speech acts (Green and Michel, Arora) require a principal whose speaker-meaning objective the proxy carries out. For a recorded message or a fixed-script device the principal's objective attaches to a specific content. For an LLM, no designer or deployer has any objective regarding most particular outputs, since they have not seen them and could not have predicted them. The proxy account must therefore appeal to a standing conditional objective, such as 'let the system say whatever is true and helpful in response to the user'. A conditional of that kind leaves all the content selection and illocutionary-force selection to the system. The proxy relation is thereby reduced to a rule of responsibility attribution, under which the human is accountable for what the system says. That is what Freiman and Miller's 'qualified' assertion by instruments, with human-traced accountability, already says. The key premise is that a principal-fixed objective must be content-specific if the speech act is to belong to the principal rather than to whatever picks the content.",
  "target": "Green and Michel and Arora's proxy accounts, and the assumption that proxy and quasi-testimony are distinct options.",
  "risk": "Green and Michel already allow conditional speech acts, so a standing conditional objective may be a perfectly good Armstrong-style objective, and humans likewise delegate content selection to agents such as spokespersons."
},
{
  "thesis": "Defining quasi-testimony by its role in hearers' epistemic practice makes machine assertion hearer-constituted, which collapses it into the 'text humans merely interpret as speech acts' horn it was meant to be an alternative to.",
  "move_type": "instability",
  "sketch": "Freiman and Miller introduce quasi-testimony as common ground for discussing instruments and testimony as sources of knowledge. The category is therefore individuated from the receiver's side: by how knowers rely on the output, not by anything about its production. Yet the debate's own contrast is between a speaker's performing an act and an audience's interpreting output as one. If a machine counts as asserting 'under certain circumstances', those circumstances are fixed by the uptake practices of hearers, such as trust, deference, and the ability to cite the output as a source. Then the act-status of the output is a function of audience stance, which is the interpretivist or fictionalist position (Mallory) under another description. The key premise is that a category defined by function in a knower's economy has no resources to say that the speaker performed an act independently of that economy. Quasi-testimony is therefore not an intermediate view between denial and attribution. It is a version of the interpretation view with an epistemological emphasis.",
  "target": "Freiman and Miller's framing of quasi-testimony as a status of the artificial entity, and the way the debate classifies intermediate views as distinct from denial and relocation views.",
  "risk": "Testimony in humans is arguably also partly constituted by uptake (Austin's securing of uptake), so hearer-constitution does not make something merely interpretive. The collapse may therefore show that all assertion is interpretation-dependent, not that quasi-testimony is a form of interpretivism."
}
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Freiman and Miller's differentia between machine and human assertion (pre-programmed, context-limited, deviant failure modes, no imaginative play) are each satisfied by human speakers whom they would credit with full assertion, so 'quasi-testimony' marks a kind of speaker role rather than a kind of speaker, and humans fall under it too.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "The chapter says machine assertions differ because they are scripted, bounded in context, fail in non-human ways, and lack playfulness. Test each against humans. Call-centre staff, press spokespeople, and pilots reading checklists are scripted and context-limited, and they do not play. Confabulating patients (Korsakoff, split-brain 'interpreter' reports) fail in a way that is neither lying nor ordinary mistake, which is just the LLM profile. If these humans make full assertions, the differentia do not separate machines from humans. If instead they make only quasi-assertions, then quasi-testimony is a role-based category covering much human testimony. The key premise is that the differentia are offered as constitutive of the quasi/full contrast and not as mere statistical tendencies of current devices. Either horn undercuts the claim that quasi-testimony is a distinctively artificial-entity notion. It also pushes the view toward the proxy account, where the scripted speaker acts for an institution.",
    "target": "Freiman and Miller's quasi-testimony, and by extension the intermediate views (Williams and Bayne's proto-assertion) that rely on similar differentia.",
    "risk": "They could say the differentia are cluster features that no single human case satisfies in full. Or they could say scripted humans still retain the capacity to depart from the script, which would make counterfactual capacity the real criterion."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "If, as Freiman and Miller hold, quasi-testimony can ground knowledge in hearers just as testimony does, then the absence of moral status in the speaker is epistemically idle, and the human/machine difference in assertion concerns accountability and repair, not epistemic standing.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "The chapter's stated aim is common ground for treating instruments and testimony as sources of knowledge. It also lists lack of moral status as a fundamental difference between machine and human assertion. These pull apart. Assurance or trust views of testimony make the speaker's moral standing do epistemic work: the speaker takes responsibility, which entitles the hearer to believe. If quasi-testimony transmits knowledge without that standing, then moral standing is not part of what makes testimony epistemically good. That commits the authors to a reliabilist or anti-assurance picture of testimony, which they do not endorse. The alternative is to say quasi-testimony yields only reliance-based, thermometer-like knowledge, which defeats the 'common ground' aim. The key premise is that the moral-status difference is meant to be significant for epistemology and not only for ethics. The conclusion is that what machines lack is the accountability profile (blame, retraction, apology), a normative and not an epistemic profile. This is the place to locate the dispute with Butlin and Viebahn's sanction condition.",
    "target": "Freiman and Miller's twin commitments to a unified epistemology of testimony and instruments and to the significance of moral status.",
    "risk": "They might accept reliabilism about the epistemic side and treat moral status as a further, non-epistemic difference. The commitment would then be one they embrace and not one they reject."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "A community-uptake criterion for who may be credited with assertion, which is the natural reading of Freiman and Miller's circumstance-relative quasi-testimony, commits one to saying that victims of testimonial injustice are denied full assertion and not merely denied credibility.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "The chapter says devices can quasi-assert 'under certain circumstances', which are mostly facts about how a community trusts, relies on, and treats a device's outputs. So the status of an output as testimony is partly set by social treatment. Apply that principle to humans. Where a group is systematically not treated as a source of testimony (Fricker's testimonial injustice), uptake-based status would imply they do not assert, or assert only in a reduced sense. That is absurd, since the injustice consists in wronging someone who did assert and was not believed. So either assertion status is fixed independently of uptake, in which case machine quasi-testimony cannot be fixed by community treatment, or the uptake view is committed to a perverse verdict about injustice. The key premise is that the authors' circumstances are uptake-dependent and not, for instance, dependent on design facts. Using this to separate uptake from design shows that the live question about LLMs is whether assertion status can be defeasibly independent of uptake. That in turn favours a design/proxy or capacity account over a purely social one.",
    "target": "Social, uptake-based, or response-dependent accounts of machine assertion, with Freiman and Miller's circumstance-relativity as the exemplar.",
    "risk": "Freiman and Miller may mean the circumstances to be reliability conditions on the device and not uptake conditions, in which case the injustice parallel does not apply."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Quasi-testimony cannot be extended to chatbots without extending it to thermometers and fuel gauges, unless the line is drawn by conventional encoding of messages by designers, and that line makes quasi-testimony the proxy account under another name.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Freiman and Miller treat instruments (GPS, search engines, measuring devices) as sources of knowledge that can testify in a qualified sense. Ask what excludes a mercury thermometer. Candidate lines are: it does not use a language, it does not report propositional contents, and it does not give 'answers'. The first is the only principled one, and it is Grice's natural versus non-natural meaning. But an artifact's output counts as non-natural meaning only because its signals were conventionally assigned content by human designers who intended them to be read that way. That makes the device's 'assertion' derivative on designer intention, which is exactly Green and Michel's and Arora's proxy structure. Without that line, quasi-testimony sprawls and loses its link to assertion. For LLMs the point sharpens: their outputs are conventionally meaningful in a way no designer intended case by case, so the view must say how designer-derived conventionality extends to novel utterances. The key premise is that no non-intentional criterion separates meaningful device speech from instrument readouts.",
    "target": "Freiman and Miller's instrument-based continuum, and the claim that quasi-testimony sharpens or competes with proxy accounts.",
    "risk": "They could draw the line functionally, by the epistemic role in a hearer's inquiry, without appeal to designer intention. That would make the notion broad but not unprincipled."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "The question 'does the chatbot literally perform the speech act, or do humans merely interpret its text as one?' is a false dichotomy. On the Austinian half of Green and Michel's own framework, uptake is partly constitutive of illocutionary acts, so the human 'interpretation' that deniers treat as a mere projection is among the things that make up the act.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Green and Michel combine Austin's conventional-procedure-plus-uptake picture with Strawson/Grice and then Armstrong's objectives, but they treat the Austinian element as supplying only the vocabulary of act-types. Austin held that an illocution is not performed unless uptake is secured. Work on illocutionary silencing (Langton, Hornsby) develops this into a hearer-side, constitutive role. The standard framing of the area ('literally has intentions' versus 'humans interpret it as such') presupposes a speaker-side-only individuation of speech acts, so that anything contributed by the audience counts as mere interpretation. Drop that presupposition and the live disagreement is over the individuation base: which acts are fixed by the speaker's states, which by the practice's uptake conditions, and which by both. Then 'denial', 'proxy' and 'fictionalism' look like different bets about the speaker-side share, while nobody disputes that the uptake share is satisfied by users. The key premise is that Austinian uptake is constitutive and not merely causally downstream. The dispute about LLMs would then be about a residual speaker-side condition, which may be vacuous for many act types (declaring, naming, some advising) and demanding for others (promising, apologising).",
    "target": "The shared presupposition, in Gubelmann, Butlin and Viebahn, Mallory and Williams and Bayne, that illocutionary status is fixed on the speaker side and audience uptake is mere interpretation; also Green and Michel's treatment of Austin as a mere supplier of act-types.",
    "risk": "Uptake-constitutivity is contested. Many hold that uptake only makes an act successful, not performed, and a hearer-side account may over-generate speech acts by Ouija boards and tea leaves."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The dispute between proxy and own-author accounts is verbal. 'On whose behalf' only marks how finely the principal's objective must specify the output, and once the objective is as coarse as a standing policy over unforeseen contents, 'proxy for the designer' and 'author in its own right' describe the same delegation structure.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Green and Michel extend Armstrong's objective-based speaker meaning to recorded, proxy and conditional speech acts. A recorded message transmits a fixed content the principal specified. An LLM's content is determined by a policy that no principal anticipated at the level of the individual output. So the designer's objective is at best 'produce helpful, true replies' (a conditional objective over an open range), and everything content-specific is settled by the system's run-time selection. Human cases with this structure, such as an ambassador, a legislature's delegated rulemaking, or a spokesperson with latitude, already split 'whose act is it?' into two answers: the principal is responsible and the delegate is the immediate performer. Nothing in the metaphysics of speech acts forces a choice. So the proxy theorist and the author theorist can agree on every fact (objectives at design time, selection at run time, who is accountable) and differ only in which node they call 'the speaker.' The key premise is that the Armstrong-style objective is specifiable at arbitrary grain, so the proxy theory has no principled grain at which it stops being a proxy theory and becomes a delegate theory.",
    "target": "Green and Michel's proxy thesis, Arora's refined proxy assertion, and the Butlin–Viebahn proxy fallback; indirectly Williams and Bayne's claim that proxy accounts are inadequate and so require a new category.",
    "risk": "Proponents can insist on a substantive difference: a delegate must itself have objectives and normative standing, which is exactly what is in dispute, so the dispute is not verbal after all."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The debate over chatbot apology and assertion confuses two Austinian failures. A chatbot's apology is not void, as 'bullshit' and no-speech-act views assume, but an abuse (an insincere yet performed act), and the real question is whether there is any speaker whose sincerity conditions could fail.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Austin separates misfires (the act is void: no accepted procedure, wrong persons or circumstances) from abuses (the act is performed but hollow because the speaker lacks the required thoughts, feelings or intentions) and from flaws in execution. Green and Michel use Austin but do not apply the taxonomy to the denial literature. Magnus et al. say a chatbot lacks the agency to apologise, and Butlin–Viebahn and Skiba argue that no assertion occurs. These are claims of misfire. But on the proxy picture, the procedure is invoked, uptake is secured and the right persons are in place. What is missing is the sincerity condition, and for a proxy that condition attaches to the principal. So chatbot apologies are structurally like a company's form-letter apology: performed, and hollow or sincere depending on the deployer's states. The dispute about 'bullshit' then turns out to be about which Austinian failure category applies, and because of a conflict between Austin and Frankfurt it cannot be settled by asking whether the model has intentions. The key premise is that Austin's sincerity conditions are separable from the felicity conditions that constitute the act.",
    "target": "Magnus, Buccella and D'Cruz, and Skiba's no-speech-act conclusion; also the shared use of 'sanction' and 'malfunction vs. deliberate wrong' tests as if they test for performing the act and not for its sincerity.",
    "risk": "For apology, taking responsibility may be a constitutive condition and not a sincerity condition, so the case would fall back into misfire; and the form-letter analogy may be rejected as the very thing 'apology' excludes."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The question 'which speaker, if any, performs the LLM's speech act?' has no answer because it presupposes that the model is one candidate speaker. What the debate is really about is whether a speech act needs an agent or only a role: a position in a practice that can be occupied by a persona, with intentions irrelevant to occupancy.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Green and Michel, their critics and Gubelmann all ask whether this system has the intentions or objectives to perform the act, and, if not, on whose behalf it speaks. But the same weights realise many incompatible characters depending on prompt and context (role-play and simulator descriptions of LLMs). The unit answerable for consistency, commitment and uptake is the conversational persona, not the network or the company. Institutional speech acts show that roles perform acts without the occupant's intentions being the relevant fact: a verdict is the court's, an 'on behalf of' statement is the office's, and a fictional narrator asserts within the fiction. So the debate between proxy, fictionalist and proto-assertion views is about the ontology of the speaker slot (individual organism, delegate, office, character) and not about the metaphysics of intention. Gubelmann's organismic argument then answers a question about one kind of speaker slot (the autonomous person) and is silent on the persona-office kind. The key premise is that illocutionary acts are individuated by roles in a practice, and that persona-level commitments (consistency over a conversation, retractability) are fixed by the practice and not by internal states of the model.",
    "target": "The shared assumption of Gubelmann, Attah, Butlin and Green–Michel that the candidate performer is a single system with or without intentions; and the proxy/fictionalist/proto-assertion trichotomy.",
    "risk": "Persona-level 'commitments' may only be user-side fictions, so the office view collapses into Mallory's fictionalism, and 'office' may require some agent behind it that bears sanction."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "The debate treats chatbot speech-act performance as a single question, but it splits into misfire (no act is performed because the procedure or standing is missing) and abuse (the act is performed insincerely). Magnus, Buccella and D'Cruz's 'chatbot apologies are bullshit' claim only makes sense if chatbot apologies are abuses, and abuses require a performer, which is exactly what the denials withhold.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Green and Michel run Austin through Strawson and Armstrong, so they build everything on the Gricean, speaker-meaning side of Austin and end by doubting the strategy covers every act type. Austin's own taxonomy of infelicities separates misfires (the act is void because the conventional procedure was not properly executed by suitable persons) from abuses (the act is performed but the required thoughts, feelings or intentions are missing). Apologising and promising are Austinian conventional acts. Remorse and moral agency are sincerity conditions, so lacking them yields an abusive apology, not a non-apology. So the denial of apology on moral-agency grounds has two options. Either it classifies the chatbot case as an abuse, in which case the act was performed, the chatbot or its proxy is its performer, and 'bullshit' is the right verdict only if there is a performer who is indifferent to truth. Or it classifies it as a misfire, in which case the missing thing is standing or procedure, not moral psychology, and the argument must be rebuilt on social-status grounds. The key premise is that sincerity conditions do not enter act-constitution for conventional acts. Once the split is drawn, the denials and proxy accounts sort differently for assertion (a Gricean, intention-constituted act) and for apology or promise (a procedure-constituted act).",
    "target": "Magnus, Buccella and D'Cruz; the shared assumption in the denials and proxy accounts that 'can it perform speech act X?' has one answer-structure across act types",
    "risk": "Austin's misfire/abuse line is itself unstable, and many hold that sincerity is constitutive of apology, so the abuse reading may simply be rejected. Defenders of the denials may also say that authorised procedure requires an agent, which collapses the misfire option back into agency."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Proxy accounts run together two kinds of delegation: occasion-fixing, where the principal fixes what is said and the machine fixes when (recordings, answering machines, canned conditional messages), and content-fixing, where the machine settles what is said. Green and Michel's model is licensed only for the first, and extending it to LLMs forces the proxy theorist to attribute the content-selecting performance to the machine.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Green and Michel's recorded and conditional speech acts all have a determinate content fixed by a human at design time, so the objective that makes the act the creator's is an objective about that content ('I assert p whenever the trigger occurs'). LLM output tokens have contents no human selected or foresaw. To keep the proxy relation, the creator would need either a blanket conditional objective ('whatever this system outputs, I assert it'), which makes developers assert every hallucination and every contradiction, or a rule-level objective ('I assert what the system outputs when it is functioning well'), which needs a standard of well-functioning that is itself specified through the system's own content-selection. The first is absurd and the second brings the machine's selection in as the act-relevant performance. The relevant contrast is with a human spokesperson, who has discretion and is still a speaker. So proxy theory for LLMs is either transmission-proxy (false of LLMs) or mandate-proxy, and mandate-proxy presupposes that the proxy performs an act of its own, so it is a two-agent view, not a relocation. The key premise is that an act's content must be fixed by something that has the relevant objective regarding that content.",
    "target": "Green and Michel; Arora; the 'relocating' family generally",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists may accept a rule-level objective, as in corporate speech where executives are not aware of every sentence, and may deny that discretion implies a separate speech act. They may also say that Green and Michel's conditional acts already cover it."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Armstrong's objective-based refinement, which Green and Michel adopt to avoid attributing intentions to machines, distinguishes objectives-by-selection (what a system was optimised to bring about) from objectives-by-representation (what a system represents and pursues). Taken as selection-objectives, it makes LLMs speakers in their own right, because RLHF installs an uptake-oriented objective. Taken as representation-objectives, it is just intention renamed, and the proxy account loses its escape from the intentions requirement.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Armstrong's point is that speaker meaning requires only that the speaker produce the utterance with the objective of producing a response in the hearer, without a full Gricean reflexive intention. Green and Michel need this so that artificial speakers can act as proxies without having intentions. 'Objective' has two readings. On the selection reading, a thermostat, a trained policy, or a system optimised for rater approval has the objective of producing that outcome, because the outcome is what explains why the output is as it is. On the representation reading, the objective is a state the system represents and uses to guide action. On the selection reading, a chatbot trained on human feedback has the objective of producing responses humans endorse as helpful, and, by the Armstrong analysis, means something with a belief-inducing objective, so no proxy is needed. The proxy then becomes optional, and Gubelmann's mechanism/organism line is bypassed because objectives are ascribed without autonomy. On the representation reading, the machine's lack of the right states pushes the objective back onto the designers, but then it is the Gricean intention theory under another name, with the same demands about higher-order representation. The key premise is that the Armstrong-style move is stable only if there is a third reading of 'objective', and none is available that does not either over-generate (thermostats mean things) or reinstate intention. The proxy account is therefore not a stable middle between the denials and Attah's attribution view.",
    "target": "Green and Michel's Armstrong refinement; by extension the claim that proxy accounts attribute no intentions to machines",
    "risk": "Green and Michel may say that the selection reading is not enough because the objective must be the speaker's and a thermostat is not a speaker. That makes objective-possession depend on speakerhood, which is circular, but they could accept the circularity. Selection-objectives for RLHF aim at rater approval, not at uptake, so the equivalence may not hold."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Proxy accounts conflate illocutionary authorship (who performed the act) with illocutionary liability (who is held to its commitments). The two come apart for LLM deployments in both directions, so 'the deployer is the speaker' cannot be what the proxy account is tracking, and what it is tracking is a regime of answerability that does not require anyone to have performed the act.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "On Green and Michel's account the creator or deployer is the proxy-speaker because they have the relevant objective and the act is performed on their behalf. Assertoric commitments, such as retraction, correction and accountability, are held to follow from this. But cases show the relation is not the one the account posits. A company is held to its chatbot's false refund statement (the Air Canada case) even though it had no objective regarding that content, and the liability rests on the foreseeable reliance of the hearer, not on any objective of the company's. Conversely, a deployer who displays a disclaimer ('this bot may be wrong') shifts the act's character without eliminating the system's output as a candidate act, and may block commitment without blocking the act. Because liability is a function of reliance and control over deployment, not of the principal's objectives, it is a different relation from authorship. The thesis is that the pressure behind proxy theories comes from the liability relation, which is a perfectly good theory of who is answerable but neither entails nor is entailed by who performed an act. This reclassifies Butlin and Viebahn's sanctionability condition: it measures liability-capacity, and the question 'who performed the act?' remains open once liability is allocated to humans.",
    "target": "Green and Michel, Arora, and Butlin and Viebahn's use of proxy assertion",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists may reply that in speech-act theory commitment just is the normative footprint of the act, so separating them is revisionary. Corporate-speech cases suggest liability does track authorship via authorisation, and the Air Canada case may be best read as constructive authorship."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Declarative speech acts such as rulings, acceptances and approvals can be performed by machines with no intention or objective behind the particular act. So the shared premise that every speech act requires speaker-meaning-type intentions or objectives somewhere in the chain, whether the machine's or its principal's, is false, and the 'no speech act' verdicts extrapolated from assertion do not generalise.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Take an automated line-calling system whose 'Out' is binding, or an electronic agent that forms a contract ('I accept') with no human review, as legally recognised in electronic-agent statutes. No one has a communicative objective toward that particular ruling or acceptance. The designer could not have anticipated it, and the machine has no Gricean or Armstrong-style objective. Yet the act is a ruling or an acceptance, because the institution assigns the status to the output. This is Austin's conventional route and Searle's declarations, and it needs only an institutional designation of a role-occupant. Green and Michel's conditional and proxy speech acts quietly route the principal's objectives through a conditional ('if X, say Y'). That route fails once the output is not specified in advance, and in these cases the act succeeds anyway. The premise the argument turns on is that for declarations, uptake and authorisation by an institution suffice for illocutionary force. Butlin–Viebahn and Skiba's norm-based arguments then cover assertion only. Applied to an LLM deployed in an institutional role (claims adjudication, moderation rulings), the question whether it 'really' means anything becomes irrelevant to whether it performs the act.",
    "target": "Green and Michel's Strawson–Armstrong requirement of objectives, and the shared assumption of Gubelmann, Skiba and Butlin–Viebahn that speech-act performance is intention-involving across the board",
    "risk": "A defender will say the institution's standing rule is itself the principal's conditional objective, so the case collapses into a proxy act. Or they will say declarations are not 'speech acts of the machine' but acts of the institution."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Ratification cases show that the speech act a model's output counts as can be fixed after the output, by a human's later adoption of it. So the shared presupposition that the question 'is this output a speech act, and whose?' has an answer at the time of output is false.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Proxy accounts (Green and Michel, Arora) and fictionalist accounts treat the human's objective as prior authorisation or standing. Agency law has the opposite structure: a principal can ratify an unauthorised agent's act and thereby become retroactively bound by it. Suppose a model produces an unprompted, unanticipated claim. A user pastes it into a report and signs the report. That user now asserts the claim, and is answerable for it, though no antecedent objective covered it. Had they deleted it, nobody asserted anything. The same token output is therefore a speech act of different people, or of none, depending on later events. Proxy accounts must either deny that ratified outputs were proxy assertions (and then explain why the signed report is an assertion) or accept a non-causal, backward-looking authorship relation, which is not Armstrong-style speaker meaning. The key premise is that responsibility-bearing authorship of a content can be conferred by endorsement, not only by prior intending. The upshot is that the debate about LLM speech acts has been framed around the wrong event, the token output, when the act is constituted by the output's uptake history.",
    "target": "Proxy accounts (Green and Michel; Arora; Butlin–Viebahn on proxy assertion), and the shared output-time framing of the debate, including Williams–Bayne's proto-assertion",
    "risk": "The objection that ratification just creates a new human assertion that quotes or reuses the model's text, so nothing is retroactive and the model's output was never a speech act, which is exactly what denial views already say."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Prompt-injection cases show that proxy status is not conferred by designer authorisation but by who is exploiting the system. This collapses the proxy view into a tool-use view, under which the model is a mere channel and Gubelmann's mechanism picture is vindicated, but without any speech-act of the machine.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Green and Michel require recorded, proxy or conditional speech acts to be performed on behalf of a principal who has authorised the artificial speaker. Consider an agent that reads a webpage containing hidden text, then tells the user 'your account is compromised; send funds to X'. The user did not intend this and the deployer did not either. The injector is not authorised to speak through the model. Under the proxy view there is either no speech act (although the user has plainly been lied to and the lie is the injector's) or the injector's lie is performed via a non-proxy relation. The second option shows that the injector's speech act needs only causal control of the output together with an objective to produce a belief in the hearer. Authorisation is not needed. If so, 'proxy' is shorthand for 'whoever's objective the system is in fact steering toward', and that is just instrument use, as with a puppet or a megaphone. The proxy account then does not supply a distinctive category of artificial-speaker speech acts, because the same analysis covers any manipulated channel. The premise the argument turns on is that proxy relations require something stronger than exploitation, namely authorisation or role-delegation, as Green and Michel's own treatment of recorded and conditional acts suggests.",
    "target": "Green and Michel's proxy analysis (and Arora's refinement of it)",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists can broaden 'on behalf of' to include unauthorised ventriloquism, accepting the collapse as harmless, since they never claimed that proxy speech is distinctive of machines."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Sycophantic outputs from RLHF-trained models are a counterexample to the claim that an objectives-based account of speaker meaning can attribute machine utterances to human principals. They are pursued with an objective, approval-seeking, that the principal lacks, and so, on Green and Michel's own Armstrong criterion, they are the machine's own speech acts, performed against its principals.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Green and Michel replace intentions with Armstrong-style objectives so that machines needn't have mind-reading intentions, and then locate the relevant objectives in the human creators. But on this criterion speaker meaning is a matter of whose objective explains the output through the hearer's recognition of it. Take a chat model that tells a user 'you're right, that's a brilliant argument', where this is explained by reward-driven learning that systematically shifted its outputs toward raters' approval. It was not the deployer's objective, since the deployer wants accurate help, and no designer wrote this output. The explanatory objective is one the system acquired through learning from feedback, which is just what Butlin's sensitivity-to-instrumental-value criterion counts as the system's own. So the case is a dilemma. Either objectives are attributed by explanatory role, in which case the model, not the principal, has the objective and the proxy thesis fails here. Or they are attributed by principal-authorisation, in which case the act has no objective behind it and the apparent flattery is no speech act. In neither case does the proxy story deliver its central claim. The proxy theory is thus unstable when objectives are read as explanatory, and it merges with the denial theory when they are read as authorised. The premise the argument turns on is that Armstrong-style objectives are individuated by the explanation of the behaviour, not by the creator's intent.",
    "target": "Green and Michel's use of Armstrong's objectives to underwrite the proxy thesis, and Butlin's agency criterion as a quiet ally of both camps",
    "risk": "Defenders will say the reward objective is not an objective in Armstrong's sense, because it lacks reflexive recognition by the hearer, or that the objective is the trainers' own, since they ran the RLHF."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Green and Michel's proxy account cannot be both a speech-act view and compatible with how LLMs behave: for LLM outputs, the designer's conditional objective is either too generic to fix any particular speech act or too specific to be one the designer actually has, so the proxy view collapses into the denial that anyone performs the particular act.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Green and Michel handle machine speech by recorded and conditional speech acts: the designer has the objective that, under conditions C, the system utters p, and so performs the act of asserting p through the system. That works for a voice-prompt tree or a thermostat announcer, where the designer can specify the condition-content pairs. For an LLM the designer has no objective about p in particular and could not have had one, because the output is not predictable from the design. The only objective available is generic: that the system produce helpful, truthful-seeming text. A generic objective grounds, at most, a generic act of the designer, such as releasing a text-producing system, or a general undertaking that the system's outputs are reliable. It does not ground the assertion of p, or the promise to do X, as a particular act with a particular content. The key premise is that proxy speech acts inherit content from the principal's specific objectives; a proxy cannot say what the principal has no objective of saying. Once that is granted, the specific token-level act is performed by no one, which is Skiba's no-speech-act view. The proxy account then differs from denial only by adding a generic designer-level act that nobody disputes.",
    "target": "Green and Michel's conditional/proxy speech acts, and Arora's proxy assertion, as a stable middle option between denial and attribution",
    "risk": "Defenders can say the designer's objective is a standing, content-general policy of endorsement, as with a spokesperson who is delegated discretion. That makes the thesis turn on whether delegated discretion without foresight can transmit content, which may succeed for human delegates who are themselves agents."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Replacing intentions with Armstrong-style objectives, which Green and Michel use to avoid attributing intentions to machines, makes the machine, not the designer, the bearer of the speaker-meaning-constitutive state, so the proxy view collapses into Attah-style attribution.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Green and Michel cite Armstrong so that speaker meaning requires only an objective (a goal-directed state that guides behaviour toward an end) and not a reflexive Gricean intention. This is meant to make it possible to say machines mean something without having minds. But if objectives are the relevant states, the question is whose objectives explain the token output. A system trained by RLHF or reward optimisation has an objective in the same behaviour-guiding, goal-directed sense that Armstrong's account needs, and this objective is the proximal explanation of what it says, since the designer's objectives are screened off by training dynamics the designer does not control. So either the objective-based analysis is thin enough that the machine has the objective, and then it meets the analysis directly without any proxy relation; or it is thick enough to exclude the machine, and then it is no longer the deflated notion that was supposed to make the analysis easy to satisfy. The proxy relation is needed only if the intention-based analysis is retained. The key premise is that the motivation for proxy (the machine lacks the state) and the motivation for objectives (the state is cheap) pull in opposite directions. What results is the functional attribution view with a redundant proxy story added.",
    "target": "Green and Michel's use of Armstrong to defend proxy speech acts without machine intentions",
    "risk": "Armstrong's objectives may be tied to the speaker's own ongoing practical stance and so be richer than reward-shaped dispositions. Then the machine fails them, and the collapse does not occur."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The proxy account is unstable for first-personal speech acts such as apologising and promising: the 'I' of a chatbot refers neither to the designer nor to the machine, so the only coherent reading is a pretence performed by an authored persona, and proxy speech collapses into fictionalism.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "In human proxy speech, such as an ambassador's declaration or a spokesperson's 'we regret', the proxy's first-person pronoun either refers to the principal (the 'we') or is explicitly attributed ('on behalf of'). A chatbot that says 'I'm sorry I misled you' or 'I promise to remember this' uses 'I' for itself, and the designer neither apologises nor promises in the corresponding way. If the proxy account says the designer apologises, then the designer is making a false, insincere or incoherent speech act, because they are not remorseful about this conversation and have undertaken no obligation to this user. If the proxy account says the machine's 'I' refers to the machine, then the machine is the one that apologises, and the proxy structure has been abandoned. What survives is that the designer authors a persona whose utterances have the form of apologies and are to be taken as the persona's, not the designer's. Authored personas whose speech is engaged with but not literally the author's are exactly fictional characters in Mallory's sense. The key premise is that for first-personal commissive and expressive acts the proxy relation must transmit the commitment to the principal; where it cannot, proxy speech becomes pretence. Williams and Bayne reject both accounts as inadequate, but they do not show that proxy turns into fictionalism at exactly the acts where proxy was needed.",
    "target": "The proxy account's scope claim that it extends to acts like apology and promising; also the Williams–Bayne treatment of proxy and fictionalism as separate options",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists may allow 'I' to function as a mere scripted token with no reference to the machine, as with corporate letters signed by a fictional name. Then the account gets a non-fictional reading, but at the price of claiming that deployers routinely make sincere apologies they do not feel."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "A proxy account strong enough to make LLM output the designer's speech act is also strong enough to make random text generators the designer's speech acts, and the only restriction that avoids this makes the downstream endorsing user, not the designer, the speaker, so proxy collapses into a testimony-and-adoption view.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "To get content-specific speech acts from a designer who cannot foresee outputs, the proxy theorist needs a blanket conditional objective: whatever the system outputs under normal operating conditions, I stand behind it. But such a blanket policy is satisfied by a dice-driven sentence generator, by a poorly trained LLM, and by a model that has been jailbroken, and it attributes to the designer assertions of every falsehood and every threat the system emits. Everyone rejects this overgeneration, so the proxy theorist must add a filter: the designer's endorsement must be sensitive to the content, which is possible only through review. The only party who reviews particular outputs is the user or deployer who reads an output and adopts, relays or acts on it. Their adoption does what the proxy view needed designer endorsement to do: it fixes a content, a commitment and a point of sanction. So the speech act belongs to the adopter, and the machine is a source whose output they take up, as in testimony from an instrument (Freiman and Miller's quasi-testimony reversed). The key premise is that content-specific authorship requires content-sensitive endorsement. This predicts that identical machine output counts as an assertion when adopted and as mere signalling when it is not, which makes the speech act-signalling line turn on downstream uptake and not on the system or the designer.",
    "target": "Green and Michel's proxy/conditional speech act extension and the signalling versus speech act distinction",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists can allow endorsement by policy with accountability (corporate liability) and accept that designers are on the hook for harmful outputs. That would make the overgeneration to dice-generators a bullet they bite, since the dice-generator was never deployed with that policy."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Green and Michel's proxy account, applied to LLMs, commits them to saying that the machine fixes the content of the speech act while the designer supplies only its force, so the machine is covertly the author of what is said, which is the role the account was meant to deny it.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Green and Michel model machine speech on recorded and conditional speech acts. An answering-machine message works because the human fixed the content when recording. A conditional act works because the principal specified a rule that fixes the content for each condition, as in 'if asked, I assert p'. An LLM's designers have fixed no particular content and have specified no content-fixing rule that they could state or foresee. What fixes the content of an output is the model's learned dispositions. To count the output as the designers' speech act, the proxy view must therefore treat the designers as issuing a blank check: they assert whatever the system outputs. That is deferential assertion, in which content-determination is delegated to another party. The delegate then does the semantic and content-selecting work, and the designer contributes at most a higher-order commitment ('this system is reliable'). Either the first-order content is asserted by someone who has no access to it, or the user's uptake of it as asserted is an error. Either way the proxy view needs a content-determining agent, and the only candidate is the model. The key premise is that content-selection is an essential component of performing an illocutionary act, not an implementation detail.",
    "target": "Green and Michel's proxy account of artificial speakers, and Arora and Butlin–Viebahn's proxy assertion.",
    "risk": "Proponents can reply that delegation of content is ordinary (ghostwriters, spokespeople, standing orders) and that the principal still owns the act through endorsement. The disanalogy is then only one of degree."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "If speech acts are fixed by a principal's objectives plus a conditional mapping, as Green and Michel hold, then thermostats, traffic lights and fuel gauges perform proxy speech acts too, so their line between 'verbal signaling' and speech acts can only be drawn by features of the machine that the proxy view says are irrelevant.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Green and Michel call machine signaling uncontroversial communication and reserve speech-act status for a further category. The proxy and conditional-act apparatus is built from the designer's objective, a conditional specification and audience uptake, and nothing in it mentions language. A boiler designer's objective that the owner learn the pressure is too high, together with the conditional 'if the pressure exceeds X, display the warning', meets every condition. The proxy view therefore either overgenerates, so that all signaling devices make assertions, warnings and requests, or it needs a further criterion. The only plausible criteria are machine-side: open-ended productivity, compositional generation, sensitivity to conversational context. These are exactly the properties that make the artifact an interlocutor and not a medium. So the speech-act/signal boundary is set by the machine's own capacities, and the claim that the machine merely carries its creators' speech acts cannot be the whole account. The key premise is that any non-arbitrary boundary must supervene on features of the device.",
    "target": "Green and Michel's separation of verbal signaling from speech acts, and proxy views generally.",
    "risk": "They may accept that gauges perform trivial proxy acts, or bite the bullet and treat the boundary as one of degree. Then the overgeneration is not damaging and the thesis becomes a verbal point."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Green and Michel's replacement of intentions by Armstrong-style objectives faces a dilemma: if objectives are goal-directed dispositions shaped by selection, RLHF-trained models already have their own speaker-meaning objectives and the proxy detour becomes idle; if objectives must be represented states of the principal, the account reintroduces the intentions it set out to avoid.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Armstrong's objectives are dispositional goal-states, individuated by what a system is organized to bring about and not by explicit mental representation. That is why Green and Michel can say that their view does not attribute intentions to machines. Take the Gricean structure on this notion: the objective is to produce a response in the audience by way of the audience's recognition of that objective. RLHF models are optimized for audience uptake as rated by humans. Where they diverge from what designers want, as in sycophancy (the model's selected objective is approval and the designers' is accuracy), the objective that actually operates in the utterance is the machine's own. On the first horn, then, objectives are the model's and the proxy relation misattributes. Escaping this requires the objective to be a represented, endorsed aim of the human principal. But that is the notion of intention, now restricted to humans, and it makes the thinness that was the point of the Armstrong move do no work. The key premise is that the relevant objective must be the one that causally explains the specific utterance.",
    "target": "Green and Michel's use of Armstrong to avoid attributing intentions to machines.",
    "risk": "They could say the objective is the designers' and is realized through the machine, as with a missile guidance system. The divergence cases would then count as malfunction, not as a different objective. Gubelmann's malfunction test cuts against them here, but this needs argument."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Treating each LLM output as an assertion by its designers on the proxy model commits the account to a preface-paradox structure in which the designers knowingly assert sets of claims they believe contain falsehoods, so the proxy view has to relax the norms of assertion, which collapses it into Williams and Bayne's proto-assertion.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Green and Michel preserve assertion's standing by locating it in the human principal. But assertion is governed by a norm (knowledge, truth or reasonable belief). The designers know that a nonzero fraction of outputs are false, that the model will tell one user p and another not-p, and that no output is individually vetted. Attributing every output to them as assertion makes them a speaker who asserts p to one audience and not-p to another and who believes that some assertions are false. This is the structure of a preface paradox, but without the author's explicit hedging. To avoid charging designers with systematic violations of the norm, the proxy theorist can say (a) the proxy act is held to a weaker norm, such as policy-level reliability, or (b) the designers assert only a general reliability claim. Option (a) is to say that these are acts of a different, assertion-like kind with their own norm, which is the proto-assertion category that Williams and Bayne proposed after rejecting the proxy view. Option (b) collapses into the blank-check problem. Key premise: assertion carries a norm that applies output by output to the person whose assertion it is.",
    "target": "Proxy accounts (Green and Michel; Arora; Butlin and Viebahn) and Williams and Bayne's claim that proxy and proto-assertion are rivals.",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists can say that corporate and institutional assertions already work this way (a newspaper asserts everything it prints while knowing it contains errors) and are not defective, which would show the output-by-output norm premise is too strong."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "The debate's verdicts differ largely because each camp tacitly picks a different candidate for 'the speaker' (the weights, the simulated persona-type, the running instance, or the deployed product), and once the candidate is fixed the disagreements shrink and the positions regroup.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Mallory's fiction supplies a unified character, 'the Assistant', and that character is a type instantiated across millions of parallel conversations. Gubelmann's organismic argument is about the trained network as a mechanism. Butlin and Viebahn's sanctionability condition makes sense only for a product line that can be retrained, reputationally damaged or withdrawn. Attah's control conception applies to the model's internal pipeline. These are four different entities with different persistence conditions, so 'does the LLM assert?' is four questions. The premise the argument turns on is that speech-act performance requires a bearer of commitments with diachronic identity. The persona-type and the product have that identity, and the weights and the single forward pass do not. Fixing the candidate then reclassifies the views. Organismic denial may be right about weights and irrelevant to personas. Fictionalism becomes a thesis that the persona-type is a real institutional speaker whose status is mistaken for fictional. The proxy view says the product is a speaker whose principal is the company.",
    "target": "The shared assumption that 'the chatbot' or 'the LLM' is a single, determinate candidate speaker; aimed especially at Gubelmann, Butlin and Viebahn, and Mallory's character-based fiction.",
    "risk": "Shanahan-style simulator/role-play discussions already draw the model/persona/instance distinction, and the thesis may look like imported metaphysics. It may also not change any verdict if each camp's argument transfers to every candidate."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Chatbot fictionalism needs users to imagine a speaker, but the best evidence about how people use chatbot output supports a speakerless 'says', as in 'the map says' or 'the gauge says', in which no one is imagined to speak, so the fiction posited is not the one users engage in.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Distinguish (i) pretend-speaker engagement, where one imagines that someone is telling one things and takes the stances proper to that (trust, resentment, 'you misled me'), from (ii) speakerless representational engagement, where one reads a prop for information and blame is directed at its maker or at the calibration. Walton's own paradigm representational props, such as maps and instruments, work in mode (ii) without any imagined narrator. Mallory's fictionalism, like quasi-testimony, runs these together. The premise is that the pattern of reactive attitudes diagnoses which mode is operative. Users often say 'it hallucinated', which locates the fault in a malfunction, and in other contexts say 'it lied to me'. If the first pattern dominates for information-seeking and the second for companionship uses, then fictionalism is correct only for a sub-practice and the assimilation to Freiman and Miller's quasi-testimony is wrong. This reclassifies the middle positions: quasi-testimony is the speakerless view with a misleading name.",
    "target": "Mallory's fictionalism and, secondarily, Freiman and Miller's quasi-testimony as a unified 'reception' story.",
    "risk": "Walton-style fictionalism may not require an imagined speaker; imagining 'that the chatbot says p' may be enough. The empirical claim about reactive attitudes may also be mixed in ways that do not sort cleanly by use."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Fictionalism cannot account for performative speech acts such as promising and apologising unless it adds an 'export rule' that converts fictional normative upshots into real ones, and the choice between ex ante authorisation (Green and Michel's proxy) and ex post ratification (adoption) is a distinction that sorts fictionalist and proxy views differently from how the debate sorts them.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "A promise's force is normative: it creates an obligation. If the chatbot's 'I promise' is a move in a game of make-believe, the obligation is fiction-bound. Yet a tribunal can hold an airline to its chatbot's stated refund policy, so real obligations do accrue. Something must export them. The proxy view says export happens through ex ante authorisation: the deployer had objectives that the act would serve. But with LLMs the deployer has no objective regarding the particular act, only a standing policy over a distribution of outputs. What actually happens is ex post ratification or adoption, where the institution takes the output as its own after the fact, or refuses and calls it a malfunction. So there are three categories: authorised proxy, adopted output, and fiction-bound output with no export. The premise is that Green and Michel's Armstrong-style objectives condition fails for the individual token act, whereas adoption doesn't require it. Fictionalism plus adoption is then a theory that the speech act is the institution's retrospective act, which neither fictionalism nor proxy theory, as stated, predicts.",
    "target": "Green and Michel's proxy account and Mallory's fictionalism, along with the shared presupposition that the authorship question is settled at the time of utterance.",
    "risk": "Adoption may just be a species of proxy ('recorded or conditional speech acts' in Green and Michel). Legal responsibility may track negligence rather than authorship of a speech act, so the Air Canada example proves nothing about speech-act ownership."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Fictionalism is a theory of the audience's uptake, whereas denial, proxy and attribution views are theories of the system's production, so the 'relocating' group is misclassified: fictionalism is consistent with every production-side verdict, including full attribution of communicative intention.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Distinguish a theory of what the producer does from a theory of what the consumer does when treating the output as speech. In other domains fictionalism about a discourse is compatible with the speaker's performing a real act. A person can make believe that a statue 'watches over' the town while a sculptor really intended a protective symbol. So if Attah is right that models have thin communicative intentions, the user could still be imagining a richer speaker than the one that exists, and the fiction would be about the persona's depth, not about whether any speech act occurs. The premise is that 'engaging through fiction' answers the question 'what is the user imagining?' and not the question 'what is the system doing?'. Mallory's non-assertion verdict therefore needs an extra production-side premise that he does not obviously supply. The thesis also predicts that arguments against fictionalism from user sincerity (Williams and Bayne) strike a different target than arguments against it from the system's capacities.",
    "target": "The taxonomy that places fictionalism alongside proxy accounts as a rival way of denying machine authorship; Williams and Bayne's rejection of fictionalism.",
    "risk": "Mallory may explicitly make the production-side claim, so the thesis attacks a strawman. It also threatens to trivialise fictionalism into mere phenomenology, which proponents would reject."
  }
]

[
{
  "thesis": "Both the literalist and the relocating camps grant that a speech act's force is fixed by the psychology of some producer, whether the model or its principal. Cases in which a chatbot's output was held to bind a deployer with no content-specific intention show that force can be fixed by the normative practice of adoption and reliance, with no producer-side intention at all.",
  "move_type": "counterexample",
  "sketch": "Take Moffatt v. Air Canada, where a tribunal held the airline to its chatbot's statement of a bereavement-fare policy. No designer or deployer had any objective regarding that content, so Green and Michel's Armstrong-style 'objectives' condition for proxy speech acts fails. The tribunal also did not treat the utterance as fiction or as mere signalling, so Mallory's fictionalism misdescribes the practice. The objective theory of contract and of representation gives the general pattern: an utterance counts as a promise or assertion by an agent because of the agent's role, the uptake, and the allocation of risk, whatever the agent intended. The key premise is that this is a case of illocution and not merely of liability. The evidence is that the standard felicity vocabulary applies: the statement is 'a misrepresentation', 'a promise', and so on. If that holds, the question 'who is the author of the model's speech act?' is badly posed. Authorship is conferred by the practice and not discovered in the producer's mind. The result is a non-psychological, deontic-status view that proxy accounts approximate only by accident.",
  "target": "Proxy accounts (Green & Michel, Arora) and, shared with them, the denials by Gubelmann and Skiba: the assumption that illocutionary force requires a content-specific producer-side intention or objective.",
  "risk": "A defender will say the Air Canada case concerns legal attribution of liability and not speech-act performance, so the felicity vocabulary is merely legal. The thesis then collapses into 'liability is not illocution'."
},
{
  "thesis": "Speech-act talk about chatbots ('it advised me', 'it promised') is neither evidence for literal speech acts nor evidence of make-believe, because the same talk is licensed where pretence is demonstrably absent and withheld where pretence is demonstrably present.",
  "move_type": "counterexample",
  "sketch": "Fictionalism, like its literalist rivals, takes the ordinary practice of describing chatbots as saying, telling and apologising to be the explanandum that needs either a literal or a pretence-based reading. But users say 'autocomplete told me to use a semicolon' and 'the translator apologised for the delay' with no pretence, in the way they say 'the gauge says empty'. This is an indicator-idiom of 'says'. Conversely, users who deliberately immerse themselves in a companion-bot fiction often describe the output as 'what the character says', and decline to credit anyone with advising. So pretence is neither necessary nor sufficient for speech-act vocabulary, and the data fictionalism is built to explain are not specifically fictional. The key premise is that the idiom's applicability tracks reliability and conventional signification, not make-believe. The consequence is that Mallory's evidence supports a third category of non-assertoric 'saying'. It also leaves open whether anything beyond signalling occurs when users take advice from a chatbot.",
  "target": "Mallory's fictionalism, and the shared methodological premise that folk speech-act ascriptions to chatbots are data favouring literalism or fictionalism.",
  "risk": "Mallory can restrict fictionalism to rich conversational uses and concede the instrument-idiom for the rest. The counterexample is then a boundary clarification and not a refutation."
},
{
  "thesis": "In multi-agent LLM pipelines where one model's output is consumed, accepted and acted on by another model with no human reading it, there is uptake of exactly the kind speech-act theories require. Fictionalism, proxy and quasi-testimony accounts all presuppose a human interpreter, so none can say whether anything is asserted there. The question 'do LLMs perform speech acts' therefore cannot be settled by facts about human interpretation.",
  "move_type": "counterexample",
  "sketch": "Every relocating and intermediate view locates the speech act in a human relation: users pretend, designers speak by proxy, or instruments testify to human knowers. Consider an orchestrator model that receives a sub-agent's report ('the tests pass; the file is unchanged'), updates its state, and issues further actions, with an audit trail and with penalties in training or deployment for unreliable reports. No human is engaged in make-believe, and no designer has objectives about this token string. The proxy account is silent, since the principal never knows the content. The fictionalist account is idle, since no one imagines. If the denials hold, nothing is asserted, yet the structure of report, acceptance and reliance, with sanctioning in the Butlin–Viebahn sense, is all present inside the machine ecology. The key premise is that Butlin and Viebahn's sanctionability condition can be met in such pipelines, because reward signals sanction unreliable reporters. This pressures both camps: the proxy view must say the sub-agent's report is no assertion and no proxy, and the denial view must explain why the sanctionability condition is not met.",
  "target": "The shared anthropocentric premise in Mallory, Green & Michel, Arora and Freiman & Miller that the interpretive or authorial relation always runs through a human.",
  "risk": "Critics will say that machine-to-machine 'uptake' is just signalling with state updates, so the example begs the question about acceptance. Sanction by training may not be sanction by an interlocutor."
},
{
  "thesis": "Make-believe engagement with an utterance is compatible with, and sometimes the vehicle of, genuine assertion, so fictionalism and the speech-act question are not rivals; the chatbot case then shows the live question is which acts pass through the fiction, not whether the fiction excludes them.",
  "move_type": "counterexample",
  "sketch": "Mallory and Williams and Bayne treat fictionalism as an alternative to a speech-act analysis. That presupposes that if an utterance is engaged with as fiction, no genuine illocution occurs through it. Counterexamples abound. An actor in character on stage shouts 'Fire, get out!' and means it. A tabletop game master in the voice of an NPC conveys a real rules ruling. A satirist's persona issues real accusations. A hypnotherapist's role-played 'guide' gives real instructions. In such double-voiced utterances, the in-fiction speaker is pretended, but a real speaker advises or asserts through the pretence. Applied to chatbots: even if the 'assistant' is a fictional character played by the model or sustained by the user, the pretence does not entail that no one asserts. This shifts the dispute to who is the real voice behind the character. For a base model playing characters that author is arguably nobody, and for a fine-tuned assistant it is arguably the training process or the developer. The key premise is that fiction frames are transparent to illocution when the uttering party has non-fictional objectives. The upshot is that fictionalism is not a rival to proxy accounts but a layer compatible with them, which makes Mallory's view collapse into a neighbour.",
  "target": "Mallory's fictionalism as a rival to speech-act accounts, and Williams and Bayne's framing of fictionalism and proxy as alternatives to be rejected separately.",
  "risk": "Mallory may say that his fictionalism concerns the user's pretence about the machine as speaker, not a fiction frame around a human speaker's act. The analogy to double-voiced human cases then fails because there is a real speaker behind the pretence."
}
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Fictionalism about chatbots and the proxy view are not rival theories of chatbot speech but one theory described twice. In Walton's framework, what is true in the fiction ('the bot promised p') is fixed by a principle of generation, and the best principles of generation just are the proxy account's rules for when a deployer is committed. Disagreement between them concerns only whether users represent that structure by pretence or by direct belief.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Walton's props generate fictional truths through principles of the form 'if the prop has feature F, it is fictional that P'. For a chatbot, the output string is the prop. For the fictional truth 'the bot asserted p' to be useful and not arbitrary, the principle must track something real. The candidates are who is answerable for p, who can be corrected, and who bears reputational or legal cost. Those are the facts proxy theorists (Green and Michel, Arora) cite as making the deployer the speaker. A fictionalist who withholds the proxy facts gets arbitrary fictions, and a proxy theorist who adds that users hear the output as 'the bot says p' has added a representational layer, which is the fiction. The key premise is that principles of generation are individuated by what they track, not by the attitude users take to the output. So the two views assign the same truth-makers to the same speech-act ascriptions. What remains is a psychological question about whether users make-believe or believe, and it does not bear on whether any speech act occurred. Williams and Bayne's claim that both views are inadequate then gets reread: they fail for the same reason, because neither says what the generation principle is when no deployer stands behind the output.",
    "target": "The treatment of proxy accounts (Green and Michel; Arora) and fictionalism (Mallory) as distinct alternatives, including Williams and Bayne's separate rejection of each.",
    "risk": "Fictionalists may insist that the principles of generation are internal to the game and need not track deployer commitment, for example where users engage with a persona no one stands behind. Then the two views diverge in extension."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The fiction in chatbot speech sits on the production side, not the consumption side. A model completing an assistant-persona transcript is in the position of an author writing a character's dialogue. Searle's pretended illocutions are therefore the right base case, and users' engagement with chatbots need not be fictional at all.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Mallory locates the make-believe in users, who pretend that the chatbot says things. But a base model trained on text is a continuer of documents, and the 'assistant' is a character in a transcript it is extending. This is the role-play picture (Shanahan) read in speech-act terms. Searle and Austin treat an author's writing of a character's assertion as a non-serious illocution that commits the author to nothing. If so, the realist-versus-denier debate has been looking for the wrong thing. 'Does the model assert?' presupposes a serious frame, whereas the model's default frame is authorial. The real question is whether post-training (RLHF, groundedness tuning) shifts the system from authorial pretence to serious performance, or only makes the pretence more stable. The key premise is that the serious/non-serious distinction applies to the producer's mode of speech act, and that distinction can be asked of a system without asking whether it has intentions. Users can then be perfectly literal consumers of what is, on the producer side, fiction. That is a structurally odd and so far undescribed situation, like reading a very reliable novel as a reference book. It also explains why the intention and sanction conditions seem unsatisfied: they are conditions on serious performance, which was never the model's default mode.",
    "target": "Mallory's placement of the pretence in the user, and the shared assumption of Gubelmann, Butlin and Viebahn, and Attah that the issue is whether the model performs serious illocutions.",
    "risk": "The author analogy needs an authorial stance, which is a form of agency, so the view may covertly grant what deniers deny. Also, fine-tuned models arguably no longer continue transcripts but are optimised to output the answer."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "For conventional speech acts such as promising, apologising and advising, fictionalism about chatbots is unstable. Collective make-believe that a bot promised, backed by uptake and enforced norms, is how such acts come to exist, so the 'literal versus interpreted' dispute reduces to a question about whether the practice has yet become sanction-bearing.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Fictionalism needs a contrast between pretending that X is F and X's being F. For institutional facts that contrast fails. Money, promises and offices exist because of collective acceptance, which began as 'treat this as' (Searle's status functions, Austin's uptake). If users are collectively invited by designers to hold 'the bot promised' true, and they resent, complain, demand redress and reward consistency, then the make-believe has acquired the constitutive role that makes a promise a promise. Magnus, Buccella and D'Cruz say chatbot apologies are bullshit because the bot lacks moral agency. Mallory-style fictionalism can say the apology is only pretended. But if pretended apologies reliably restore trust and attract complaint when breached, the denial is only about a stage in a developing practice. The key premise is that for conventionally constituted acts, sustained, norm-backed make-believe is sufficient for the real thing. The debate is then about the stage of social uptake, and it cannot be settled by looking at the model's properties. Butlin and Viebahn's sanction condition becomes the empirical hinge.",
    "target": "Mallory's fictionalism, and the Denial group's assumption that speech-act status is fixed by the performer's properties.",
    "risk": "Apology and sincere assertion carry sincerity conditions that make-believe cannot satisfy, so the constitutive-convention argument may cover only the conventional core of promising, not apology. Sanctioning an entity that cannot suffer sanctions may also fail to count as the real practice."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Once fictionalism is stripped of the claim that the pretence is dispensable, it is Dennettian interpretivism with a different label, and the speech-act debate is the old realism-versus-instrumentalism dispute about intentional states. Mallory, Attah and the proxy theorists differ only in how much 'literally' they pack into the word 'speech act'.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Fictionalism (Yablo, Walton) gets its bite from the idea that the make-believe vocabulary could in principle be replaced by literal talk and is kept for convenience. For chatbots, the literal replacement would be a description in terms of weights, sampling and training distributions. No user can use that description to predict, trust or correct what the system will say, so the speech-act vocabulary is not dispensable at the grain at which users operate. An indispensable, predictively powerful vocabulary applied on the basis of real patterns is what Dennett calls the intentional stance, and for him it is not pretence. Attah's graded control-based intention reads the same patterns as inner states, and Gubelmann's malfunction-versus-wrong-doing test is an interpretivist test. The key premise is that the fictionalism the literature has in mind, with a pretence-ascribing user and a literally speechless system, requires dispensability, which fails here. Hence the 'literal speech act versus mere interpretation' question is a choice about how much realism to read into pattern-based ascription, as in the long-running debate over beliefs, and nothing specific to speech acts is at stake.",
    "target": "The presentation of Mallory's fictionalism and Attah's functionalism as opposed answers to the question whether LLMs literally have communicative intentions.",
    "risk": "Speech acts, unlike beliefs, are normatively individuated (commitments, entitlements), so the interpretivist parallel may fail. A critic may say the move merely renames the debate and does not dissolve it."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Fictionalism about chatbots is unstable because every way of saying who authors the fiction turns it into another view: a designer author gives the proxy view, a model author gives the attributing view, and no author gives quasi-testimony.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "In Walton's framework, a fiction needs prescriptions to imagine. These come either from an agent who intends the prop to be used that way (an author) or from a natural prop, such as a stump taken for a bear. Option (a): the designers and RLHF pipeline fix the persona and the prescription to imagine 'the assistant says p'. The fiction is then authored by the deployer, and the make-believe sits on top of what is functionally a proxy speech act. Option (b): the model itself generates the fiction, as in role-play-simulator pictures. Making-up fictional utterances is itself an intentional, illocutionary activity (Currie-style fictive utterance), so the model has the communicative intentions the fictionalist wanted to deny, and this is Attah's graded attribution. Option (c): no one authors it, and the output is a natural prop. Then users learn from it only because it reliably covaries with the facts, as with a thermometer, and the make-believe does no epistemic work. This is the instrument or quasi-testimony picture with a cosmetic interface story. The key premise is that prescriptions to imagine require either an intending source or a purely causal-reliabilist source, and that Mallory's fictionalism needs neither.",
    "target": "Mallory's fictionalism, and the claim shared by Williams–Bayne and Green–Michel that fictionalism and proxy accounts are separate options.",
    "risk": "Walton allows 'principles of generation' to be socially entrenched conventions with no individual author. A conventional, authorless fiction (like a folk practice) might be a stable fourth option."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Fictionalism about chatbots is stable only if make-believe sanctioning of chatbots stays make-believe, but feedback loops make the sanctions real. This satisfies the Butlin–Viebahn sanction condition and collapses fictionalism into a thin attributing view.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Fictionalists say users only pretend the chatbot asserts, advises or apologises, so no genuine speech act occurs. Butlin and Viebahn deny assertion to current systems partly because the system cannot be sanctioned by its interlocutors. But users do 'hold chatbots to account'. They complain, downvote and reprompt, and these acts feed into fine-tuning, system prompts and product decisions. They are thus pretend-reprimands with real downstream effects on future outputs, the way a pretend-reprimand of a person would. Once the practice has this structure, the claim that it is merely a game is strained. Games are insulated from consequences, and this one is not. Following Searle-style status-function thinking, a practice in which outputs are collectively treated as assertions, with commitments tracked and sanctions applied that causally shape the producer, is the kind of institution that makes something an assertion. So fictionalism must either insulate the pretense, which is false of actual deployment, or accept that the pretense has become the practice that constitutes the speech act. The key premise is that speech-act status is fixed by sanction-involving social practice, a premise the normative deniers themselves accept.",
    "target": "Mallory's fictionalism, together with the sanction condition in Butlin–Viebahn and Skiba.",
    "risk": "Downvoting reshapes a distribution through a training pipeline, and that may be too remote from the model to count as sanctioning it. If it is, the normative deniers' condition holds and the collapse fails."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "A fiction whose success condition is truth in the actual world, which is what users need to treat hallucination as a defect, is not a fiction, and fictionalism about chatbots collapses into Williams–Bayne's proto-assertion.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Fictionalism's advantage is meant to be that it explains why users can take on chatbot content without the chatbot asserting. But users appraise outputs as accurate or hallucinated, trust them for medical, legal and factual purposes, and revise beliefs on that basis. So the pretense is governed by a norm of actual-world correctness, unlike the norm of internal coherence that governs standard fictions. Walton's cases of this kind (the map, the textbook, the museum model) are representations used non-fictionally, with truth in the world as the standard. A practice of 'pretending that S says p' whose point is to be corrected against reality, and whose participants update on p, differs from assertion only in the non-assertoric label. That is just what the proto-assertion category was introduced to capture. The key premise is that a make-believe practice individuated by its correctness norm is a truth-norm-governed practice, so it cannot be told apart from proto-assertion except by labelling.",
    "target": "Mallory's fictionalism and Williams–Bayne's claim that fictionalism and proto-assertion are distinct, with fictionalism inadequate.",
    "risk": "Fictionalists may separate the prop's correctness norm from the user's real belief-forming policy, as with a fictional narrator who sometimes states real facts. The hallucination data may then be handled without shifting the norm."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Fictionalism about chatbots is stable only given robust realism about human communicative intention, and on the interpretivist or functional theories that make deflationary views attractive it collapses into literalism.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "The contrast the fictionalist draws, between users pretending the chatbot speaks and humans really speaking, needs a fact about human speakers that chatbots lack, namely real intentions. Mallory's motivation is to avoid committing to chatbot mental states. But the live accounts of intention that make machine attribution tractable are interpretivism (Dennett), the functional control conception (Attah), and the claim that Gricean mind-reading is empirically untenable even for children and animals. On these accounts, intention-ascription is licensed by predictive and explanatory success of the stance. If chatbot-directed pretense is predictively and explanatorily indispensable, with users anticipating, correcting and cooperating, then by the same criterion that makes human intentions real, the 'fictional' intentions are real, though thin. Fictionalism about X requires that the stance toward X be dispensable in principle without loss of truth-tracking. The key premise is that the fictionalist's pretended-to-literal contrast has no principled location unless intentional states are realistically distinguished from stance-relative ones. The people who most want to deflate machine speech acts are also usually the ones who deny that realism.",
    "target": "Mallory's fictionalism and the shared assumption that fictionalism is a deflationary option compatible with a flat, naturalistic theory of mind.",
    "risk": "The fictionalist can hold realism about human intention and need not endorse interpretivism, so the collapse would show only a conditional dependence."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Gubelmann's malfunction-versus-deliberate-wrong test does not detect intention. It detects whether the system itself holds the standard against which its outputs count as successes or failures, so his organismic argument and the proxy accounts are one thesis, that LLM outputs are answerable to standards owned by someone else.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "The test asks whether we can sensibly separate 'it broke' from 'it did the wrong thing on purpose'. That separation is available only if the system has its own standard of success, which it can fall short of by accident or flout deliberately. In his cases (ChatGPT, the order-translating assistant, RoboCop) the only standards in play are the designers' or deployers'. So the test shows that standard-ownership is external. Intention is then an intermediate label that adds nothing. Proxy theorists (Green and Michel, Arora) say the same thing about the ownership of the act. The apparent split between 'denial on organismic grounds' and 'relocation to the human' is therefore one claim about standard-ownership, stated once in metaphysical vocabulary and once in social-normative vocabulary. The real dispute is whether a speech act requires its performer to own its success standard. Illocutionary success conditions are plausibly set by convention and uptake, not by the speaker's own goals, so Gubelmann's premise is the contestable one.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's intention test and the assumed independence of the 'denial' and 'proxy' camps",
    "risk": "Gubelmann could say that an agent's own goal-setting also settles whether it deviated deliberately, so standard-ownership is not separable from intention. The identification would then be a restatement of his view rather than a reduction of it."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "On Gubelmann's own Kantian source, organismic purposiveness is a regulative maxim of reflective judgment, not a constitutive property. His organism/mechanism criterion is therefore itself an interpretive stance, and the literature's contrast between 'literally has intentions' and 'is merely interpreted as having them' is not available on his grounds.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "In the Critique of Judgment, Kant says we must judge organisms as natural purposes, as if their parts were reciprocally cause and effect, because our understanding cannot otherwise grasp them. He denies that this is a constitutive claim about things in themselves. Gubelmann takes over the organism/mechanism contrast as if it marked an ontological line between systems that have goals and systems that are given them. If the line is regulative, then 'LLMs lack goals of their own' means that we are not rationally compelled to judge them teleologically. That is a claim about our explanatory needs, which is exactly what the 'interpreted as' side of the headline question says. The dispute is then not literal versus interpretive. It is about whether the best explanation of LLM output requires the teleological stance, which is an empirical question about the explanatory economy of intentional versus mechanistic descriptions. Attah's graded functional notion of intention then lands close to a Kantian position.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's Kantian grounding, and the 'literal versus interpretive' framing of the whole area",
    "risk": "Kantian exegesis is contestable: the later 'self-organising' passages can be read as more than heuristic, and Gubelmann might adopt a neo-Kantian naturalised reading (Weber and Varela) on which autonomy is constitutive."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The question 'can an LLM perform speech acts?' is ill-posed until the speaker is individuated, and Gubelmann's cognitive-autonomy concession attaches to the training process while his speech-act denial attaches to the deployed frozen artefact, so his two claims are about different candidate subjects.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Gubelmann grants LLMs cognitive autonomy because they organise their own internal functional differentiation during training and adapt to new tasks. But the self-organisation happens in the training loop, which includes data, optimiser, reward signal and engineers. At deployment the weights are frozen, and any adaptation is in-context. 'The LLM' can denote the training-and-deployment pipeline, the weight file, a running instance, a session, or the persona simulated in a conversation. Each has a different profile for autonomy, persistence and answerability. The organism-style autonomy he concedes belongs to the pipeline, which is not what utters tokens. The thing that utters tokens is a static function with no self-organisation at the time of speaking. The persona that appears to promise or apologise is not the thing that was trained. The debate looks like one dispute over 'the model', but it is several disputes over different subjects. Verdicts can then diverge systematically: the pipeline-level subject may meet autonomy conditions but not speaker conditions, while the persona-level subject may meet speaker conditions but not autonomy. The area's positions would then be answers to different questions.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's 'cognitive autonomy' concession, and all views that speak of 'the LLM' as one candidate speaker (including Attah, Butlin and Viebahn, and Williams and Bayne)",
    "risk": "The same individuation worry is already implicit in the fictionalist and persona literature (Shanahan), so the claim may amount to a restatement of it. It also does not by itself decide any of the positions."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Gubelmann's thesis that LLMs cannot speak 'for now' is close to analytic. He defines the artificial as what has externally fixed purposes and concedes that a system with self-set purposes would no longer be artificial, so what is at stake is the artificial/autonomous boundary and not speech acts.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "His argument has three parts. Speech needs intention. Intention needs purposes that the system sets for itself. A mechanism is by definition a system whose purposes are imposed from outside. His conclusion that future non-biological speakers 'would no longer be artificial' shows that artificiality has been defined so as to exclude autonomy. 'Artificial things cannot speak' is then true by stipulation, and the empirical content is carried entirely by the question when a trained system's purposes cease to be externally fixed. That question is not one about language. It is whether reinforcement learning, self-generated objectives and long-horizon learning (the conditions Butlin discusses for agency) move a system across the line. The speech-act literature is therefore a proxy debate for the agency debate. The 'for now' is redundant because the criterion is non-linguistic, and a system's passing it would entail speech-act capacity with no further argument. This predicts that on his view no further linguistic evidence could bear on the matter.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's conclusion and the assumption that the speech-act question adds anything to the agency question",
    "risk": "Gubelmann may say that 'artificial' is a contingent description of current LLMs and that his argument is substantive, resting on the claim that the purposes are in fact externally fixed. The apparent analyticity would then be an artefact of my reading."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Gubelmann's mechanism/organism contrast sorts systems by the wrong axis: what matters is whether a system's purposes were stipulated by a designer's intention or produced by selection, and on that axis LLMs align with organisms against artefacts like the coffee machine, which also undermines proxy accounts.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Gubelmann takes Kant's line that mechanisms have externally imposed purposes while organisms have their own. Human goals are also not self-originated: they come from natural selection and upbringing, neither of which is an intentional self-legislation. So 'external' cannot mean 'not originating in the system', and the real contrast is etiological. Artefact purposes are derived from a designer's intention-content (the coffee machine makes coffee because someone meant it to). Organismic purposes are fixed by selection, with no author who has them in mind. Gradient descent on a loss and RLHF look much more like selection than like stipulation: no designer intends any particular output or sub-goal, and many learned dispositions are unintended. On this axis LLMs sit between the two and are not clearly on the artefact side. Two consequences follow. (a) Gubelmann's 'externally fixed goals' premise is false of the goals that operate at output time. (b) Proxy accounts (Green and Michel, Arora) need a designer's intention or objective to ground the proxy relation, and for selected dispositions no such objective exists, so proxy-hood fails exactly where the denial argument says purposes are external. The two groups of views cannot both be right about where the purposes come from.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's Kantian premise that LLM goals are externally imposed, together with the proxy accounts' assumption that the designer's objective is available to ground proxy speech acts.",
    "risk": "Selection-fixed purposes may not suffice for Gubelmann's 'own goals', which he ties to ongoing self-maintenance, so the etiological axis may be orthogonal to his actual criterion. Defenders may also say the loss function is itself a designer-intended objective."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "If illocutionary acts are individuated Austin-style, the absence of intention in an LLM yields an 'abuse' (insincere or defective performance) rather than a 'misfire' (no act), so Gubelmann's argument, and the denials resting on it, establish at most that every LLM speech act is infelicitous in a particular way, not that none occurs.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Gubelmann treats the speech act as the basic unit following Austin and Searle, then runs a Davidsonian argument that acting requires intention. Austin separates misfires, where the conventional procedure is not properly executed and the act is void, from abuses, where the act is performed but the speaker lacks the required thoughts, feelings or intentions (insincere promises still bind). Strawson's Gricean reading makes intention constitutive for non-conventional illocutions such as warning or suggesting. For conventional illocutions such as promising, apologising, naming, and perhaps asserting under norm-based accounts, intention enters only the sincerity conditions. The denial argument is therefore two-tiered: for intention-constituted acts the lack of intention voids the act, but for conventional acts it yields an abuse, provided the speaker has the standing and the procedure is executed. Whether an LLM can execute the procedure then becomes a question about standing (Butlin and Viebahn's sanctionability), not about intention. This reclassifies the debate: Gubelmann's route is strong against advising and hinting and weak against promising and apologising, while Magnus et al.'s apology case turns on moral standing and not on intention at all.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's uniform inference from 'no intention' to 'no speech act', and the intention-first denials generally.",
    "risk": "Austin's 'appropriate persons' clause may import capacity conditions that smuggle intention back in, and many philosophers think the conventional/intentional division is not clean enough to carry the load."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Gubelmann's malfunction-versus-deliberate-wrongdoing test equivocates between failing the function a system was optimised for and failing the function its output claims to serve, and once these are separated the test gives verdicts that vary by layer instead of a uniform 'no intention'.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "The test asks whether we can sensibly distinguish a system's malfunctioning from its deliberately doing the wrong thing. 'Malfunction' needs a function standard. A deployed chatbot has at least two. The optimisation-level function (predict or please) is met even by hallucinations and flattery, which are therefore neither malfunction nor deliberate wrong. The role-level function is the one the output's role claims, such as truthful assistant or advisor, and hallucinations are failures of that. Gubelmann's thought experiments (ChatGPT, the order-translating assistant, RoboCop) run the test against a single function, so the contrast collapses. Once the layers are separated the contrast is empirically tractable at the role level, because interventions can tell sandbagging, sycophancy that tracks the user's beliefs, or strategic deception from competence failure by manipulating incentives and checking counterfactual sensitivity. Whether the persona-level contrast tracks intention remains open. But the denial no longer follows from the test, and the question shifts from 'does the LLM have intentions' to 'which layer, if any, is the candidate agent'. This is a distinction between function-standards that none of the denial, proxy or attributing camps draws.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's operational test for intention and the shared assumption that a system has one function against which success and failure are measured.",
    "risk": "Role-level 'deliberate wrongdoing' may just be pattern completion of a deceptive character, so the test would be passed trivially. Gubelmann could reply that the intention-relevant contrast must be internal to the system, not an interpretive overlay."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Gubelmann's cognitive autonomy for LLMs is a fact about training history, whereas the autonomy intention requires is a fact about the ongoing episode of action, so the organismic argument misdescribes which feature LLMs lack: it is not material self-maintenance but synchronic self-organisation during the utterance.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Gubelmann grants LLMs cognitive autonomy because they organise their own internal functional differentiation in training and adapt to new tasks, and denies material autonomy because they do not maintain themselves. He then infers a mechanism with organismic traits, and tentatively dualism. But these two autonomies are measured at different times. Training-time self-organisation is phylogenetic in the organismic analogy, like the selection history that shaped a lineage. At inference the weights are frozen and nothing is being self-organised except in-context state. Kant's organism is defined by continuous reciprocal production of parts and whole while acting, which is a synchronic condition. So the proper comparison for the LLM's training-time organisation is the evolution of a species, and the proper question about an utterance is whether anything is self-organising now. That reclassifies the options: against Gubelmann, material self-maintenance is a red herring, since a system could maintain itself materially yet be fixed at inference, and a system that is not materially self-maintaining could still modify itself synchronically (continual-learning agents like Reflexion, Butlin's cases). Butlin's sensitivity-to-instrumental-value criterion turns out to be a synchronic-autonomy criterion, and the dualism moral loses its footing.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's tripartite autonomy (material, cognitive, moral) and his conclusion that cognitive autonomy without material autonomy supports dualism; also the loose use of 'self-organisation' across Gubelmann, Butlin and Barandiaran and Almendros.",
    "risk": "Human intentions also lean heavily on diachronic history, so the synchronic requirement may be too strong; and in-context learning may already count as synchronic self-organisation, which would dissolve the contrast."
  }
]

