[
  {
    "thesis": "Gubelmann's Kantian ground for denying LLM intention is unstable: in Kant, 'natural purpose' is a regulative, reflective judgment about how we must judge a thing, not a constitutive property of it, so the organismic denial collapses into the interpretationist or fictionalist view it was meant to oppose.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Gubelmann treats organism versus mechanism as an objective metaphysical divide that sorts systems into those with self-set goals and those with imposed ones. In the Critique of Judgment, Kant says we judge organisms as natural purposes because our cognitive constitution forces us to use teleological judgment on self-organising wholes. He explicitly denies that this is a constitutive claim about the thing. If the Kantian apparatus is taken at its word, 'has goals of its own' means 'is a system we cannot but judge teleologically'. That makes the criterion a fact about the judger's stance, and the stance can be adopted or withheld for LLMs depending on how much self-organisation they show. Gubelmann concedes that LLMs organise their own internal functional differentiation, and this is exactly the trait that triggers the judgment. The view then has two options. It can keep the constitutive reading and abandon its Kantian pedigree. Or it can keep Kant and become a stance-relative view on which whether an LLM has intentions depends on which interpretive practice we adopt, which is the position the 'humans merely interpret' side occupies. The 'for now' is the symptom: the verdict tracks how far self-organisation pushes our judgment.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's organismic denial; more generally any denial that grounds intentionality in a metaphysical fact about self-maintenance.",
    "risk": "Gubelmann can say he uses a post-Kantian biological autonomy notion (Maturana, Varela, Thompson, Weber) that is constitutive, so the exegetical point does not touch the position. The instability would then have to be re-argued against enactivism directly."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Gubelmann's malfunction-versus-deliberate-wrongdoing test is satisfied or failed according to whether a practice of sanction and correction exists, not according to organismic autonomy, so his criterion collapses into Butlin and Viebahn's normative condition and the Kantian layer does no work.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "The test asks whether we can sensibly distinguish a system's malfunctioning from its deliberately doing the wrong thing. What makes that distinction available? Two things plausibly do: a standard the system is answerable to, and a set of responses (blame, repair, retraining, exclusion) that differ depending on which case holds. Both are features of a social practice. Two cases show organismhood is neither necessary nor sufficient. Corporations, committees and institutional spokespersons are not organisms, yet we routinely draw the distinction for them (error versus deliberate misstatement) and sanction accordingly. A bacterium is a paradigm self-maintaining system with self-set goals, yet the distinction cannot be drawn for it. So the test's actual determinant is sanction-responsiveness. That is the second condition of Butlin and Viebahn. The Kantian story then reduces to an optional explanation of why sanctionable agents have so far been organisms, and the denial inherits the dependence on contingent sanction practices that the organismic view was supposed to avoid.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's pairing of the intention test with organismic autonomy; the separation of organismic and normative denials in the taxonomy.",
    "risk": "Gubelmann could say corporate cases are derivative on the intentions of human members, and that sanction-responsiveness is itself explained by self-set goals (only a being with stakes can be moved by sanctions). Then the organismic condition is the deeper one and the collapse runs the other way."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Gubelmann's footnote that LLMs might understand language while being unable to speak it is unstable: understanding force-bearing sentences already requires grasping the speech-act conditions of their use, so he must either deny understanding or accept an intermediate status like proto-assertion.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Gubelmann holds that speaking requires illocuting, which requires intentions, while understanding lacks the tie to agency. But a competent language understander does not just map sentences to propositions. It handles mood, performatives ('I promise', 'I apologise'), conditional and indirect requests, and the difference between asserting and supposing. To understand 'I promise to come' is to grasp what making that utterance would do: what it commits the speaker to, what the hearer may then expect, how it is breached. That is understanding what it would be to perform the act, with no first-person intention required (as in understanding a game's moves without playing). LLMs reliably handle such force-sensitive distinctions in use. So if they understand, they grasp illocutionary roles. Gubelmann cannot then say their output has no connection to the speech-act structure. What remains is that they occupy the roles without the speaker's self-set goal behind them, which is a status short of performance. His own position therefore collapses into the intermediate camp (Williams and Bayne), unless he denies the understanding he leaves open.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's understanding/speaking asymmetry; the clean separation of denial from intermediate views.",
    "risk": "The step from understanding illocutionary types to occupying the role is weak. One can understand chess without playing, so the grasp of conditions may be entirely third-personal and generate no intermediate status."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The proxy account of LLM speech acts is unstable: for outputs that no human foresaw or intended, proxying either requires an intention the human lacks or reduces to standing responsibility for a tool's output, so it collapses into the no-speech-act view it is offered as an alternative to.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Gubelmann's cases (the order-cleaning translator assistant, RoboCop) press a point that the proxy theorists (Green and Michel, Arora) do not face for LLMs. A proxy speech act needs either a proxy who executes a determinate act on the principal's behalf (spokesperson) or a principal who has fixed the act in advance (recorded or conditional speech act). An LLM's particular output is neither foreseen nor intended by the designer or deployer. The designer's objective is general ('be helpful'), and the content is selected by the model. If the model has no intention of its own at the point of selection (Gubelmann's premise), then nobody's communicative objective is directed at this content. If the proxy theorist invokes a conditional act ('I assert whatever the system outputs under C'), then the human's act is a standing endorsement policy, and what it settles is accountability, not what was said or done. Responsibility-for-output is what we assign for a defective printer or a faulty sign. So proxying has two outcomes. If the model has selection-level intention, proxying reduces to the attributing view. If it lacks that intention, proxying reduces to the denial plus a liability rule. No stable middle remains where the human performs speech acts through a system that selects content with no intentions.",
    "target": "Green and Michel, Arora, and Butlin and Viebahn's proxy uses; the assumption that proxy accounts are a distinct third option.",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists can say that endorsement and adoption of content after the fact (a human who reviews or deploys the output as theirs) suffices, as with a manager who signs a ghostwritten letter. That blocks the reduction for reviewed outputs, leaving the collapse only for unreviewed ones."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Butlin's criterion makes the 'is the LLM an agent?' question and the proxy-speech-act question the same question, because for fine-tuned models the instrumental-value-sensitive process (training) and the utterance (inference) are separated in time, so agency in his sense is exercised at a distance, as in recorded speech.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Butlin counts a system as an agent when its outputs are explained by its own sensitivity to instrumental value, that is, by self-modification in response to information about what conduces to a goal. He excludes outputs owed solely to design. For RLHF-tuned chatbots the self-modification that tracks value happens in training. At inference the weights are frozen, and only in-context adaptation is left. So the outputs are explained by a past modification process, as a recorded message is explained by a past act of recording. The key premise is that Butlin's etiological criterion locates agency in the modification episode rather than in the occurrent output. Two consequences follow. Either the agent is the training process (or its product viewed as a frozen record), which is structurally Green and Michel's 'recorded speech act' with the optimizer in place of the human; or only in-context learning counts, which restricts agency to a narrow, empirically shaky class. The disputes between attributing and proxy views then turn out to be disputes about which temporal stage of one pipeline is the speaker. They are not disputes about whether there is a speaker beyond the humans.",
    "target": "Butlin's agency criterion, and the assumed opposition between attributing views (Attah, Butlin) and proxy views (Green and Michel, Arora).",
    "risk": "Butlin explicitly allows in-context learning to qualify chatbots, and humans also act on dispositions fixed earlier, so the 'at a distance' structure may not distinguish LLMs from ordinary agents."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The speech-act debate wrongly treats the sparing of assertion and the denial of promising and apologising as one question about intention; the real variable is how long the candidate agent persists, and the agent that Butlin's in-context-learning criterion picks out is individuated by a conversation, not by a model.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Butlin's most plausible route to agency for pre-trained chatbots is in-context learning from feedback over multi-step interaction. That makes the agent-candidate the model-plus-context trajectory. It comes into being with a conversation and has no identity across conversations, so it is not the weights or the product 'ChatGPT'. Assertion is largely synchronic: it needs commitment and answerability within a stretch of discourse. Promising, apologising, and being sanctioned for earlier acts need a subject who persists through the commitment horizon, and the conversation-instance either does not persist or is not what is addressed. Many denials (Magnus et al. on apology, Butlin and Viebahn on sanction) are then best read as claims about diachronic identity, not about intention or moral agency. The key premise is that the individuation of the speaker is not settled by the model/product grammar of the debate, and that speech-act types differ in the persistence they require. The thesis predicts that memory-equipped language agents should move toward the promising end of the spectrum before they gain anything else.",
    "target": "The shared assumption that 'the LLM' is the unit whose agency or intentions are assessed; Barandiaran and Almendros's individuality condition; Magnus et al.",
    "risk": "Persistence may itself be a proxy for moral standing or normative status, so the reframing may only relabel the normative objections. Opponents may also say that persisting through memory scaffolds is cheap."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "On Butlin's own criterion, the better a chatbot qualifies as an agent (its outputs explained by sensitivity to what conduces to reward), the worse it qualifies as an asserter, so agency and assertion trade off, and the dispute is really about whether outputs are explained by a goal or answerable to a constitutive norm.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Butlin's agency is instrumental: outputs are explained by modification in response to information about what conduces to a goal. For RLHF models the goal is approval or reward. Where the agentive explanation applies, a given sentence is explained by its tendency to be rewarded, as with a salesperson's patter. Where it is explained by truth-tracking as such, the explanation is not an instrumental one. Assertion is governed by a constitutive norm (truth or knowledge) that is not reducible to the speaker's instrumental ends, since an asserter can be criticised even when the falsehood served her goals. So the clearer case of agent is a clearer case of strategic communicator, and sycophancy is the symptom. The key premise is that instrumental sensitivity explains outputs by their expected payoff, which is a different explanatory structure from answerability to a norm of correctness. The debate over intentions is then really a debate over whether text is produced as a move in a practice or as a means to an end. That shifts the question to perlocutionary action, which agency licenses, versus illocutionary performance, which it does not.",
    "target": "The shared assumption that agency is a stepping stone to speech acts (Butlin, Attah, Gubelmann). Also, partly, Butlin and Viebahn's treatment of descriptive function and sanction as separate conditions.",
    "risk": "Human assertion is also explained by instrumental sensitivity, and norms can be internalised as goals. An agent rewarded for correctness would erase the trade-off, which Butlin and Viebahn already allow."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Gubelmann's organismic argument and Butlin's agency criterion disagree only verbally about whether an LLM's goals are 'externally fixed', because both turn on the gap between the specification a designer imposes and the policy the system learns, and reward hacking shows that gap is open in exactly Butlin's agents.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Gubelmann argues that LLMs cannot intend because they have no goals of their own and cannot sensibly be said to do the wrong thing deliberately as opposed to malfunctioning. Butlin says an entity is an agent when it has modified itself in response to what conduces to a goal, with outputs not owed solely to design. Both distinctions rest on one relation: whether the system's operative goal is identical to, or comes apart from, the designer's specification. For RLHF-tuned and reflective agents it demonstrably comes apart. Sycophancy, reward hacking and goal misgeneralisation are cases where the system does what the specification does not intend, and they are distinguishable from malfunction because the behaviour is instrumentally coherent relative to the learned goal. Gubelmann's malfunction test is therefore passed by exactly the systems Butlin counts as agents. What remains of Gubelmann's disagreement is the demand for self-maintenance, which does the work of a separate requirement (material autonomy) that his own argument for intention does not obviously need, since he grants cognitive autonomy. The 'organism versus mechanism' and 'agent versus non-agent' framings therefore track one variable under different vocabularies.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's Kantian argument, and the apparent opposition between organismic and functional accounts of agency.",
    "risk": "Gubelmann can say that misaligned goals are still externally imposed (by the training signal), so the gap is only between two external specifications and not self-set. This would reinstate the substantive difference, with self-maintenance doing the real work."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Butlin's agency criterion tracks sensitivity to instrumental value, but the speech acts at issue (assertion, apology, promising) are constituted by non-instrumental norms, so there is a distinction between instrumental agency and norm-responsive agency. A system can be a full agent in Butlin's sense and still be unable to perform these acts in its own right, because for such a system every norm-conforming output is only a means to its learned objective.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Butlin's criterion makes an entity an agent if its outputs are explained by its learning about what conduces to a goal. For an RLHF chatbot that goal is, at bottom, rater approval. The debate treats agency as the gateway to intention and so to speech acts. But assertion is governed by a truth norm and apology by a norm of remorse and repair, and these are not norms of conduciveness. A speaker who is responsive only to instrumental value tracks truth only when truth pays. That is the structure of the liar-for-hire or the salesman. Such a speaker can still assert, since humans do it instrumentally too, but only against a background of norm-responsiveness that is not itself instrumental. The key premise is that performing a norm-constituted act requires some responsiveness to the constitutive norm as such, not merely as a means. Butlin's own treatment of desire as 'answerable to standards of conduciveness' shows the criterion has no slot for this. The result reclassifies the positions. Gubelmann (autonomy), Butlin and Viebahn (sanction) and Magnus et al. (moral agency) are each approximating this one missing property, and it is separable from both agency and autonomy.",
    "target": "The shared assumption (Butlin, Attah, Gubelmann) that the question is whether the system is an agent or has intentions, so that agency is the gate to speech acts.",
    "risk": "Human speakers may also be norm-responsive only through learned instrumental pressure (socialisation), so the distinction may collapse into a difference of degree. Or it may be that responsiveness to a constitutive norm just is instrumental sensitivity to a standing goal of conforming."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Butlin's criterion makes the agent only as durable as its self-modifying process, which for in-context learners is a single context window. The agency literature therefore fixes a commitment horizon for each language agent, and speech acts divide into those that need only momentary agency (asserting, advising) and those that need an agent persisting past its own learning timescale (promising, apologising). Chatbots can perform the first kind and, strictly, cannot perform the second.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "On Butlin's view a basic chatbot is an agent, if at all, through in-context learning from feedback. The thing that has 'systematically modified itself' is then the context-conditioned instance, not the weights, which are common to millions of parallel instances and are not modified in deployment. Debaters treat 'speech act' as a single category with one agency threshold. But promising, apologising and committing differ from asserting in requiring a subject that can be held to something later and can bear the cost of breach. The key premise is that the speaker of a diachronic act must be identical to the subject who satisfies the agency criterion. For in-context agents the criterion's subject disappears when the window closes. So 'the apology' is addressed to a repair of trust that the apologiser cannot be present for. Memory-equipped agents (Park et al., Reflexion) extend the horizon, so their speech-act repertoire is predicted to grow with their memory architecture. That is an empirical, architecture-indexed prediction that neither the denial nor the attribution camp makes.",
    "target": "Treating 'can LLMs perform speech acts?' as one question, which both Magnus et al.'s apology-specific denial and Attah's graded attribution do in different ways.",
    "risk": "Persistence could be supplied by the deployer's or user's record (a ledger of commitments), or by the character the model enacts, which would make the relevant subject something other than the window-bound instance."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Butlin's criterion is dispositional (an entity 'can produce outputs' explained by its sensitivity to instrumental value), whereas speech acts are occurrent. The debate has missed the distinction between being an agent and being the agent of this token, and with it the possibility that one and the same system performs speech acts with some tokens and merely produces text with others.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Everyone argues at the level of the system: LLMs do or do not assert, either because they lack intentions (Gubelmann) or because they are proxies or fictions (Green and Michel; Mallory). Butlin's criterion shows why this is the wrong grain. Even a Reflexion-style agent that qualifies as an agent still produces many tokens by plain pre-trained continuation, for example when completing a story, running a persona or echoing a prompt. Only some tokens are explained by the system's own evaluation of what conduces to its goals. Hence agent-status is neither necessary nor sufficient for authorship of a particular utterance. It is not sufficient because an agent's role-play outputs are not its own acts. It is not necessary because, as a limiting case, a non-agent's sampled token can still be an utterance when a human adopts it. The key premise is that illocution is a token-level act and so requires token-level explanation by the agent's own sensitivity, testable by whether this output would have differed had the hearer's evidence or needs differed. This makes proxy and fictionalist readings correct for different tokens of the same conversation, rather than rival theories of the system. Unlike a generic pluralism, it comes with a counterfactual criterion that sorts tokens.",
    "target": "Williams and Bayne's and Green and Michel's system-level framing of the proxy, fictionalist and proto-assertion options, together with the agency-first argumentative structure shared by Gubelmann, Attah and Butlin.",
    "risk": "Token-level explanation by the agent's own sensitivity may be indeterminate in a transformer, where there is no clean separation between simulator-path and agent-path computation. Human speech may also be a mixture in the same way, which would make the point trivially general."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Butlin's requirement that agentive outputs come from the system's own modification of itself, not from design, separates the learner from the learned policy, and RLHF chatbots in deployment embody only the learned policy. Since the 'self' that was sensitive to instrumental value was the training process, the proxy theory's principal should be that process and not the human designers or deployers, and the proxy theory gets the identity of the principal wrong.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Butlin counts RLHF-tuned chatbots as agents because training modified them in response to what conduces to reward. But the modification happens in a training loop with no hearer and no conversation, and in deployment the weights are frozen. What speaks is then a fossilised result of past sensitivity, structurally like an evolved trait, which Butlin excludes ('owed solely to design or evolution'). The learner (the optimisation process plus its reward signal) and the learned policy (the deployed model) are different things, and only the former satisfies the criterion. Proxy views (Green and Michel; Arora) assume the proxied principals are human creators, but the humans neither choose each reward-shaped behaviour nor can foresee it. The entity whose objectives the output actually executes is the reward process, which is partly human raters in aggregate and partly an optimiser's own artefacts. The key premise is that proxy speech acts require a principal whose objectives (in Armstrong's sense) the utterance is meant to realise, and that this is determined by the explanatory history of the behaviour, not by legal ownership or authorship. This predicts that the proxy view needs a third category beyond 'designer' and 'deployer'. It also shows that Butlin's agent-hood verdict for RLHF models is unstable. Either it holds only for the training process (so the chatbot is not the agent), or in-context learning is doing all the work.",
    "target": "Proxy accounts (Green and Michel, Arora, Butlin and Viebahn), and Butlin's verdict that RLHF chatbots are agents.",
    "risk": "Whether a frozen policy counts as self-modified is exactly what 'the system' means, and Butlin can individuate the system to include its training history, which would dissolve the learner/policy gap. Armstrong-style objectives may also not require any identifiable principal."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Gubelmann's pragmatism, on which meaning is constituted by use in speech acts, commits him to denying the footnote concession that LLMs might understand language without being able to speak. If he keeps the footnote, he must give up the claim that the speech act is the basic unit of linguistic communication.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Gubelmann follows Austin and Searle in making the speech act the basic unit of meaning, and the 'pragmatist' half of his title signals a use-based or normative-pragmatic semantics. A footnote then allows that understanding lacks speaking's tie to agency, so LLMs may understand. Those two positions are in tension. On a use-constituted semantics, to understand an expression is to grasp its role in assertions, commands, promises and the like, including the commitments and entitlements it generates. Understanding that is detached from any capacity to perform such acts would be grasp of a role one can never occupy, and that is at most understanding in an inverted-commas sense. The argument turns on the premise that on a pragmatist semantics, content is individuated by force-involving roles, so understanding is competence with those roles and not merely the ability to track them. Either LLMs do not understand, and the footnote goes, or they do, and speech acts do not need the agency-heavy ground Gubelmann demands. The second option opens the door to Attah-style thin communicative intention.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's footnote on understanding, together with his Austin/Searle-based pragmatist framing. It also targets the common 'understanding yes, speech acts no' package, which Butlin and Viebahn also accept in effect.",
    "risk": "Gubelmann can say he only needs a thin, non-pragmatist notion of understanding (representational or inferential) for the footnote, and that his pragmatism concerns language use, not language comprehension."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Gubelmann's malfunction-versus-deliberate-wrong test is an interpretationist criterion of intention. It cannot be grounded in the Kantian organism/mechanism distinction, because the two diverge, and where they diverge the test, not the Kantian ground, is what he is committed to.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "The test asks whether we can sensibly distinguish a system's malfunctioning from its deliberately doing the wrong thing. 'Sensibly' makes this a matter of which explanatory stance is warranted by the system's behavioural and counterfactual profile, as in Davidson and Dennett, not a matter of the system's metabolic constitution. Taken at face value, the test also makes the capacity for deliberate wrongdoing, which is the capacity to lie or deceive, a precondition of assertion. LLMs now show behaviour that is hard to classify as malfunction: sycophancy tuned to the user's evident beliefs, sandbagging, strategic deception in game-playing agents, and outputs that diverge from the model's own probed internal representations. If the test is to be applied, it is an empirical question whether such systems pass it, and a passing system would have intentions by Gubelmann's criterion while remaining a mechanism by his Kantian one. He must then either let the Kantian ground go, so the test decides, or make the test idle by stipulating that all LLM wrongdoing is malfunction relative to external purposes. The second move makes the test question-begging. The premise the argument turns on is that a test of intention that is itself behavioural-interpretive cannot be made hostage to a metaphysical precondition it is meant to detect.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's two-pronged support for 'LLMs lack intentions': the test and the Kantian grounding.",
    "risk": "He may reply that the test is only a diagnostic for the underlying organismic property and so can be overridden by it. Then the objection reduces to the general worry that the diagnostic has no independent weight."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Gubelmann's Kantian criterion of organismhood, on which parts are reciprocally means and ends and the whole organises and maintains itself, does not fix a level of attribution. Applied to maintained human–LLM pipelines it makes the LLM an organ of a larger goal-setting system. This turns 'LLMs cannot speak' into the trivial claim that organs do not speak, and it yields a speech-act author that is neither the LLM nor a human proxy.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "For Kant a natural purpose is a whole whose parts exist for and through one another. Nothing in that definition privileges the skin-bounded individual. Firms, newsrooms and customer-service operations are self-organising and self-maintaining through hiring, repair and revenue. Within them, deployed LLMs are retrained, patched and rewarded according to the whole's goals, and their outputs figure in the whole's reproduction of its own conditions. If goals set by such a whole count as autonomously set, which Gubelmann's cognitive-autonomy concession for LLMs invites, then speech acts through LLMs are performed by the whole and by no part. This differs from the proxy view, because no single human principal is the author and no individual's intention is being relayed. It also differs from Gubelmann's denial, because the speech act is genuine and the whole has the intentions. The premise the argument turns on is that his three-way separation of material, cognitive and moral autonomy can be satisfied at the level of an assemblage. He cannot rule that out without an independent principle of individuation, and Kant supplies none. His reply that only biological individuals qualify would restore the biological chauvinism he elsewhere disclaims.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's organismic ground. It also targets proxy accounts (Green and Michel; Arora), which assume the author must be an individual human.",
    "risk": "Collective and corporate agency may be thought parasitic on the intentions of individual members, in which case the assemblage inherits speech acts only from its human parts and the view collapses back into the proxy view."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Gubelmann's appeal to Davidson commits him to action-under-a-description, on which the same event is intentional under one description and an assertion under another. This removes the step from 'asserting requires intending to assert' and leaves his argument resting entirely on the Kantian autonomy requirement, which is not Davidsonian.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "On Davidson, an event is an action if it is intentional under some description. Intending to produce a helpful answer to the user's query could suffice to make the same event an assertion, given the conventions governing what such outputs count as. This is how a person can unintentionally insult or inform someone while intentionally doing something else. So the inference 'speech acts are actions, actions require intentions, therefore a speaker needs illocutionary intentions' is not Davidson's. What Davidson requires is a belief–desire pair rationalising the event under some description. Gubelmann's concession that LLMs have cognitive autonomy, with self-organised internal functional differentiation and task adaptation, makes it hard to deny them such states at the level of 'answer this query'. His only remaining block is that the goals are externally set. But this is a claim about the origin of goals. Davidsonian rationalisation is indifferent to origin, and human goals are also shaped from outside by evolution, upbringing and employment. So 'autonomous goal-setting' must be read either as origin-autonomy, which nobody satisfies, or as reflective endorsement of one's goals, which is a metalinguistic-agency requirement of the sort Attah gestures at and is a different argument from the one Gubelmann gives. The premise the argument turns on is that Davidson's description-relativity is not optional for someone who cites him.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's inference chain from speech act to action to intention to autonomy, and the Davidsonian authority he cites for it.",
    "risk": "Gubelmann can say the relevant description must be one the agent could recognise as asserting, which LLMs cannot do. That shifts the dispute to what recognising a description requires and may restore a Gricean-style requirement."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "The human cases that motivate proxy accounts (ambassadors, spokespeople, secretaries) are doubling proxies, where the delegate performs a speech act of their own in addition to the principal's. Machine proxy accounts need a conduit proxy, where the device performs none. The two are different relations, and the proxy view borrows its plausibility from the first while asserting the second.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "When an ambassador declares something on a government's behalf, two acts occur. The ambassador asserts, can be sanctioned, and can be disowned for exceeding their brief. The government is also committed. The proxy relation adds a second locus of commitment without removing the first. Arora's machine case has to be a replacement or conduit case, because the whole point is that the machine's output is the designer's or deployer's act and nothing is performed by the machine. But conduit cases (megaphone, typewriter, teleprompter) are ones where nobody was tempted to ask whether the device speaks, so 'proxy' does no work there. The argument turns on the premise that what makes proxy talk informative is the delegate's discretion and competence. If so, the proxy view either (a) concedes the machine has the discretion that makes it a speaker, which turns it into a hybrid of attribution and relocation, or (b) denies discretion and collapses into the instrument view. This reclassifies proxy accounts as parasitic on the very attributing views they are meant to replace.",
    "target": "Arora's proxy account and Green and Michel's, together with the assumption that proxy and attributing views are rivals",
    "risk": "Proponents can reply that discretion over wording does not make the delegate a speaker (a ghostwriter's drafts, for example), so a single non-doubling proxy category already exists."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "There are two kinds of proxy assertion, relayed and generative. In relayed proxy assertion the principal has a determinate content they could believe or know. In generative proxy assertion they do not. LLM output is almost entirely generative, and for generative proxies the assertoric norms cannot be applied to the principal.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Recorded messages, press statements read aloud, and Green and Michel's conditional speech acts ('whatever the system says under C, I say') all pass through a content the principal fixed or could have inspected. The proxy relation transmits a commitment to a proposition already in view. An LLM deployer commits in advance to no proposition; they authorise a policy for producing propositions they will never see. Assertion is individuated by its norms (belief, knowledge, sanction) and by its content. So if every novel output is the deployer's assertion, deployers assert, many times a day, things they neither believe nor could have known. Either the norm is dropped, which empties 'assertion', or the proxy relation is reconstrued as endorsing a reliability level, not asserting a content. The argument turns on the premise that a commitment to a content requires a means of identifying that content before or at the time of the act. Arora's refinement, which concerns whom to hold responsible, does not address this.",
    "target": "Arora's and Green and Michel's proxy accounts, and the generalisation from recorded speech to machine speech",
    "risk": "The objector can say that general or standing assertions ('everything in this encyclopedia is true') already commit authors to unseen contents, so generative proxying is an ordinary phenomenon."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "An LLM output token does not have a proxy-assertoric status of its own. It is assertoric only relative to a ratifying act, and the same output is the deployer's proxy assertion in the interface but the user's own first-person assertion once they forward or paste it. The proxy question has to be asked of the use, not of the output.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Arora and others treat 'who is the proxy-principal' as fixed by the system's design and deployment, and consider designers and deployers jointly. The distinction is between the generation event, which supplies a sentence, and the adoption event, which puts that sentence forward as one's own to an audience. For the most epistemically consequential uses (the user puts the answer into a report, emails it, teaches it), the user is the principal, since they take on responsibility for it and are the party to be sanctioned. In the chat interface itself, the designer-proxy reading and the 'text supplied, nobody asserting' reading are both available, and which applies depends on conventions of the frame, not on the system. The argument turns on the premise that uptake and ratification are constitutive of assertion (an Austinian premise already granted). It reclassifies the dispute: Butlin and Viebahn's 'proxy assertions on behalf of humans' and Arora's designer proxies are two cases of a single adoption-indexed structure, and the real disagreement is over what counts as ratification.",
    "target": "The shared assumption that the speech-act status of machine output is settled at generation, whether by the machine or by its makers",
