[
  {
    "thesis": "The debate looks for speech acts at the wrong grain: the assertoric act is performed once, at deployment, as a standing policy commitment, and individual chatbot outputs are instances of that act, not acts in their own right.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Arora's proxy account, like the denial, attribution and intermediate views, treats the unit of analysis as the token utterance and asks who asserts it. Her own practical worry shows why this fails. With machine-learning systems no designer or deployer has formed any attitude toward the specific content of a given output, so 'the designer asserts p by proxy' is false of almost every token. A better model is a legislature or a publisher of a reference work. They perform one act, such as 'I will stand behind what this system says in mode M', whose content is general and open-textured, and particular outputs fall under it. On this picture the questions 'does the model have intentions?', 'is the token an assertion?' and 'who is the proxy?' are all misposed. The remaining questions are what the general commitment covers and which outputs fall outside its scope (hallucinations as 'unauthorised' speech). The key premise is that illocutionary commitment can attach to a generative policy without being distributed over its instances, as it does with standing orders and published rules.",
    "target": "Arora's token-level proxy assertion, and the shared assumption of every camp that the speech-act question is about individual outputs.",
    "risk": "Defenders of proxy accounts can say that conditional or standing speech acts are already part of Green and Michel's apparatus. The reframing may then be a refinement and not a reclassification, and it struggles to explain how users hold the system to account for a single wrong answer."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The dispute over whether machines assert is a dispute about whether illocutionary acts are constituted on the hearer's side (uptake and functional role) or the speaker's side (accountable commitment), and LLMs matter only as the first case where these systematically come apart.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Arora rejects 'functionalist' ascription because machine utterances resemble human speech phenomenologically and serve the same communicative functions, and she replaces it with attribution to an accountable principal. Both sides treat this as a disagreement about machines. But the functionalist standard individuates illocutions by what the audience can do with the utterance, which is Austin's uptake. The proxy standard individuates them by who is on the hook, which is the commitment side. For humans the two are co-instantiated, so speech-act theory never had to say which one is constitutive. LLM output separates them: full uptake-conditions are met while no one has formed the commitment. The intermediate views (quasi-testimony, proto-assertion) are then best read as hedges between two constitutive theories, not as positions about machines. The key premise is that the human literature contains no argument for the priority of either side, only coincidence. The machine debate therefore cannot be settled by facts about machines, and positions on it should be predictable from prior commitments about the metaphysics of illocution.",
    "target": "Arora's rejection of functionalism, and the whole functionalist–proxy axis as a debate about AI.",
    "risk": "Norm-based theories (Butlin and Viebahn, Skiba) may already be explicit about taking the commitment side. Others may argue that uptake and commitment never separate even in LLM cases, so there is no new crux."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The proxy account's real difficulty is not about assertion but about the first-person pronoun: whatever 'I' refers to in chatbot output, it is not the designer or the machine, and so the dispute over speech-act authorship is a disguised dispute about the semantics of a fictional or persona-denoting 'I'.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Chatbot outputs are overwhelmingly first-personal ('I think', 'I apologise', 'I recommend'). Proxy accounts must say that the designer asserts or apologises by proxy, but then the output's 'I' would refer to the company. Users would hear 'we' as the natural form, and an 'I'-form apology by proxy is not an apology the company has offered. Direct accounts take 'I' to refer to the model, which presupposes agency. Fictionalism takes it to be a character, but then the speech acts are the character's. Each position is really a thesis about the reference of 'I' in these outputs: designer, system, persona, or nothing. Once that is stated, much of the apology and promise literature (Magnus et al.) turns on whether a persona can be the target of moral address, and the assertion literature turns on whether a persona can be sanctioned. The key premise is that illocutionary force in these outputs is routed through indexical reference. If the referent of 'I' is a stable persona with an interactional history, as for a fictional narrator or a brand mascot, the speech-act question reduces to ordinary questions about personas.",
    "target": "Arora, Green and Michel, and Mallory on proxy and fictional authorship.",
    "risk": "Many outputs and many acts (informing, advising in impersonal prose) carry no 'I', and a proxy theorist can say the pronoun is merely part of the interface. The reframing may then cover only first-personal acts such as apologising and promising."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "'Proxy assertion' is best understood as an attribution regime like the law of agency's apparent authority, which deliberately ignores the proxy's mental states, so the debate between proxy theorists and intention-based theorists is a clash between juridical and metaphysical senses of 'who said it'.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Arora's refinement of proxy assertion is driven by design implications: who should answer for what the system says. Agency law attributes an agent's statement to the principal when a third party reasonably took the agent to speak for the principal, whatever the agent's mind or the principal's actual instructions. Applied to LLMs, this gives the proxy result without any claim that the designer performed an illocutionary act in the Gricean sense. Opponents (Gubelmann, Attah) argue about intention and autonomy, which settle metaphysical authorship. Both camps can be right, because they answer different questions: who is bound by what was said, and who meant it. The reframing predicts that a scenario with no human principal at all (an autonomous system deployed by no one) leaves the metaphysical question untouched but removes any attribution, a case the proxy theory cannot handle. It also predicts that the proxy theorist's 'implications for design' are normative claims about liability that no theory of speech acts could deliver.",
    "target": "Arora's proxy refinement and Green and Michel's proxy speech acts, together with their critics who argue from intention.",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists intend a genuinely illocutionary reading and would reject the juridical gloss as a retreat. Apparent authority also requires reasonable third-party reliance, which may reintroduce the functionalist element Arora rejects."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Proxy assertion is unstable for machine-learned systems because it needs a distinction between on-script and off-script output, and a system with no script cannot supply one; the account therefore collapses into mere product liability, which is not assertion.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "A human proxy such as a spokesperson, interpreter or signatory can go rogue. Her principal is committed only to what falls within a mandate, so the principal asserts p only if p is mandated. Arora's refined view says designers and deployers assert via the machine. But for ML systems the designers have no content-level mandate, and any output, including contradictory pairs and outputs the deployer would disown, is equally the product of the same training. There are two options. If every output is the principal's assertion, the principal asserts contradictions and things she never saw, which wrecks the link between assertion and commitment to a content. If only outputs the principal would endorse count, the proxy relation holds only for the endorsed cases, and the rest is a blanket policy of accepting responsibility for whatever the device emits. That policy is the structure of strict liability for a product. The premise the argument turns on is that proxy assertion requires a content-sensitive mandate and cannot rest on a content-insensitive acceptance of risk. Arora's own design recommendations for ML devices are then a concession that the proxy structure is missing.",
    "target": "Arora's refined proxy-assertion account and the wider proxy family (Green and Michel; Butlin and Viebahn's proxy uses)",
    "risk": "Proponents can reply that humans also assert via standing policies, such as a company's published FAQ or an automated notice generated by rules. A defender may add that endorsement can be retrospective, as when a firm ratifies a bot's output by leaving it in place. Together these would show that blanket acceptance is a real form of assertion."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "A proxy account of chatbot speech cannot handle first-person self-referential utterances without positing a persona to which 'I' refers, and a persona-involving proxy account is fictionalism under another name.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "When a ghostwriter or press office speaks for a principal, 'I' or 'we' refers to the principal. Chatbots routinely say things like 'I'm a language model', 'I can't access that file', 'I'm sorry I got that wrong' and 'I'll remember that'. If proxy assertion is the designer's assertion, 'I' refers to the designer, and the utterances are false or absurd: the company is not a language model. If instead 'I' refers to the system, the system is the one the utterance is about and is plausibly the locus of the act, which abandons the proxy view. The remaining option is a persona, 'the assistant', that is neither company nor machine, and the company speaks through it. But a persona that is spoken through, and that users address and make-believedly engage with, is the fictional character in Mallory's account. The premise the argument turns on is that self-ascriptive content is central to chatbot speech and cannot be set aside as peripheral. The proxy–fictionalism contrast, which Williams and Bayne treat as a choice between two rivals, becomes a distinction between two descriptions of one persona structure.",
    "target": "The proxy-versus-fictionalism contrast, as treated by Arora and by Williams and Bayne",
    "risk": "A defender can adopt a theory of indexicals on which 'I' in proxy speech refers to the proxying device, as with an answering-machine message ('I am not here'), which would avoid any persona. The collapse would then fail, because referring to a device does not imply fiction."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Any principled way of choosing the principal of a proxy assertion, namely control over content, makes the user the principal in most chatbot cases and turns 'machine assertion' into tool-assisted self-assertion, so the proxy account either picks its principal arbitrarily or dissolves its own explanandum.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Arora assigns assertions to designers and deployers. Yet a proxy relation needs a non-arbitrary account of whose act it is, and the natural candidate is whose communicative objectives the output realises, or who has counterfactual control over its content. In ordinary use the user's prompt fixes topic, stance, register and often the claim itself ('write a paragraph arguing that X'). The developer's contribution is a standing disposition and the deployer's is a system prompt. On a control criterion, the user is the principal in most interactions, and the output is like predictive text or dictation: the user adopts or does not adopt the sentence. That is not a distinctive category of machine assertion at all, since it is the ordinary use of a writing aid. If instead the principal is whoever bears accountability, the criterion is sanctionability rather than authorship, and the proxy view becomes the Butlin–Viebahn sanction condition applied to a different entity. The premise the argument turns on is that 'on behalf of' needs a criterion of authorship that is the same across human and machine proxies.",
    "target": "Arora's attribution of machine utterances to designers and deployers; Green and Michel's creator-proxy thesis",
    "risk": "The proxy theorist may accept layered or role-relative principals, so that the user is the principal for prompted claims and the deployer for default claims. That is a respectable pluralism rather than a collapse. The argument also fails if assertion to a user can be proxied only by the party who has the relationship with that user."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The proxy and relocating accounts are not rivals to attributing views, because 'on behalf of' presupposes that the proxy itself performs an act of its own that commits the principal; the proxy account is therefore stable only for systems that are agents on Butlin's criterion, which are the cases where the machine acts as well.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "In human cases such as an ambassador, an interpreter, an attorney or a signatory with power of attorney, the principal is bound because the proxy performs an act that the institution recognises as binding. Proxy structure is two-level: the proxy utters, and her utterance counts as the principal's. This is why proxy acts can be defective in ways peculiar to proxies (ultra vires, misrepresenting the principal) and why the proxy can also be a sincere or insincere speaker. If Arora and Green and Michel keep this structure, machine proxies must perform an act, an utterance with the objective of conveying the principal's communicative aim, and that aim-directed sensitivity is what Butlin's instrumental-value criterion of agency measures. For systems with RLHF or reflective loops, the proxy view therefore does not relocate the act but presupposes machine agency. For systems that are not agents, the machine is a medium (a letter, a recording) and the proxy structure is idle, because the designer does not assert via a proxy but only via an instrument, as with a printed sign. Proxy accounts therefore have no stable region of their own: they fade into instrument views at one end and into intermediate or attributing views at the other. The premise the argument turns on is that the proxy relation is irreducibly two-agent.",
    "target": "The classification of proxy views as 'relocating' rather than attributing (the four-group map), and Arora's rejection of functionalism",
    "risk": "Defenders may say that proxy acts need only an act-type individuated by the principal's institution, so that a machine need not act as an agent and only has to be a conventionally recognised vehicle, as a corporate letterhead is. The two-level structure would then be a feature of human proxies and not of proxy assertion in general."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Proxy, fictionalist and proto-assertion accounts are not rivals about one speaker. Each is a correct answer for a different candidate speaker (deployed system, simulated character, base model), and 'does the chatbot assert?' is ill-posed until the speaker is individuated.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Williams and Bayne treat 'the chatbot' as a single, obvious candidate asserter and then test three theories against it. But at least three entities answer to that description: the weights (a next-token predictor), the persona or character the system is shaped to simulate, and the deployed product with its system prompt, fine-tuning and operator. Proxy accounts are natural for the product, since the operator speaks through it. Fictionalism is natural for the persona, since the user engages with a character. Proto-assertion, with its descriptive-function and learning-from-feedback profile, fits the weights as shaped by RLHF. Each of the three papers' objections to the others (e.g. that the proxy view fails because no human endorses each output, or that fictionalism fails because users take outputs seriously) works only by holding the speaker fixed at a different entity. The key premise is that the three candidate speakers have different individuation conditions and different relations to the output tokens. Once the speaker is indexed, the apparent dilemma dissolves into a set of compatible claims, and the real residue is the question of which entity users should hold responsible.",
    "target": "Williams and Bayne's dilemma (proxy vs fictionalism) and the shared assumption that 'the chatbot' is a determinate subject of the speech-act question.",
    "risk": "Proponents will say the speaker question has been answered implicitly, and that the disputed entity is the product. The thesis then reduces to a familiar point about role-play (Shanahan) with no new bite on speech acts."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The dispute over whether chatbots can be 'sanctioned' (Butlin and Viebahn) and over whether assertion 'emerges' (Williams and Bayne) is really a dispute about the timescale of the feedback loop. Training-time sanction is a legitimate form of sanction, so the sanctionability condition is met, and what the debate has missed is that it is met by a different relation than the one in human practice.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Butlin and Viebahn require that an asserter be capable of being sanctioned by interlocutors, and conclude that fine-tuned models fail this. Williams and Bayne's genealogical approach treats assertion as the output of a practice that shapes producers. Taken together, the question is where the shaping happens. In humans, sanction operates online (reproach, loss of trust) and offline (reputation). In RLHF models, sanction operates almost entirely offline, through aggregated human ratings that modify dispositions of later versions, and in-conversation sanction has only in-context effect. The relevant contrast is therefore between a sanction loop closed within a conversation and one closed across model generations, which is how the practice shapes a lineage or species. The key premise is that norm-governance of a kind is constituted by selection on the producers' dispositions, not by the producer's real-time responsiveness. If so, the 'can it be sanctioned' question is not a question about the chatbot's capacities. It is a question about whether the relevant unit of sanction is the individual conversation partner or the lineage, and the existing positions disagree because they fix this unit differently without saying so.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's sanctionability condition, and the shared assumption that sanction must be addressed to an individual speaker in the exchange.",
    "risk": "Lineage-level selection arguably cannot make an output an assertion by this particular system. It may also collapse into the proxy view, on which the sanctioned party is the developer."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The question 'does the model perform a speech act, or does it merely produce text humans interpret as one?' rests on a false dichotomy. On an Austinian uptake-constitutive view, the apparent act is performed jointly by the exchange, so the dispute is about where the act is located, not whether it occurs.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "All four groups, including Williams and Bayne's proto-assertion, assume the act is a product of the speaker's properties: intentions, norms, sanctionability, agency. The 'only interpreted' alternative is the deflationary foil. But if uptake is partly constitutive of illocutionary acts (Austin, Hornsby, Langton), then a user who takes up an output as advice, and who relies on and can challenge it within a stable practice, is thereby a co-constituent of an advising. This is not mere interpretation, because uptake can fail. A user can misfire by treating a hallucination as testimony, in the way one can misfire in trying to bet with someone who doesn't accept. The key premise is that illocutionary force is a two-place achievement and that at least some speech act types (advising, warning, perhaps assertion in its testimonial role) can be secured with an impoverished speaker contribution. This explains why proxy and fiction accounts both feel half-right: each captures one component of a jointly constituted act. It also relocates the real philosophical question to what entitles the hearer's uptake, which is an epistemological and ethical issue and not one in the philosophy of mind.",
    "target": "The speaker-centric framing shared by Gubelmann, Butlin and Viebahn, Skiba, Attah and Williams and Bayne, and the interpretation-versus-performance dichotomy in the area's guiding question.",
    "risk": "Uptake-constitutive views are controversial and may overgenerate (a thermostat 'warns' if I take it up as warning). The thesis may also just be Williams and Bayne's proto-assertion under another name."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The assertion debate is individuated at the wrong grain. Speech acts are token acts, but a chatbot's reply is a draw from a policy. The live question is whether assertion can attach to the policy (what the model reliably would say) rather than to the sampled token, and on that question the cases for denial and for proto-assertion come apart from where they are now placed.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Williams and Bayne ask whether particular outputs are assertions or proto-assertions, and the denial literature (Butlin and Viebahn on descriptive function, Gubelmann on malfunction versus deliberate error) likewise evaluates individual responses. But each response is one sample from a distribution, and the regenerate button shows the speaker 'would have said' otherwise at the same state. Assertion involves commitment to a content, and one sample commits to nothing, since the same prompt yields contradictory outputs. A model's dispositional profile (high-probability answers across resamples, calibrated confidence) is the closest analogue of a commitment. The key premise is that the commitment-bearing unit for an LLM is the policy or distribution and not the token. Two consequences follow. First, many chatbot outputs fail to be assertions not because the model lacks intentions or sanctionability but because the individuating unit is wrong, which is a different diagnosis from any in the literature. Second, outputs where the policy is sharply peaked (stable facts) can qualify as assertions with respect to the policy while low-entropy-failing outputs (confabulations) are mere samples. This shows that a genuine 'edge of assertion' runs through the same system and not between system types.",
    "target": "Williams and Bayne's token-level application of proto-assertion, and the shared assumption that the unit of speech-act evaluation is the individual output.",
    "risk": "Human speakers also vary across occasions, so the sample-versus-policy contrast may differ only in degree. The policy-level 'speaker' also has no obvious act of uttering, so attributing assertion to it may be a category mistake."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "'Proto-assertion' conflates a genealogical category (a precursor that belongs to a lineage leading toward assertion) with a structural category (an output that meets only some of assertion's conditions). Chatbot outputs fit the structural reading, which does no explanatory work, and fit the genealogical reading only if a further condition holds that current deployments fail.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Williams and Bayne motivate proto-assertion by looking at how assertion emerges. On the genealogical reading, proto-assertion is a stage in a process where a practice of informing acquires commitment and sanction through feedback among participants. On the structural reading, it is whatever partially satisfies assertion's profile (descriptive function, some responsiveness to evidence, uptake as testimony). Partial satisfaction is cheap: a weather display, a signpost and a thermometer all meet it, so the label would not mark chatbots out or settle the dilemma. The genealogical reading is substantive but has an extra requirement. For something to be a stage in a lineage, the practice must be one in which the added norms can stabilise, which needs the speaker-side entity to be a continuing participant whose conduct is shaped by the community's responses. The premise the argument turns on is that stage-hood is a relational fact about a practice's dynamics, not an intrinsic profile of the output. Whether chatbot outputs are proto-assertions then depends on whether the loop of user uptake, correction and developer updating is in fact driving the system toward answerability. That is an empirical and institutional question, not a classificatory one, and it may be answered differently for different deployments.",
    "target": "Williams and Bayne's proto-assertion as the resolution of the dilemma; also the general practice of treating 'intermediate' statuses (quasi-testimony, proto-assertion) as stable classifications.",
    "risk": "Williams and Bayne may already intend the genealogical reading and take the loop (RLHF, user feedback) to qualify. The thesis would then reduce to a disagreement about whether that loop is enough."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The dispute over whether chatbots assert is unsettled because the parties individuate the putative speaker at different levels (the trained model, the deployed product, the conversation-bound persona), and Butlin and Viebahn's sanction condition is met only at the persona level, which none of the standard options targets.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Denials, proxy views, fictionalism and proto-assertion all talk about 'the chatbot' as if the bearer of speech acts were obvious. But the same weights run many concurrent conversations, are updated across versions, and are steered by system prompts into distinct characters. Distinguish three candidate speakers: the weights, the product-and-operator, and a session-scoped persona whose commitments are held in the context window. Assertion requires that earlier commitments constrain later turns and that the speaker can be challenged, can retract, and can lose standing with the hearer. The weights cannot be sanctioned: nothing the user does later reaches them. The persona can: within the session it is held to what it said, it retracts, and it loses the user's trust. Proxy accounts relocate authorship to the operator, and fictionalism makes the persona a fiction. A third option is that the persona is a real but ephemeral discourse participant, constituted by the practice of keeping score, so that its commitments are real, with the model as its substrate. The premise is that commitment and sanction are scorekeeping facts in a conversation, not facts about the persistence of an organism or mechanism. The four positions then turn out to answer different questions about different bearers.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's sanction condition; the proxy and fictionalist relocations; the shared assumption that 'the chatbot' picks out a unique candidate speaker.",
    "risk": "Persona-level sanction may be only a shadow of real sanction, because the user cannot impose costs that persist or matter to anything. The view may collapse into fictionalism, with the persona as the fictional character."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Whether an intermediate status between assertion and non-assertion is available depends on whether the speech act's norms are regulative of a pre-existing behaviour (assertion, advising) or constitutive of the behaviour (promising, apologising). Proto-assertion therefore has no analogue for most of the speech acts that the literature treats alongside assertion.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Proto-assertion is credible because informing behaviour (signalling, reporting) can exist and be recognisable before assertoric norms are layered on, so one can pass by degrees from signal to assertion. Assertion's norms are regulative of an existing practice. Promising and apologising have no such substrate. Nothing counts as promising-without-obligation or as incipient apology with the moral work taken out. The act just is the undertaking of an obligation, or the taking of responsibility. For these acts the question has a bivalent shape: either the normative status is generated or it is not. A chatbot's 'I promise' is then either a promise (perhaps by proxy) or merely a sentence, and an emergence story cannot be told. This reclassifies the field. Intermediate views (quasi-testimony, proto-assertion) are about informational acts only, the Magnus–Buccella–D'Cruz verdict on apology is not in tension with a more permissive verdict on assertion, and Skiba's generalisation from assertion to all speech acts is unjustified in the other direction too, since the argument has to be run separately for each type. The premise is that gradability of performance tracks whether the act's norms attach to a behaviour that can exist independently of them.",
    "target": "The assumption, shared by Skiba and by Williams and Bayne's extension of their view, that a single verdict about chatbot assertion transfers to speech acts generally; the intermediate views' claim to settle the area.",
    "risk": "Apology and promising may also have emergent, graded precursors (expressions of regret, announcements of intent), so the constitutive/regulative line may not be clean."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "If any chatbot output is an assertion, the asserted content is not what a sampled token sequence says but what the model's output distribution commits to. The token-level framing used by every side is mistaken, and the 'no stable commitment' objection to chatbot assertion misfires.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Debate so far assumes the candidate speech act is an individual utterance. But one prompt can be resampled to yield p, then not-p, with no change in the system. A human who did this would be insincere or incoherent, so this is read as evidence that no speaker-level commitment exists. Distinguish token-assertion (the sampled string as an act) from policy-assertion (the stable dispositional profile across resamples, including its calibrated uncertainty). On the second view, a single output is an exemplar of the policy, and what is asserted is something like 'p, with this confidence' where the confidence is reflected in the spread of outputs. This changes the status of hallucination: it is a miscalibrated policy, not a string of lies. It changes what a sanction should target: retraining the policy, not penalising a draw. It also makes the descriptive-function condition of Butlin and Viebahn checkable at the policy level. The premise is that the unit of a speech act is fixed by the level at which the producing system is answerable to norms, as with a human speaker's settled opinions versus a slip of the tongue. This also explains why users are right to treat chatbot outputs as defeasible reports, and why proto-assertion looks like the right verdict on single outputs, since single outputs are not the primary bearers.",
    "target": "All parties who evaluate chatbot assertion by looking at individual outputs and their conversational consistency, notably Butlin and Viebahn and Williams and Bayne's treatment of the objection from commitment.",
    "risk": "Human assertion also has settled dispositions behind each utterance, so this may only restate the standard distinction between speaker's belief and utterance. And users encounter and rely on the sampled token, not the policy."
  }
]

