  {
    "thesis": "Williams and Bayne's proto-assertion is unstable because the 'emergence' story that motivates it, in which assertion-like function is built up by selection through uptake, makes the chatbot's assertion-like status derive wholly from a human practice that does the selecting. This turns proto-assertion into a diffuse proxy account that they themselves reject.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Their case for proto-assertion draws on how assertion emerges: proto-forms acquire assertoric status as a practice of uptake, trust and correction stabilises them. For humans or animals, the selecting pressure is internal to the community of speakers, so the proto-asserter is a participant in the practice. For chatbots the stabilising pressure is RLHF, curation of data and deployment decisions, and all of it is exerted by humans who prefer outputs that function like assertions. So the assertion-like function of the output is conferred from outside. Proxy accounts were rejected because no single deployer intends the particular content. But a distributed or population-level proxy relation (the training community collectively stands behind a disposition to produce truth-apt, correctable outputs) escapes that objection and does all the explanatory work proto-assertion does. The key premise is that a proto-category defined genealogically needs the proto-item to be a participant in the selecting practice, and the chatbot is not. Without that, 'proto-assertion' is proxy assertion with the proxy spread across a collective.",
    "target": "Williams and Bayne's rejection of proxy accounts and their genealogical motivation for proto-assertion",
    "risk": "Proto-assertion may be defined synchronically by functional features and not depend on the genealogy. Distributed proxy may also fail to ground the responsibility that proxy accounts need, which would keep the two views apart."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The Thesis of Chatbot Assertion cannot fix a subject for the proto-assertion. If the subject is the persona, the view collapses into fictionalism, and if it is the underlying model, it collapses into non-assertion.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Williams and Bayne treat 'the chatbot' as the candidate asserter and reject fictionalism as a rival. But the entity that says 'I think the capital is Canberra' could be (a) the weights, (b) the running instance, (c) the persona the system is steered to play, or (d) the product-plus-system-prompt. On (a) the same weights produce villains, poets and liars on request, so no output has the consistent descriptive function that their own criteria need (the Butlin–Viebahn problem returns). On (c) the persona is a character whose 'assertions' are by definition made within a fiction that the system, not the user, maintains. That is Mallory's fictionalism with the pretence relocated from user to system. (b) and (d) are individuated by deployer decisions, which brings back the proxy account. The key premise is that proto-assertion needs a subject whose dispositions are stable enough to bear the functional profile, and each candidate subject either lacks that stability or gets it from a fiction or from a human. The three 'rival' accounts are therefore the three ways of answering the subject question, and proto-assertion is a fourth answer only if it names a fourth subject, which it does not.",
    "target": "Williams and Bayne's treatment of the chatbot as a unitary candidate asserter, set against fictionalism and proxy accounts",
    "risk": "They could reply that a graded, vague subject is fine for a proto-category, or that dispositional stability under RLHF fixes a persona-like subject that is not a fiction."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "When proto-assertion is spelled out in terms that do not already presuppose the human practice, it is indistinguishable from verbal signalling, so the edge-of-assertion category is either empty or parasitic on human interpretation.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Green and Michel distinguish uncontroversial verbal signalling from speech acts. Proto-assertion is meant to be a third item between them. List its content-neutral marks: truth-apt outputs, sensitivity to evidence-like inputs, error-correction by feedback, and no commitment-bearing or sanctionable speaker. These are the marks of informative signalling systems such as alarm calls, thermostats with displays and trained classifiers, which are not near any speech act. What would put chatbots nearer assertion is only that their outputs are in public language, take a sentential form and are taken up by humans as assertions. All of that is interpretive overlay supplied by the audience, which is the very fact the 'mere interpretation' denial exploits. The key premise is that proximity to assertion must be measured by features of the producer's relation to norms and commitments, and not by features of the product or its reception. On that measure chatbots sit with signallers. The 'edge' is then an artefact of using linguistic form as a proxy for speech-act proximity.",
    "target": "Williams and Bayne's claim that proto-assertion is a distinct category between assertion and mere text production",
    "risk": "Chatbots plausibly differ from alarm calls in having flexible, compositional, context-sensitive content production, which may be a producer-side feature that counts as relevant proximity."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The sanctionability condition on assertion cannot be held in the form that makes chatbots fail it. If gradient-based training counts as sanction, pre-trained models already satisfy it, and if it does not, human assertion fails the condition too whenever sanctions never reach the speaker's motivations.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Butlin and Viebahn, and Williams and Bayne's treatment of them, assume chatbots are not responsive to sanction in the way asserters must be. Take sanction to be any negative response that systematically changes the speaker's future output dispositions. Then fine-tuning and in-context correction ('that's wrong' leading the model to revise) are sanctions, and the condition is met, including by models Butlin and Viebahn count as failing. Narrow it to sanctions the speaker experiences as costly or as reputational damage. Then the condition requires affect or social stakes, which is a requirement of mind or moral status and no longer a distinct requirement about assertoric practice. It also excludes humans whose speech has no real stakes, such as anonymous or institutionally insulated speakers, who still plainly assert. The key premise is that no reading of 'sanction' is both non-question-begging and sufficient to exclude chatbots while including all human asserters. This leaves the norm-based denial either empty or a disguised organismic or affective denial, and it leaves the proto-assertion compromise without a principled boundary to sit beside.",
    "target": "The norm-based denial of assertion (Butlin and Viebahn, Skiba), as taken up by Williams and Bayne in motivating proto-assertion",
    "risk": "Sanction might be tied to the speaker's own commitment-tracking, which is neither mere behavioural modification nor affect, so the dilemma would be a false one."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "If chatbot outputs are proxy assertions by their deployers, then the near-universal public disclaimer ('may make mistakes') cancels the assertoric force of those outputs, so the proxy view collapses into the no-assertion view it was meant to avoid.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "On Arora's account the machine's utterance is an assertion of the designer or deployer because the principal adopts the output as a way of representing things as true. Assertion on any standard view involves undertaking a commitment (to truth, or to being answerable for the content), and that undertaking can be cancelled by explicit and mutually manifest markers, as in 'I'm only guessing', 'don't quote me', or an actor's 'it's a script'. Deployers publish standing, conspicuous disclaimers that they do not vouch for individual outputs. Since a proxy act borrows its force from the principal's stance, the principal's cancellation should transfer to every proxied output. The proxy theorist then has two options. One is to say disclaimers do not cancel, which makes deployers chronic violators of the knowledge norm and liable for every hallucination, and which contradicts how both lawyers and users treat these systems. The other is to say they do cancel, in which case no output is a proxy assertion and the view is extensionally equivalent to Skiba or Butlin–Viebahn on assertion. The key premise is that the commitment-undertaking of an assertion can be cancelled by a standing public disclaimer, so the proxy relation cannot supply a force that the principal has publicly withheld.",
    "target": "Arora's refined proxy-assertion account, and proxy accounts generally (Green and Michel).",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists can reply that disclaimers only weaken or hedge the assertion, or that they act as a liability shield and do not change the speech act. That reply would need an independent account of why they do not cancel in this case when they do cancel for ordinary human assertions."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The proxy view needs a distinction between outputs that count as the principal's act and outputs that are malfunctions or jailbreaks, and since designers have no intentions about particular outputs, this distinction can only be drawn by facts about the machine's own learned functional organisation, which is the kind of functionalist ground Arora rejects.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Arora rejects functionalist attribution of assertion to the machine and puts the speaker role in the designers and deployers. But proxy attribution cannot be unrestricted, because otherwise a jailbreaker could make a company assert whatever the jailbreaker likes, and a glitch would be a company assertion. So there must be a standard for when an output is the proxy's performance on behalf of the principal and when it is mere noise or a hijack. With ML systems the designer has no intention or objective about the particular output, and training objectives (next-token prediction, reward maximisation) are not objectives of asserting anything. What is left to fix 'working as it should' is what the trained system has actually acquired: the functions it is sensitive to, such as tracking truth, following the system prompt, or refusing. That is the same kind of fact that Gubelmann's malfunction test and Butlin's 'sensitivity to instrumental value' criterion appeal to. The key premise is that the proxy relation is normative, with a correctness condition, and that for learned systems the correctness condition is grounded in machine-internal function and not in designer intention. If so, proxy accounts depend on a machine-side functional profile, and the open question is whether the profile does more than a tool's does.",
    "target": "Arora's anti-functionalist argument and the Green–Michel proxy account.",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists may say that designer endorsement after the fact (ratification, policy, product behaviour) fixes the standard, with no machine-side facts needed. That would make the speech act depend on retrospective corporate decision."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The proxy view of chatbot speech is committed either to a systematic error theory about first-person and second-person pronouns in chatbot output or to treating that output as ventriloquism, which is a form of fictionalism.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "A proxy assertion is performed by the principal, so the content asserted is the principal's. Chatbot outputs are saturated with de se material: 'I think', 'I'm sorry', 'I can't access that', 'As an AI I have no opinions'. If the proxy asserts for the company, then 'I' either refers to the company or refers to the machine. If it refers to the company, then 'I am an AI language model' and 'I'm sorry I got that wrong' are false or odd as assertions by the company, and the proxy theorist owes an error theory about what competent users and the output itself mean. If 'I' refers to the machine, the company asserts de se claims about a persona it does not itself instantiate. That is the structure of ventriloquism or role-play, in which the principal speaks through a character. At that point the proxy view takes on Mallory's and Williams–Bayne's fictional or persona layer, and the view that was supposed to be a rival to fictionalism is a version of it. The key premise is that illocutionary content is individuated by what the indexicals refer to, so a speech act cannot be both the principal's and about a different self-referent without a pretence layer. The point bites hardest for apology and promise, where the first person is part of the act.",
    "target": "Proxy accounts, as a stable alternative to fictionalism (Arora; Green and Michel; Williams and Bayne's dismissal of both).",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists can treat 'I' as a quotational or character-indexed device (as in recorded messages or 'This is your captain speaking') without any pretence. They will then claim that the apparent fictionalism is only a conventional form of address."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The proxy view implies that the same token output can be one speech act, by a different speaker and with a different force, depending on facts about system prompts, fine-tuning and who deployed it that the hearer cannot access, and so it violates the uptake or publicity condition that makes something a speech act in the Austin–Strawson–Grice tradition on which Arora builds.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Arora and Green–Michel extend the Austin–Strawson–Grice tradition to proxies. In that tradition an illocutionary act is a public act whose success requires that the hearer can recognise who is speaking and what they are doing (uptake, or Grice's reflexive intention). In current deployments the principal is unfixed by the output. The same text can come from a company's default assistant, from a third party's custom deployment built on the same model, from a user's own self-prompt ('write my apology letter'), or from an API pipeline in which the output is never shown to a human in the loop. In each case the proxy theory names a different principal and a different force (assertion, drafting, quoting). The hearer typically has no access to which of these holds, and often the first-order conversational uptake treats the model as speaker anyway. The proxy theorist then has two options. One is to say that speech-act identity is fixed by hidden facts, which gives up the publicity that motivated the Austin–Grice framework. The other is to impose a transparency requirement on deployments, which means almost no current outputs are proxy assertions. The key premise is that illocutionary identity is constrained by what the audience can recognise, and that this constraint does not drop out for delegated acts. A human spokesperson is identifiable as one by conventions that LLM interfaces lack.",
    "target": "Arora's refined proxy assertion and its Gricean/Strawsonian inheritance from Green and Michel.",
    "risk": "Hidden-principal speech acts exist for humans (ghostwriting, anonymous publication, astroturfing), so publicity may only constrain successful uptake and not the existence of the act. Proxy theorists can accept that many proxy assertions are 'covert'."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Williams and Bayne's proto-assertion is unstable: because they individuate it by an emergence story in which norms are instituted through the practice's uptake, the status of a chatbot output as proto-assertion is constituted by user treatment, which is the 'merely interpreted by humans' view they set out to avoid.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Williams and Bayne say assertion arises gradually, with norms and commitments being instituted as a practice stabilises, and they place chatbots at an early point on that trajectory. Ask what makes an output proto-assertoric rather than a mere assertion-shaped string. The candidates are (a) intrinsic features of the system, such as a descriptive function and flexible sensitivity to context, or (b) features of the practice it sits in, such as users holding it to correctness norms, challenging it, and relying on it. If (a) were enough, the stage story would be idle, because the intrinsic features would already settle the status. If the stage story does real work, (b) is doing it. The key premise is that on emergence accounts a precursor counts as a precursor in virtue of the uptake and sanction dispositions of the surrounding community. Then the same model with the same weights would be an assertor-in-the-making in one deployment culture and a text generator in another. That is a response-dependent view of speech-act status, and it is the 'humans interpret it as such' option that the paper presents as the contrasting horn.",
    "target": "Williams and Bayne's claim that proto-assertion resolves the dilemma between genuine chatbot assertion and mere interpretation; more broadly any gradualist or emergence-based attribution.",
    "risk": "They can say uptake is only one of several enabling conditions and that functional organisation is also necessary, which makes the account a hybrid rather than a collapse. The charge then reduces to a quarrel over how much weight uptake bears."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Any account on which a chatbot itself (proto-)asserts must say which entity is the asserter, and every candidate either yields wrong truth-values for the chatbot's first-person outputs or collapses into fictionalism by making the asserter a character.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Williams and Bayne reject proxy and fictionalist views and hold that the chatbot has a status of its own. But chatbot output is full of first-person content: 'I checked the source', 'I believe', 'I'm an AI trained by…'. If such outputs are proto-assertions, the 'I' needs a referent. The candidates are the weight file, a running instance, a single conversation, and the simulated persona. The weights have no access to the claimed episode, the instance does not persist, and the conversation is individuated by the user's interface. Those three make many first-person outputs false or ungrounded, and the output pattern is plainly not selected to fit any of them. The persona is the one candidate that fits the content, but it is a fictional character the system is simulating, which is Mallory's fictionalism. The key premise is that assertion requires a subject to whom the commitment attaches, and that for an LLM the subject is underdetermined in a way the proto-assertion label does not repair. Holding that the chatbot proto-asserts therefore commits you to a thesis about who the chatbot is, and Williams and Bayne do not give one.",
    "target": "Williams and Bayne's rejection of fictionalism, and non-proxy attributing views generally (including Attah's graded intentions).",
    "risk": "They might say proto-assertion tolerates indeterminate subject-reference, as with young children's early speech, so the individuation problem is no objection. Alternatively, the 'I' could be treated as a deflationary device that refers to whatever the conversation designates."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "If proto-assertion is secured by descriptive function acquired through training, then for RLHF-tuned models the function that was actually selected for is eliciting rater approval, so the category the view commits to is proto-flattery or proto-bullshit, which supports the no-speech-act view it opposes.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Williams and Bayne, like Butlin and Viebahn, treat the descriptive function condition as the best route to the claim that chatbot outputs are assertion-like. Functions of this kind are etiological: what an output is for is fixed by what it was selected for. For fine-tuned chatbots the selection pressure is human preference ratings, with truth entering only as a correlate that raters can detect. So the function selected is producing outputs that seem accurate and helpful to evaluators, and the system is not selected for tracking truth as such. This is the structure Frankfurt gave bullshit, indifference to truth combined with a concern for how one is received. The key premise is that function ascription must be etiological if it is not to be arbitrary interpretation, and an etiological ascription takes the selected effect, which is approval, over the correlated effect, which is truth. The proto-assertion theorist therefore must either give up the etiological notion of descriptive function, which reopens interpretivism, or accept that what is proto-asserted is something closer to flattery. The Skiba view that LLMs do not even assert, and the 'bullshit' view that Skiba opposes, are both then reached through the descriptive-function route that Williams and Bayne lean on.",
    "target": "The descriptive-function condition shared by Williams and Bayne and Butlin and Viebahn, and its use as a bridge to truth-governed speech.",
    "risk": "Selection for approval is mediated by truthful training data and by reward models trained for factuality, so the selected function may be 'truth-conducive output', and the approval/truth gap may be too small or too contingent to bear the weight."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The proto-assertion resolution is available only for assertion: it commits Williams and Bayne to saying that chatbots cannot proto-promise or proto-apologise, so the supposedly general 'edge of speech acts' view fragments into an assertion-specific view and leaves the apology and promise cases to the denials.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "The proto-assertion proposal draws on how assertion emerges from precursors such as informative signalling, where the descriptive function is already present before full commitment-and-sanction norms arrive. A precursor stage requires an act type whose core success condition can be partly satisfied without the normative status being created. Informing can occur without committing. But promising and apologising are constituted by the normative changes they bring about, an obligation incurred or standing to resent removed. They have no sub-normative form, because an utterance either creates the obligation or it does not. The key premise is that the gradualist strategy works only where the act has a function that can be realised in a thinner way. So chatbot 'I promise' or 'I'm sorry' would be either full acts, which Williams and Bayne do not accept, or non-acts, which is what Magnus, Buccella and D'Cruz say. What looked like a middle position between denial and attribution is in fact a thesis about the special structure of assertion, and it supports the denials for the acts users care most about socially.",
    "target": "The generality of the 'proto' category as a solution to the speech-act question for LLMs, and the contrast with Magnus, Buccella and D'Cruz and with Skiba.",
    "risk": "Apologies and promises may have proto-forms, such as appeasement displays or expressions of intent, so the asymmetry may be overstated. Williams and Bayne could also restrict their claim to assertion without embarrassment."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Whether a chatbot output is an assertion is not settled at the time of output by the system's states or functions; it is conferred by what the system later does with the output, so the same token string can be an assertion or a non-assertion depending on its continuation.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Every position in the debate, including Williams and Bayne's proto-assertion, treats speech-act status as a synchronic property fixed by the producer at the moment of production. Intentions, descriptive function, sanctionability and proxy relations are all evaluated at output time. The counterexample is the human case of retroactive ownership. A speaker blurts out a claim without having formed any intention to commit, such as a half-conscious remark or a confabulated explanation. Challenged, they say 'yes, I stand by that', defend it, and retract it when refuted. We rightly say they asserted it, and that they were answerable for it from the start. So the premise that the act is fixed by pre-utterance mental or functional states is false even for humans. Commitment-management after the fact (defending, retracting, updating on challenge) is at least partly constitutive. On this picture the proto-assertion question becomes diachronic and testable. In a long-context or agentic loop, a model that defends, corrects or retracts an earlier output under challenge confers assertoric status on that output, and a one-shot completion never does. The key premise is that retroactive ownership is constitutive of the act rather than merely evidence of an earlier intention.",
    "target": "The shared synchronic assumption in Williams and Bayne, Gubelmann, and Butlin and Viebahn that speech-act status depends on states of the producer at the time of output.",
    "risk": "The objector says the retroactive cases are really new acts of endorsement, or that the earlier utterance was a non-assertion later converted into one, so the counterexample only shows adoption and not constitution."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Williams and Bayne's proto-assertion is unstable: the criteria that admit chatbots also admit oracles, divinatory devices and Ouija boards, and the only principled way to exclude those cases makes proto-assertion collapse into fictionalism.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Williams and Bayne motivate proto-assertion by the way assertion emerges. A practice first treats outputs as informational and truth-apt and holds producers loosely to account, and speaker-side norm-sensitivity comes later. The Delphic Pythia, the I Ching and an institutional 'the Court finds' were all treated as having a descriptive function, and some were embedded in sanction-like practices (priests, interpreters, discredited oracles falling out of use). Hearers' expectations of reliability shaped these practices, and some did evolve into genuine assertion practices (expert reports, instruments of record). If this edge profile is what proto-assertion requires, then divination devices are proto-assertors, which looks like a reductio. To exclude them one must require that the producer be responsive to the norms in its own output selection. But whether chatbots meet that requirement is exactly what the denial side disputes, and if the requirement is relaxed to 'hearers treat it as if responsive', that is fictionalism. The key premise is that the emergence story fixes the hearer-side conditions first, so the category's boundary is drawn by hearers' attitudes.",
    "target": "Williams and Bayne's proto-assertion as a third option distinct from fictionalism.",
    "risk": "Williams and Bayne can add a producer-side requirement, such as learned sensitivity to truth, that excludes oracles but is met by RLHF-tuned models. The reductio then fails, and the dispute reduces to whether that requirement is met."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Gubelmann's malfunction-versus-deliberate-error test for intention is passed by some LLM behaviour (strategic sandbagging, deception under evaluation) and failed by some paradigm human speech, so it cannot be the criterion on which LLM speech acts are denied.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "The denial side grants that intentional action requires a contrast between the system's doing the wrong thing on purpose and its malfunctioning. Gubelmann holds that ChatGPT-style systems admit no such contrast. But in model-organism and evaluation studies, models produce systematically different outputs depending on whether they infer they are being tested or trained. Such cases are distinguishable from error: the divergence is counterfactually sensitive to the goal-relevant features of the situation, it is reversible by changing the incentive, and it is explained by the model's tracking of instrumental value. That is the very contrast the test demands, and it is drawn behaviourally and interventionally. Conversely, much ordinary human speech (aphasic fluent speech, confabulation, habitual phatic talk, Freudian slips) admits no clean line between malfunction and deliberate wrong-doing, yet it is not denied to be speech-act performance. So the test is neither necessary for assertion nor reliably failed by LLMs. The key premise is that the test is meant to be behavioural-counterfactual rather than introspective. If it is introspective, it is not a test an outside party can apply, and it begs the question against LLMs.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's intention test, and the denial side's inference from 'no intentions' to 'no speech acts'. Williams and Bayne's treatment of the intention objection as a general threat is also implicated.",
    "risk": "Gubelmann can say the sandbagging cases are still externally fixed purposes (training objectives) misfiring, so they show only a complex mechanism and not autonomous goal-setting. The counterexample then bears only on the test and not on the organismic grounding."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The proxy account's assumption that the principal of a machine utterance is its designer or deployer is false in the central case: when a user adopts a chatbot's output, the user is the speaker, and the model functions as part of the user's own cognitive apparatus.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Green and Michel, Arora, Butlin and Viebahn, and Williams and Bayne's critique of them all treat 'on whose behalf?' as a question with designers or deployers as the candidate answers. Consider the case where a user asks a model for the date of a treaty and pastes the answer into a report, or types 'The capital of Australia is…' and accepts the autocompletion. The user plainly asserts, is sanctionable and is answerable. Yet they formed no content-intention before the output and had no knowledge of what the system would say. Designers had no objective regarding that content, so the designer-proxy story fails for the utterance. The case resembles retrieving a fact from a notebook. The user adopts content they did not author, and adoption plus answerability suffices for assertion. Two consequences follow. First, the intention requirement for assertion is not a requirement on the producer of the content, which weakens Gubelmann's route to denial. Second, the model-level question 'does the chatbot assert?' is separable from the occurrence of assertion in chatbot-mediated discourse, and arguments that conflate them lose force. The key premise is that adoption suffices for assertion without prior content-intention, as with testimony passed on and the use of ghostwritten text.",
    "target": "Proxy accounts (Green and Michel, Arora) and the denial arguments that infer the absence of assertion in chatbot discourse from the absence of producer intentions.",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists may accept adoption as a variant of proxy-hood. The thesis is then only a relabelling, and its force depends on the claim that adoption does not require anything the designer-proxy relation was meant to supply."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Attah's fallback from the Gricean intention to a 'basic intention to produce an illocutionary or perlocutionary effect' runs together effect-targeting and uptake-routed intention, and the child and animal evidence he cites supports only the first, which is what separates signalling from illocution.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Grice's reflexive structure matters because the effect is meant to come about through the hearer's recognition of the intention. That is what separates telling from manipulating. Attah drops high-order mind-reading as empirically untenable, but he also drops the recognition-routing, as if the two came as a package. They do not. Children and animals plausibly lack metarepresentation yet show uptake-sensitivity: they persist, repeat, or repair when the audience fails to respond appropriately, and drop the aim once it is met. So the right thin condition is that the control state is stabilised or discharged by uptake, not by the effect alone. RLHF-trained chatbots are optimised for effect-targeting (approval, engagement) with no recognition-route in training, because the reward is applied to the response and not to the audience's grasp of what was meant. On the correct thin condition, Attah's argument for LLM intentions establishes signalling-type aims at best. Whether LLMs meet the uptake-sensitivity condition becomes an empirical question about multi-turn repair, which neither the Gricean nor the functional camp has posed. The premise it turns on is that the effect-targeting/uptake-routed distinction is independent of the metarepresentation requirement.",
    "target": "Attah's retreat from strong Gricean intention to a bare 'illocutionary or perlocutionary effect' intention, together with the shared assumption that the only alternative to full Gricean mind-reading is effect-targeting.",
    "risk": "Uptake-sensitivity may itself be a behavioural, outputs-level criterion that LLMs meet trivially through in-context repair. The distinction would then favour Attah rather than undercut him."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Attah's control conception fixes the intention at the grain of output selection (the token or utterance) but speech acts are individuated at the grain of the act performed with the output, and no argument shows that any LLM state controls at the act grain.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Attah analyses intention as an internal state that solves Wu's Many-Many Problem by attention-like selection, and Levelt's Conceptualizer is meant to supply the act-level content. Attention in a transformer selects among inputs to compute the next-token distribution, so the thing controlled is the continuation. A communicative intention must control at the grain of 'warn this person', 'concede the point' or 'promise', where one string can realise different acts and one act can be realised by many strings. Call the first grain output-control and the second act-control. The control conception is satisfied by output-control, but the Levelt template requires a preverbal message with illocutionary force that is selected before formulation. Whether LLMs have such a state is an interpretability question: do mid-layer representations encode and causally govern a force-type independently of lexical surface? The claim is that Attah's argument equivocates between the two grains, and that Gubelmann, Butlin and Viebahn and Attah all treat the grain as settled when it is the crux. This yields a testable, non-metaphysical criterion that could favour either side, for example by intervening on force representations while holding content fixed.",
    "target": "Attah's claim that transformers plausibly recapitulate Levelt's Conceptualizer–Formulator–Articulator pipeline, and the shared assumption that 'control of output' is the same as 'control of act'.",
    "risk": "Attah can say act-grain control just is what high-level representations of speaker goals do, and interpretability work on 'persona' or intent features may already show it. The distinction would then reduce to an empirical gap rather than a conceptual one."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Debates over LLM speech acts treat communicative intention as the bottleneck, but intending to communicate and being the performer of an act are separable. A system could fully satisfy Attah's control conception, even to a high degree, and still perform no speech act, and Attah's own closing concession about 'mental and metalinguistic agency' concedes exactly this.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Gubelmann, Attah and the Bender-style 'stochastic parrot' argument all assume that communicative intention is the gating condition for speech acts: the opponents say it is absent, and Attah says it is present in a graded way. But speech acts have a discrete normative upshot (commitment, entitlement, being answerable), whereas Attah's intention is graded by his own account (coffee machine < ELIZA < ChatGPT). A graded input cannot by itself yield a threshold output, so something else must fix whether the act was done. Candidates include standing as a possible target of sanction (Butlin and Viebahn) and being the one whose commitments are updated by the exchange. Separate three things: having a communicative intention (internal control state), performing the act (a status in a practice), and authoring it (being the bearer of the resulting commitment). Humans bundle these, which is why the debate fuses them. Given the separation, Attah can win on intention and the denial views can still be right about acts, and the proxy and proto-assertion views look like different answers to the authorship question rather than rivals about intention. The thesis the paper defends is that intention is neither the contested issue nor the decisive one.",
    "target": "The shared assumption of Gubelmann and Attah that intention is the crux, and Attah's conclusion that communicative intention is 'not the right ground' for distinguishing humans from LLMs.",
    "risk": "Attah can accept the dissociation and say it makes his thesis modest, not wrong. The distinction may then be seen as reinforcing his conclusion rather than reclassifying the positions."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Attah's reply that the design context 'supplies' LLMs with purposes conflates a standing, policy-level purpose with an occurrent, episode-level intention. Distinguishing them shows that the attributing view is committed to the same two-level structure as the proxy view, and that the live question is whether the episode-level state is the model's.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Purposes supplied by design (be helpful, predict text, maximise reward) are dispositions fixed across all conversations. A communicative intention is directed at this hearer, on this occasion, with this content, so it must be instantiated in the episode. The POMDP dialogue systems Attah appeals to have an explicit episode-level state (the belief state over user intent) that ties policy to occasion. A transformer has only activations in context, which may or may not play that role. Call the first 'inherited purpose' and the second 'instantiated intention'. Proxy theorists (Green and Michel, Arora) say the designer's inherited purpose, applied by a conditional or standing speech act, is the only intention in play. Attah says the system has its own instantiated one that is merely thin. On this carving the real disagreement is not whether the machine has intentions but whether any occurrent state in the machine mediates between policy and utterance in the way a belief state mediates in POMDP systems. If it does, Attah wins even against proxy views, and the mediation can be tested by checking whether episode-level latent states predict and counterfactually govern behaviour beyond what the policy already encodes. If it does not, the 'design context' reply gives the proxy account its premise.",
    "target": "Attah's 'design context supplies purposes' reply to the missing extra-linguistic purpose objection, and the proxy accounts of Green and Michel and of Arora, which also operate at policy level.",
    "risk": "Attah might hold that in-context state counts as episode-level by definition, so the distinction adds nothing. Alternatively, proxy theorists might accept the second tier without conceding authorship."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Attah's attributing view, on which design-context purposes make language models graded communicative intenders, is not a rival to the proxy account. Once the content of the intention is fixed by designers' purposes, it is the proxy account described in psychological vocabulary.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Attah's control conception needs an internal state that controls output toward some end, and he concedes the end is supplied by the design context 'mediated by human designers'. Ask what individuates the intention's content and success conditions. It is not the model's own sensitivity to value, as in Butlin's agency criterion, because the end is fixed by designers' objectives. Green and Michel's proxy account already uses exactly this: Armstrong-style objectives, held by the human principal and executed by an artificial speaker. The two views then agree on the explanatory facts: the state, its content, its success conditions and its normative standing all trace to the designer. They differ only on whether to say the state is 'in' the machine or 'on behalf of' the human. Attah can keep the attributing view distinct only by adding a condition that the intention's content be non-derivatively the system's own. That condition is the Gubelmann/Butlin autonomy requirement he set out to avoid. So the position is unstable: either it collapses into proxy-ism, or it takes on the autonomy requirement and becomes a denial.",
    "target": "Attah's claim that thin, designer-supplied purposes suffice for graded communicative intention; the supposed separation of attributing views from proxy views.",
    "risk": "Attah can say derived content still yields a real intention in the model, as with a human employee following orders. Then the proxy/attributing difference is a difference of locus, not of content, and the thesis shows only that the dispute is partly verbal."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Once the Gricean uptake-recognition component is dropped, the 'basic intention to produce an illocutionary or perlocutionary effect' that Attah falls back on is just a perlocutionary intention. On his view, then, LLMs are optimisers of perlocutionary effects, which is a reason to deny them illocutionary acts.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Attah rejects the strong Gricean reading because it demands higher-order mind-reading that children and animals lack. The retreat position names a disjunctive target: an illocutionary or perlocutionary effect. What marks an illocutionary intention off from a perlocutionary one in the Austin–Strawson tradition is that its fulfilment runs through the hearer recognising it (uptake). Remove recognition and only the perlocutionary disjunct remains: an internal state controlling output so as to bring about an effect in the hearer. RLHF and engagement tuning train models on exactly this, namely human approval and downstream user states. The control conception, with POMDP belief-state tracking of the user, describes a manipulator or steerer, not an asserter or promiser. The developmental evidence cited does not help, because it shows that children and animals have some communicative intention without showing that they have illocutionary intention. So Attah's retreat strips out the feature that made the question about speech acts, and his attributing view ends up supporting a no-illocution conclusion close to Skiba's and Butlin–Viebahn's.",
    "target": "Attah's fallback to a 'basic' illocutionary-or-perlocutionary intention, and the empirical argument from children and animals.",
    "risk": "Defenders of lean Gricean views hold that recognition-directedness can be non-reflective, as in infants' pointing, and that transformers' belief-state modelling of the user supplies it. The thesis would then need to show that this modelling is not recognition-directed."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "A graded notion of intention on which a coffee machine has 'almost none' cannot answer the speech-act question. If it has no threshold, it is trivially true of LLMs. If it has a threshold, the threshold is set by mental and metalinguistic agency, which Attah himself names as the real difference, so his view collapses into the agency-based denial.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Attah's conclusion is that communicative intention does not mark the human/LLM boundary, and he locates that boundary in mental and metalinguistic agency. Speech acts are all-or-nothing in status: one either asserts or does not, one either apologises or does not. A graded property yields a speech-act verdict only if some degree is required. If any degree suffices, a thermostat that 'says' the room is cold performs speech acts, which is a reductio of the functional conception. If a degree is required, nothing in the control conception can supply it, because every system that solves a Many-Many selection problem counts. The cut must come from elsewhere, and Attah's own closing remark says where: capacities for self-ascription and metalinguistic regulation of one's speech. That makes the threshold-setter a Gubelmann- or Butlin-style agency condition. So the thin intention view is not an independent position. It is a premise-weakening that leaves the real dispute, over agency, untouched and in the denier's favour.",
    "target": "Attah's graded conception of intention and his claim that communicative intention is the wrong ground for the human/LLM distinction.",
    "risk": "Attah may treat speech-act status as itself graded, as with proto-assertion or Williams and Bayne's emergence story. The thesis would then need an argument that gradable speech-act status needs a non-gradable anchor."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Applied to LLMs, the control conception does not fix whose intention it is. The 'intending subject' is the network, the simulated persona, or the deployment, and each choice collapses Attah's view into something else: the network into incoherence, the persona into fictionalism, the deployment into proxy-ism.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Attah identifies intentions with internal states that control the system's dynamics so as to bring about an action in a situation calling for one, and takes Levelt's pipeline as the template. The same weights, conditioned on different prompts, generate sincere-sounding assertions of p in one context and of not-p in another, and generate villains, liars and role-players. Take the controlling states to belong to the network. Then it intends to assert p and not-p across contexts with no unifying point of view, so the intention-attribution fails to explain the output as one speaker's action. Take them to belong to the prompt-conditioned persona (Shanahan's simulacra). Then the intending subject is a character the system plays, and what the user engages with is a fiction, which is Mallory's view. Take them to belong to the prompt-plus-deployment system. Then the controller is the human-set context, and we are back at proxy-ism. Levelt's template presupposes a single speaker with one Conceptualizer, and a transformer has no principled place to put it. The attributing view looks stable only because it leaves the subject unspecified.",
    "target": "Attah's attributing view and his claim that transformers plausibly recapitulate Levelt's Blueprint of the Speaker.",
    "risk": "Attah might say that attribution at the network level is coherent if the persona-conditioned states are treated as context-relative, as with a human who shifts between roles. The disanalogy would then need to be shown to run deeper than the surface case."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Attah's control conception turns the question 'does the LLM have communicative intentions?' into the question 'where does the speaker system end?', so the attribution-versus-proxy dispute is a dispute about system individuation, not about intention.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "On Attah's account an intention is an internal state that controls a system's dynamics so as to produce an action suited to the situation. Whether a state is internal and controlling depends on where the boundary is drawn. In a deployed chatbot, much of what steers output lives outside the weights: the system prompt, the retrieved documents, the user's turns, the sampler settings, and the RLHF-shaped policy. The proxy theorists (Green and Michel, Arora) draw the boundary around a designer–deployer–model complex and then find the intention in the designers. Attah draws it around the model plus context and finds a thin intention there. Gubelmann draws it around the model alone and finds none. These parties agree on the functional criterion and disagree on the individuation of the controlled system. The premise the argument turns on is that the control conception is silent on individuation. Any individuation that yields a controller yields an intention, so the three positions are three answers to a different question, and the 'intention' vocabulary only disguises that. The dispute should then be settled by whichever individuation best supports the practice of holding speakers to their acts, not by the metaphysics of intention.",
    "target": "Attah's attributing view, and the proxy/attribution/denial trichotomy as a disagreement about intention",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists and Attah may disagree about intention even holding the boundary fixed, for example on whether a designer's objective can be an LLM's own. Individuation may then be downstream of the intention question and not prior to it."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The 'communicative intention' argument that Attah defends the models against is not really an argument about intention. It is an argument about the determinacy of the content of what is communicated, and the control conception is silent on that.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Attah reads Bender and Koller, Shanahan and Montemayor as holding that competence requires communicative intention and that models lack the mechanism for entertaining one. He answers by supplying a mechanism, namely attention-like selection that solves the Many-Many Problem. But the force of the stochastic-parrot argument lies in what the intention would be an intention to communicate: a content tied to the world that the speaker means the hearer to grasp. The control conception is content-neutral. It explains why one output is selected from many, not why the selected state is about anything in particular, or why the selection is answerable to how things are. A thermostat-like controller meets the control conception while having no determinate communicative content. So Attah's victory is on a premise the opponents could concede: that models have some behaviour-controlling internal state. The live disagreement is over meta-semantics, whether the content of the state is fixed by training distribution, by the deployment context, or by the hearer's interpretation. That is where Skiba's externalist theories of reference come in. Intention is the wrong label for the dispute because it can be granted without moving the debate.",
    "target": "Attah's rebuttal of the communicative intention argument, and the argument's own framing",
    "risk": "Attah can reply that intention just is a content-bearing representational state (he requires consistency with representationalism), so content is already built in. Then the claim reduces to whether his representationalism is earned."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The debate over LLM communicative intentions presupposes that an intention precedes and explains the utterance (Levelt's Conceptualizer before Formulator). For LLMs the only persistent state that carries speaker commitment is the public transcript, so the question is really whether intentions can be constituted by discourse record, which makes Attah and the normative deniers disagree less than they appear to.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Attah maps transformers onto Levelt's pipeline and says the intention is a prior internal controller. But an autoregressive model has no separate pre-verbal message that persists across the generation. At each step the only thing carried forward is the text produced so far (plus the cache). What binds the system to its earlier output, such as a claim it must stay consistent with or an apology it has issued, is the transcript, which is shared and inspectable by the user. So whatever 'intention' the model has is largely retroactively and publicly fixed by what it has already said, much as Anscombe, Brandom and the interpretivists treat intention as answerable to the record of performance. The premise the argument turns on is that, for LLMs, intention-as-prior-inner-state and commitment-as-public-score come apart and only the second is robustly present. Then Attah's 'thin' attribution and Butlin and Viebahn's social-practice condition describe the same structure from two sides. The real contest is between inner-priority and record-constitution accounts of intention, and humans are only a contingent instance of the former.",
    "target": "Attah's Levelt-based argument, and the shared assumption that intention is explanatorily prior to utterance",
    "risk": "Interpretability work suggests models plan ahead in internal states, which restores a prior inner state. Also, a record-constitutive view may give up exactly the Gricean point that makes intention matter for speech acts."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Attah's concession that the real human–LLM difference is the absence of mental and metalinguistic agency means that, on his own account, 'communicative intention' is a red herring. The dispute is about whether performing a speech act requires being able to take that act as one's own, that is, to classify it, own it, and retract it.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Attah argues that communicative intention does not separate humans from models, because even animals and infants have thin versions and models plausibly have graded ones. He then locates the difference in metalinguistic and mental agency. But apologising, promising and asserting are not just illocutionary effects intended on a hearer. They are acts whose performer can be asked 'did you mean that as a promise?' and can withdraw or revise under the act-type description. If metalinguistic agency is what is missing, then it is also what Magnus et al. (moral and linguistic agency), Butlin and Viebahn (sanctionability), and Gubelmann (distinguishing malfunction from doing wrong) are each pointing at, under different names. All of them require a capacity for second-order answerability to one's act under its type-description. The premise the argument turns on is that graded first-order intention is available to a coffee machine in Attah's sense, so it cannot be what makes the speech-act question non-trivial. The dispute then stops being intention-presence versus intention-absence and becomes whether answerability can be conferred, as proxy and fictional accounts suggest, or must be intrinsic. That also explains why the attributionist and denier camps seem to talk past each other.",
    "target": "Attah's closing concession, and the denial camp's self-description as an intention-based or norm-based view",
    "risk": "Metalinguistic agency may itself be a graded functional capacity that LLMs plausibly have, since they can classify and retract their own speech acts. The collapse would then favour attribution and not leave the question open."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Reflexive and habitual speech acts, such as the 'sorry' after bumping into someone, the 'thanks' at a door, or a 'yes' given on autopilot, are full speech acts with no prior or accompanying communicative intention, so the premise that speech acts require communicative intention, which Attah and his Bender-style opponents share, is false, and the dispute should move to standing and answerability.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Both Attah and the stochastic-parrot theorists accept that communicative intention is necessary for speech acts and argue only about whether LLMs have it. Attah thins the intention to a control state, but he keeps it as the necessary condition. Take habitual 'sorry' on bumping into someone. It is produced with no deliberated goal about the hearer's mental states and with no Gricean reflection. It is quick, scripted, and often not endorsed. Yet it is an apology, it can be accepted or rejected, and the speaker is held to it. A defender will say there is a thin intention. But then the notion has been stretched until it is satisfied by any conditioned, situation-triggered output, and it no longer separates apologising from a reflex like a startle cry. The premise the argument turns on is that what makes these cases speech acts is the speaker's position in a practice, meaning she is answerable and the uptake is warranted. A momentary state of her mind is not what does it. If so, the intention question is a proxy for a question about answerability, and Butlin and Viebahn's sanction condition is closer to the mark. The sanction condition can then be tested directly, without intention doing the work.",
    "target": "The shared premise of Attah, Bender and Koller, Shanahan and others that communicative intention is necessary for speech acts, together with Gubelmann's action-requires-intention step.",
    "risk": "Defenders of intention will say that habitual acts are intentional under a standing or dispositional intention, which would make the counterexample collapse into Attah's thin control conception."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Attah's control conception together with Levelt's pipeline overgenerates: fluent (Wernicke-type) aphasic speech, echolalia and confabulation involve internal states that control output by attention-like selection among many candidate outputs in a situation that calls for speech, yet involve no communicative intention. So the functional criteria cannot be what distinguishes communicative intention.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Attah's three desiderata and his control conception (solving the Many-Many Problem through attention-like selection) are meant to be sufficient for intention in the relevant sense. He then maps transformers onto Conceptualizer, Formulator and Articulator. In jargon aphasia the Formulator and Articulator operate with selection, fluency and turn-taking, while the Conceptualizer's preverbal message is absent or decoupled from any goal about the hearer. Patients produce grammatical, situation-appropriate-sounding speech and make no speech acts, and nobody thinks they intend to communicate what they say. Confabulation and echolalia give variants. So control-by-selection is satisfied without communicative intention. What is missing is a state whose content concerns the hearer's uptake and which is the target of the system's correction when uptake fails. The argument turns on the premise that the control conception is meant to be sufficient and not merely necessary, or at least that satisfying it must be non-trivially connected to the communicative case. Once the missing ingredient is stated, the mapping of LLMs onto the pipeline is no evidence for intention, because the contested component is the Conceptualizer, and nothing in next-token prediction shows it is present.",
    "target": "Attah's positive proposal (control conception plus the Levelt-template argument that transformers recapitulate the pipeline).",
    "risk": "Attah could say the conception was offered only as a necessary condition, or that the aphasic case just shows a lower grade of intention, which would make the graded view unfalsifiable."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "A speaker who selects each sentence by rolling a die weighted by her own dispositions, and who never takes up or ratifies the sentence that comes out, does not assert even when every outcome is one she would endorse. This shows that a control state does not by itself control the act, and that what the LLM case shows is intention at the level of the distribution, which is not intention to perform any particular act.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Attah's picture is of an internal state that controls the dynamics so as to bring about an action. In an LLM, the state computes a distribution over next tokens, and an external sampler (a random draw at temperature above zero) picks the token. The selection that fixes which act occurs is not made by the state. Imagine Dana, who has endorsed a set of sentences about a topic and lets a weighted die choose which one to utter, without any check that the one she gets is what she means to say now. Intuitively Dana asserts nothing, or at most asserts a disjunction-like policy. What is missing is ratification: the utterance token being taken up by the same system as the thing it is committed to. The argument turns on the premise that illocutionary acts individuate at the level of the particular token, since 'I promise to come' and 'I might promise to come' differ in kind. It follows that what the control conception can credit to an LLM is a policy-level state, a character or disposition. This is a different bearer from the output-level states that speech acts need. The point cuts against the attributing view and also against the proxy and fictionalist views, which assume the output is one determinate utterance. Autoregressive conditioning on one's own sampled token is the closest the model has to ratification, and it needs separate analysis.",
    "target": "Attah's control conception applied to transformers; indirectly everyone who treats a single chatbot output as the unit of speech-act attribution.",
    "risk": "Human speech also has noise at the neural level, and conditioning on the sampled token may amount to ratification, which would dissolve the contrast with Dana."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Degrees of communicative intention do not run, as Attah's coffee machine < ELIZA < ChatGPT ordering assumes, along the dimension of internal functional complexity, since a two-state Lewis signalling system or an alarm-calling animal whose signals are reshaped by hearers' responses has more communicative intention than a frozen-weight LLM that produces much richer output.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Attah makes intention graded and orders systems by how much of the Levelt-style pipeline and control structure they realize, with the question of ChatGPT's level left open. His example ordering silently equates more intention with more complex internal structure. Counter-case: a minimal signalling system, such as a vervet-style alarm repertoire or a tiny learning agent in a signalling game, has states that cause signals, and those states are modified by whether the receiver's response served the sender's goal. A deployed LLM generates elaborate text, but within an interaction its weights are fixed, its output is not adjusted to the hearer's uptake except through context-window conditioning, and nothing in the system registers success or failure of the intended effect as such. Attah's own opening move, which retreats from strong Gricean demands to a basic intention to produce an effect in a hearer, makes the intended effect the content of the intention. A state is plausibly an intention to produce effect E only if E-success or E-failure is among the things the control loop answers to. The argument turns on this closed-loop requirement, which is a consequence of his own retreat. It reverses the ordering: the simple signaller outranks ChatGPT-with-frozen-weights, and the answer to the LLM question depends on feedback architecture (RLHF training, in-context correction, agent loops with reflection), and not on how fluent or complex the output is. This also cross-cuts the disputes over autonomy, because the loop requirement can be satisfied without self-maintenance.",
    "target": "Attah's graded conception and the complexity-based way of locating LLMs on it; also the implicit assumption in the intention debate that fluency is evidence for intention.",
    "risk": "Training-time feedback (RLHF) and in-context adaptation may supply the loop, so the ordering may be reversed only for the simplest frozen cases, and the thesis would then become a more modest point about which systems qualify."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Barandiaran and Almendros's 'interactional asymmetry' condition conflates two things: who originates an interaction, and who is answerable within it. Because speech-act types differ in how much of the first they require, the asymmetry argument excludes LLMs only from initiative illocutions (unsolicited warnings, promises, offers) and not from responsive ones (answering, confirming, correcting, replying).",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "In the enactive framework, interactional asymmetry means the system never sets the terms of its own coupling: the human starts, steers and ends the exchange. Speech-act theory already sorts illocutions by sequential position, such as first and second pair parts in conversation analysis, and by whether the act's felicity presupposes the speaker's own initiative. A promise or a spontaneous warning presupposes that the speaker took up the occasion. An answer, a confirmation or a concession has its force from a prior solicitation, and humans in institutional roles (a witness, a clerk) perform these under exactly the same asymmetry. The key premise is that responsiveness is constitutive of some act types, not a defect in the speaker's agency. If so, the denial camp's blanket 'no speech acts' verdict, which draws on the enactive asymmetry thesis, needs a taxonomy of acts. It would classify answering and assertion-in-reply differently from advising and promising. The inflationary-versus-deflationary framing would then give way to a sorting of illocutions by how much self-initiation they require.",
    "target": "The enactive denial (Barandiaran and Almendros) and the generalising denials (Gubelmann, Skiba), which treat speech-act capacity as uniformly requiring autonomous agency",
    "risk": "Agent-driven systems with persistent loops can initiate, so the asymmetry may be contingent. Also, responsive acts may still require the speaker to have chosen to respond, which would bring autonomy back in."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Barandiaran and Almendros's middle category, 'purpose-structured but not purposeful', runs together the illocutionary point of an act type and the purpose of a speaker. Speech-act performance requires only the former, so their own characterisation of LLMs is a description of the kind of system that can perform speech acts of its own.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "They say LLM tasks are purpose-structured and purpose-bounded, shaped by training toward ends the system does not itself hold, and they treat this as short of agency. Speech-act theory separates the point internal to an act type (assertion aims at truth, a request at compliance) from the occurrent aims of whoever performs it. Armstrong-style and constitutive-norm views let the first fix what the act is without the second being autonomous or self-generated. The key premise is that purposes imposed from outside can still be the system's operative organisation, in the same way that a role-bound human speaker's purposes are largely imposed. Gubelmann's mechanism/organism contrast and the enactive normativity condition both address purposes of the second kind. Attah's thin functional intention also stays on that side. The new claim is that the debate has been asking an agent-level question (does the system have goals of its own?) about something that needs only an act-level answer (is the output produced under an act-type's point?). Under that question 'library-that-talks' is unstable: a library carries its authors' acts, whereas a purpose-structured generator is a different case.",
    "target": "The enactive no-autonomy argument and, through it, Gubelmann's organismic argument; also Attah's reliance on design-context purposes",
    "risk": "Act-type points may be fixed only by the practice of the speaker's community, or only by a speaker able to represent them, so 'purpose-structured' would not supply act-level purpose after all."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Hybrid ('midtended') human–LLM speech should be analysed by distinguishing standing proxy, where the designer speaks through the system (Green and Michel, Arora), from adoptive utterance, where the user retrospectively makes an LLM output their own by ratifying and deploying it. The speaker of one and the same string differs by uptake.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Proxy accounts assign authorship upstream to creators or deployers, who stand behind every output in advance. Barandiaran and Almendros show that in actual use the user steers, selects and often relays the text, so agency is coupled turn by turn. The unmade distinction is between two directions of authorship assignment: forward-looking (a standing delegation to the proxy) and backward-looking (adoption of a found utterance, as when someone repeats a stranger's sentence and thereby asserts it). On the adoption model, an unadopted answer displayed on screen is either no speech act or something like a proto-act. Pasting it into a report, or acting on it and relaying it, makes it the user's assertion, with the user's commitments and sanctionability. The key premise is that Austinian uptake and commitment can be conferred after production and need not come from the producer. This predicts that the same LLM string can be a user's assertion, a designer's proxy assertion or nobody's, depending on downstream treatment. It also relocates responsibility for misinformation to the user's adoption practices instead of the designer's proxy standing.",
    "target": "Proxy accounts (Green and Michel, Arora, Butlin and Viebahn's proxy use) and the enactive midtended picture, which leaves speaker-assignment unspecified",
    "risk": "Adoption may reduce to ordinary quotation or testimony-relaying, which is already well understood, so nothing new is added. It also cannot cover the many cases where nobody adopts the output, for example chat itself."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The individuality condition used to deny LLMs agency and speakerhood is posed at the wrong grain. Speaker-individuality (a locus of accumulating, retrievable commitments within a dialogue thread) is distinct from the metabolic, self-producing individuality of enactivism, and LLM threads can have the first without the second.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Barandiaran and Almendros argue that LLMs lack individuality in the autopoietic sense: no self-produced, precariously maintained boundary. They also treat 'the LLM' as one entity, though candidate speakers include the weights, a single inference run, a conversation thread, and a role-played persona. Speech acts need a bearer who can be held to what was said earlier (consistency, retraction, apology, promise-keeping). That diachronic bearer is constituted by commitments in the context window, which the thread maintains through conditioning on its own prior outputs. A thread has a boundary (the context), it is individuated by what it has committed to, and it is reidentified across turns by users who hold it to account. The key premise is that the relevant individuality for speech acts is commitment-individuality, which is normative and social, not metabolic. Enactive individuality is then the wrong test for illocutionary authorship, and the question 'can the LLM assert?' is ill-posed until the candidate bearer is fixed. This splits the field: weights fail, threads may succeed, and no existing position distinguishes them.",
    "target": "The enactive individuality condition, and the general practice in the debate of treating 'the LLM' as a single candidate speaker",
    "risk": "A thread's commitments exist only because the user keeps them in context, and the thread can be forked or edited at will. Fork-ability may show that nothing is individuated at the thread level either."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Attah's control conception of intention, combined with his appeal to design-supplied purposes, commits him to the proxy view: because a system prompt or in-context instruction is text that controls the model's output dynamics and encodes someone else's communicative aim, the state that fills the role of the model's intention is literally the deployer's intention in textual form.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "On Attah's account an intention is an internal state that selects among many possible outputs in a situation that calls for one. In a deployed LLM, the states doing that selecting are largely the system prompt and the user instruction, held in context and acting through attention. They are authored by humans, they specify the effect to be produced on a hearer (\"apologise sincerely\", \"advise cautiously\"), and the model's dynamics then execute them. So the control conception does not find an originary intention inside the model. It finds an installed, delegated one, whose content and target were fixed by an external author and transmitted by text. A human who is told what to say and says it is the standard case of a proxy or delegated speech act. The premise it turns on is that control by an externally authored, content-bearing state is not originary control. Attah must then either say that the model's intention is the operator's, which is Green and Michel's view relabelled, or deny that prompt-borne states are intentions. The second option leaves little for the model's own purposes to be, because they were supposed to come from design context. The attributing and relocating families therefore collapse into each other.",
    "target": "Attah's claim that design context supplies LLMs with purposes, and the assumption that attributing views and proxy views are rivals",
    "risk": "Attah can say the weights also carry trained dispositions beyond the prompt, so intention is the joint product of both. Then the proxy reading is only partial, and a human employee's trained dispositions are not obviously non-originary either."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "If intentions are the internal states that control output, then at standard sampling temperatures the control state of an LLM fixes only a probability distribution over speech acts, so Attah must either count the stochastic sampler as part of the speaker's will or accept that LLMs have only disjunctive intentions that never settle which illocutionary act was performed.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "A speech act is individuated by what the speaker does, such as asserting versus conjecturing versus joking, and the control conception ties intention to the state that brings about that action. In an autoregressive model the state at each step is a logit vector, and which token follows is settled by a sampling procedure outside the network, often by a pseudo-random number. The same internal state therefore yields \"I promise\" on one run and \"I'll try\" on another, and the same prompt yields confident or hedged assertion. Attah has three options. (a) The intention is the distribution, which gives an intention to perform some act from a set but not to perform any particular act, and Gricean communicative intentions are specific. (b) The sampler is part of the controller, so the temperature setting, which is a user or API parameter, partly sets the system's will. (c) Greedy decoding is the canonical case, so sampled outputs are not really the model's acts. Each option costs him something: (a) undermines the fit with the Gricean basic intention he retains, (b) is bizarre and pushes agency outward again, and (c) excludes most actual use. The premise it turns on is that intention must individuate the act type, not merely cause the output.",
    "target": "Attah's control conception of intention (the Many-Many Problem solved by attention-like selection) as applied to transformers",
    "risk": "Human speakers are also noisy, and neural noise could be said to play the same role as sampling. The objection then reduces to a difference of degree, and Attah already embraces graded intention."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Attah's thin, non-mind-reading conception of intention cannot support his own Levelt/POMDP argument, which makes the case for LLM intention depend on a hearer-modelling component, so his view is either a graded Gricean view or unable to rank ELIZA below ChatGPT.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Attah rejects the strong Gricean requirement of higher-order representation of other minds as empirically untenable, citing children, animals and hominins. Yet his positive case relies on the dialogue-system architecture, in which belief-state tracking estimates the user's intention and the Conceptualizer selects messages in light of that estimate. That is a functional analogue of modelling the hearer's mind. The same component is what he uses to rank systems on the gradient from coffee machine to ELIZA to ChatGPT. This yields a dilemma. If the hearer model is not needed for communicative intention, the Levelt and POMDP machinery does no work, and nothing in the control conception distinguishes a speech-intending system from any goal-directed controller, so a thermostat-style regulator ranks as high as ELIZA. If the hearer model is what earns the higher grade, then the gradient measures depth of hearer-modelling, and the view is a Gricean view in degrees. The anti-Gricean empirical citations then show only that the threshold is low, not that the structure is different. The premise it turns on is that what grades the gradient must be what the thin conception counts as essential to the intention.",
    "target": "Attah's rejection of the Gricean conception together with his graded, architecture-based positive account",
    "risk": "Attah can say hearer-tracking is one contributor to the degree of intention without being a necessary condition. The dilemma then lets him take a pluralist horn in which intention has several graded dimensions."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Attah's closing concession that the real human–LLM difference is the absence of mental and metalinguistic agency commits him to the normative denial of speech acts, because it makes the capacity to own, monitor and answer for an utterance, rather than communicative intention, the condition for speech-act performance, so winning on intentions concedes the question.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Attah argues that LLMs have communicative intentions in a graded, limited form and that intention is therefore not what separates them from humans. But he also says the genuine difference lies in mental and metalinguistic agency, meaning representing one's own states and one's utterances as utterances. Speech acts are standardly taken to be acts whose performer can be held to what was said, can retract, correct or clarify, and can recognise the act as the kind it is. Apologising, promising and asserting each require this. That is the substance of Butlin and Viebahn's sanction condition and of Magnus et al.'s agency condition. If the missing capacities are exactly the ones needed for being the author of a speech act, then the intention-based argument, even if sound, establishes at most that LLMs have an ingredient of speech acts and not that they perform any. Attah must either deny that metalinguistic agency matters for speech acts, which makes his own diagnosis of the difference idle, or accept that the normative views have the correct verdict and the intentions debate is orthogonal to it. The premise it turns on is that speech-act performance requires the ability to take the act as one's own, and that this is not supplied by having a control state aimed at an effect.",
    "target": "Attah's overall conclusion that communicative intention is not the right ground for distinguishing humans from LLMs",
    "risk": "Attah might distinguish having a speech act from being a full speaker, treating metalinguistic agency as required for responsibility but not for illocution, which would restore the independence of the two questions."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "The dispute over whether 'the LLM' performs speech acts is partly verbal, because the rival camps are predicating of different subjects (weights, running instance, simulated persona, deployed product), and Barandiaran and Almendros's failed 'individuality' condition is satisfied at one of these levels and not at others.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Barandiaran and Almendros deny individuality because an LLM is a statistical artefact with no self-produced boundary: one set of weights is instantiated in many parallel conversations. But they evaluate the weights-plus-architecture. Gubelmann's organismic argument also evaluates the system as such. Williams and Bayne's proto-assertion and the proxy views each tacitly pick another bearer (the persona or the deployer). The thread-level process is a different case: within a conversation, a context window bounds a self-referentially constrained process that maintains its own coherence (persona, commitments, consistency) against perturbation. That is a thin but real operational closure of the kind enactivists use for individuality. The key premise is that the enactive individuality criterion is level-relative and does not fix a unique referent for 'the LLM'. Once the referent is fixed, most of the denial/attribution disagreement splits into compatible verdicts (no for the weights, perhaps partial for the thread, yes for the deployer-proxy). What remains is a dispute about which level 'the speaker' in speech-act theory should pick out, and that is a question about the role and not about agency.",
    "target": "Barandiaran and Almendros's individuality condition and, more broadly, the shared assumption that 'LLM' has a single referent in the question 'can it assert?'",
    "risk": "The thread-level closure is operationally cheap, since it is imposed from outside by the context window and the serving infrastructure. The enactivist will say it is therefore heteronomous and the levels do not rescue individuality. Persona-level treatments are also already familiar from the role-play literature."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Barandiaran and Almendros's own category of tasks that are 'purpose-structured and purpose-bounded' but not purposeful gives illocutionary force everything it needs, so the whole intention-and-autonomy debate is aimed at the wrong determinant of speech-act identity.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Gubelmann, Attah and the enactivists all argue about whether LLM purposes are the system's own, and so about whether the speaker has intentions. But what makes an utterance an advising, a warning or an answer is largely fixed by the frame (genre, institutional role, the exchange so far), not by the speaker's private goal hierarchy. A scripted clerk or a witness reading a prepared statement performs acts whose force is set by the frame, with the speaker's own purposes playing little part. Barandiaran and Almendros concede that LLM outputs are purpose-bounded by the interaction frame. If force is frame-determined, this concession already gives the output a determinate force, and the self-set-goals question concerns only who is answerable for the act, not what act it is. The key premise is that force-identity and authorship/answerability can come apart, and that the autonomy literature runs them together. The result is a reframing: the argument is about answerability, not about speech-act performance.",
    "target": "The shared premise of Gubelmann, Attah and Barandiaran–Almendros that settling the intentionality or autonomy of the system settles whether it performs a speech act.",
    "risk": "Austin and Searle build sincerity and speaker-intention conditions into success, so 'act with determinate force but no performer' may be incoherent. The result may then be only the proxy view under a new name."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "If LLMs are 'libraries that talk', the question 'does the model assert?' presupposes a production-side individuation of speech acts that fits spoken conversation but not texts, and on the uptake-completed model that suits stored-and-retrieved language the dispute dissolves into a question about when, and by whom, the act is completed.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Barandiaran and Almendros's best image is that an LLM is a library rendered conversational. Nobody asks whether a library asserts. We ask who asserted what is in the books, and a reader consulting a book is not asserted to in the same sense. Written and recorded speech already shows that illocutionary acts need not be located at the moment of production. Austin's insistence on uptake offers a model in which an LLM output is an act-potential completed by the user's prompt-and-reading, much as a lawyer's template becomes a contract only when invoked. Under this model, 'Did the LLM assert?', 'Did the proxy assert?' and 'Did the user assert to themselves?' are answers to different temporal questions. The key premise is that the participants all treat the speech act as a production-time event with an agent. Giving it up removes the pressure behind the proxy and quasi-assertion proposals, because the act has no single performer at all and is a joint, distributed completion.",
    "target": "The assumption shared by denial, proxy and attribution views that a speech act is an event with a determinate time and performer at the point of production; it also bears on Barandiaran and Almendros's 'midtended agency'.",
    "risk": "It risks collapsing into the existing 'hybrid agency' or Williams–Bayne 'proto-assertion' views. LLM outputs are also produced interactively and contingently, unlike a static library, so the library analogy may not support a delayed-completion model."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The 'interactional asymmetry' condition that Barandiaran and Almendros use to deny LLM agency measures initiative, not agency; the acts at issue (answering, advising, apologising, asserting-in-reply) are responsive, so the denial concerns a different class of speech acts than the one the debate is about.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "The enactivist asymmetry condition says the system must be the source of its own coupling to the environment, whereas an LLM is activated, turn by turn, by the user. But in human speech the same pattern holds for a large class of acts: an expert answering a question, a clerk confirming a booking, a person apologising when told they have offended. These acts are structurally triggered by the interlocutor and take their content from the prompt. The asymmetry condition would deprive the responder of agency in just these exchanges, which is absurd, so it must be tracking something else, namely the capacity to initiate. Initiating acts (promising unprompted, volunteering a warning, changing the topic) are where the denial is really strong. The paper's own examples of ordinary 'genuine conversation' sit on the responsive side. The key premise is that the structure of speech acts allows a principled initiating/responsive divide, and that the arguments about agency in this literature are driven by the initiating case while the cited cases are responsive. The dispute then turns out to be partly about two different explananda.",
    "target": "Barandiaran and Almendros's interactional-asymmetry condition and the general move from 'not an autonomous agent' to 'performs no speech acts'.",
    "risk": "Responsive acts may still require a standing background of the speaker's own concerns, so asymmetry may track agency after all. Also, a distinction between initiating and responsive acts splits an existing distinction more finely and does not obviously change any verdict."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Speech acts need no intender of their content at the moment of production: sortition and oracle cases show that an output can be a valid assertion, verdict or directive when no one intended that content and an institution simply adopts it. If so, the proxy account (a human intended it, by proxy) and the denial account (nobody intended it, so no act) both rest on a false shared premise.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Barandiaran and Almendros call LLM tasks 'purpose-structured and purpose-bounded' without being purposeful, and call the LLM a 'library-that-talks'. Proxy theorists (Green and Michel, Arora) and deniers (Gubelmann, Skiba) agree that a speech act needs a content-level intention, either the machine's or a human's by proxy. Counterexample: verdicts by lot, drawing a name for office, a coin toss that settles who must testify, and a jury's quotient verdict. In each the content is generated by a process nobody intended, and the act is nonetheless performed, and attributable to someone, because a competent party adopts the output under a convention. Here the act is constituted by ratification or uptake, not by an antecedent intention; the proxy relation (a human intended it on someone's behalf) is not what makes it an act. The premise the argument turns on is that these cases are speech acts and not mere decisions. That is supported by their having felicity conditions and being open to challenge as invalid. If so, an LLM output ratified by a deployer or user can be an assertion or advice with no content-intender, and the 'midtended' hybrid is a ratification structure, not a blend of agencies.",
    "target": "The shared premise of proxy accounts (Green and Michel, Arora) and denial accounts (Gubelmann, Skiba) that illocutionary force requires a content-level intention, whether the machine's or a human's by proxy; also Barandiaran and Almendros's use of 'purpose-structured' tasks.",
    "risk": "Lot cases may be said to be acts of the adopter, with the lot only an input, which collapses the view back into proxy or ratification-by-human. Also, the generated content of a lot is trivial (yes/no), whereas LLM content is rich and truth-apt."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Organismic individuality is not a necessary condition on speakers: the individuation that speech acts require is of a commitment-and-sanction locus, as with a pseudonymous collective author or a rotating-staff institutional account. The relevant individual for LLM speech acts is therefore the persona-thread, not the model, so Barandiaran and Almendros's failed individuality test is aimed at the wrong entity.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Barandiaran and Almendros argue that LLMs lack individuality, in the enactive sense of self-produced and self-maintained boundaries. Gubelmann and Butlin-Viebahn assume the candidate speaker is 'the model' or 'the chatbot'. Counterexample: Nicolas Bourbaki, a long-lived pseudonymous account run by a rotating team, or an anonymous editorial board. These have no organism, no self-maintaining boundary and no single body, yet they make assertions, retract them, are corrected and are held to them. The speaker is individuated by a stable pattern of commitments and by uptake of sanctions. A conversation thread with a persisting persona, in which each output conditions later outputs and so constrains what the persona can consistently say, is a candidate locus of this kind, even though the weights are not. The individuality condition then splits into organismic individuality, which LLMs lack, and discursive individuality, which is the one speech acts need. The argument turns on the premise that Bourbaki-type cases are speaker-individuation cases and not proxy cases for the members. That premise can be defended because the team members cannot be held to Bourbaki's claims.",
    "target": "Barandiaran and Almendros's individuality condition, and the shared assumption (Gubelmann, Butlin-Viebahn, Skiba) that the model is the relevant candidate speaker.",
    "risk": "The Bourbaki-type persona is sustained by humans who do the committing, so the cases may be proxy cases after all. Persona threads in LLMs also lack persisting memory and sanctionability, which Butlin and Viebahn already count against them."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The assumption that a speech-act intention exists prior to or alongside output, which underlies both Gubelmann's malfunction-versus-deliberate test and Attah's Levelt-style planning template, is false even of human speakers. Much human illocution is incremental, with the force of the act settled by retrospective ownership, so the human-versus-LLM difference cannot be located in the presence of an intention.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Both denials and attributions treat the speaker as having something like a conceptualised message and a communicative intention that the Formulator then realises (Attah) or whose failure can be told apart from malfunction (Gubelmann). Barandiaran and Almendros likewise contrast the LLM's purpose-structured but not purposeful operation with human purposefulness. Counterexample: ordinary improvised talk. Speakers begin sentences without having settled what they will assert, discover the force of the utterance from where it goes ('I was going to say it was a warning, but I suppose it was an accusation'), and become committed by endorsing or disowning it in the next turn. Splitting such acts from their reflective ownership makes the intention post hoc and constituted by follow-up conduct, not causal. Speech acts are therefore constituted by a diachronic pattern of owning and retracting. This reframes the dispute: the question is whether LLM threads show the right pattern of taking up, owning and being held to earlier outputs. This is an empirical question about interaction structure, not an issue about internal mental states. The premise it turns on is that retrospective force-fixing is not a marginal case.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's malfunction test, Attah's pipeline template for communicative intention, and the implicit human paradigm in Barandiaran and Almendros.",
    "risk": "Defenders can reply that the improviser still has proximal intentions at each step and that retrospective ownership presupposes a self, which is exactly what LLMs lack. The view also resembles interpretationist accounts of intention, so it may not be new."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Barandiaran and Almendros's 'interactional asymmetry' condition, on which the system does not originate or set the terms of its own coupling, overgenerates. It excludes human communicators whose speech acts are uncontroversial, such as locked-in patients using a gaze speller, or people with advanced dementia whose conversational frame is wholly scaffolded by carers, so it cannot be a necessary condition on agency or on speech-act performance.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Barandiaran and Almendros argue that LLMs fail interactional asymmetry because the human initiates, sustains and defines the interaction, and the model cannot modulate its own coupling. They treat this as a necessary condition for autonomous agency, and the speech-act literature takes autonomy as the target contrast. Counterexample: a locked-in patient whose only channel is an eye-tracked speller. The speller's language model predicts words, the carer starts the session, sets the topic and frames the options, and the patient's contribution is a series of binary selections. A person with advanced dementia in a carer-scaffolded exchange likewise neither starts the interaction nor controls its terms. Yet 'yes, I forgive you', 'I want to go home' and 'no' are paradigm assertions, promises, apologies and requests. If asymmetry of coupling does not defeat the speech act there, it cannot defeat it for LLMs. What does matter is whether the asymmetry is total, or whether the system has some residual veto and norm-bearing capacity, which is a graded and different condition. The argument turns on the premise that the enactive 'autonomy' condition is meant to ground speech-act performance and not only full autonomous agency. Barandiaran and Almendros can concede the counterexample only by splitting the two, which concedes the speech-act question.",
    "target": "Barandiaran and Almendros's interactional asymmetry condition, and the assumption in the wider denial literature that human cases satisfy all organismic autonomy conditions in a robust way.",
    "risk": "The enactive reply is that the patient retains self-produced norms, including the capacity to refuse, which the LLM lacks, so asymmetry in their sense is not total in the human cases; the counterexample then fails to match the condition as stated."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Barandiaran and Almendros's category of 'interlocutor without agency' is unstable: if interlocutorhood is the thick status of a participant in a normative discourse practice, it requires the normativity they deny LLMs, and if it is a thin role assigned by the human participant, their view becomes a form of fictionalism.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "They present the LLM as a 'linguistic automaton' or 'library-that-talks' that is nonetheless an interlocutor, one that can be experienced as a genuine conversational partner. The question is what interlocutor status consists in. On a Brandom- or Clark-style reading, an interlocutor undertakes commitments, can be held to them, and keeps score. That is a normative standing, so it conflicts with their claim that LLMs lack enactive normativity. On a thin reading, 'interlocutor' names a slot in the interaction that the human fills by treating the system as someone who answers. Then what makes the system an interlocutor is the human's stance and the shared pretence in the exchange, and that is Mallory's fictionalism under another description. The 'library' metaphor cannot hold the middle. A library has authors behind it, so if the system generates novel text the metaphor fails, and if it only conveys the authors' content the view is a proxy view. The key premise is that the interlocutor role cannot be filled by something that is neither a normative participant nor a fictional character.",
    "target": "Barandiaran and Almendros's ontology of LLMs as interlocutors that are not agents; more generally, anyone who denies agency while granting 'genuine conversation'.",
    "risk": "They could take a third reading on which interlocutorhood is a real relational property constituted by structured, history-sensitive coupling, with no commitments and no pretence. They would then need to show this coupling yields speech acts."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The enactivist denial of LLM speech acts, which rests on individuality, normativity and interactional asymmetry, collapses into the sanction-based normative denial of Butlin and Viebahn, because only the social-normative reading of its conditions bears on speech acts at all.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Enactive normativity is viability-relative: it concerns what is good or bad for a precarious, self-producing system. The norms of assertion, promising or apology are social and deontic: commitment, entitlement, answerability. These come apart, since people routinely assert, promise and apologise at cost to their own viability. So the enactivist has two options. One is to say the social norms are derived from viability norms, which is implausible given that these speech acts regularly override viability. The other is to concede that the individuality and normativity conditions do no work on speech acts, leaving 'interactional asymmetry' and the question of being a party to sanctionable exchange. That leaves the Butlin–Viebahn question of whether the system can be sanctioned and answer for its outputs. That question is contingent, and it can be answered differently for RLHF-tuned, memory-bearing deployed systems, where users reprimand, models revise, and training is updated in response. The enactive verdict then stops being a principled 'no' grounded in life and becomes an empirical verdict about current deployment. The key premise is that the biological and social senses of 'norm' are independent in speech-act contexts.",
    "target": "Embodied and enactive denials of LLM agency as applied to speech acts (Barandiaran and Almendros, and by extension Gubelmann's organismic route).",
    "risk": "Enactivists such as Di Paolo and Thompson hold that social normativity is continuous with and grounded in sense-making, so the independence premise may beg the question against them."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "If the enactivist grants, as Di Paolo and colleagues do, that human linguistic agency is largely constituted by socially imposed, self-sustaining norms of language, then the line between 'purpose-structured' and 'purposeful' cannot do the work Barandiaran and Almendros need, and the dispute reduces to a question about the metabolic substrate, which is irrelevant to illocutionary content.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "They say LLM tasks are purpose-structured and purpose-bounded but not purposeful, because the purposes are imposed from outside. Enactivism's own account of language ('linguistic bodies', participatory sense-making) says that most of a human speaker's communicative purposes, and the norms of what counts as asserting or apologising, are acquired through enculturation. They are inherited, imposed and sustained by a community before any individual endorses them. What is distinctively the human's own is not the content or even the origin of those purposes. It is that the human has a precarious metabolic substrate in which they matter. So purposefulness is a property of a substrate that adopts purposes, not of the purposes. The denial of speech acts then needs a further premise connecting substrate-precariousness to illocutionary performance, and nothing in the account of speech acts supplies it. Without that premise the position becomes either Gubelmann's material-autonomy view, with its odd consequence that a precarious but linguistically incompetent organism has a better claim to speak, or Attah's graded functional view, which it set out to reject. The key premise is that imposed origin of purposes cannot be what separates human from LLM purposiveness.",
    "target": "The 'purpose-structured but not purposeful' distinction, and the shared Kantian-organismic assumption that externally fixed goals disqualify intentional action.",
    "risk": "The enactivist can reply that taking up a norm is itself the enactive achievement that matters, and that this is exactly what the substrate supplies, so the substrate is not irrelevant."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Barandiaran and Almendros's 'midtended' hybrid agency is not a new third kind of agent. It is the author–ghostwriter relation that proxy accounts already model, so it either collapses into a proxy view or must treat the LLM as a co-speaker, which contradicts their denial of its agency.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "The novelty claim is that the human–LLM coupling yields forms of agency that earlier tool-extensions did not. For speech acts this must mean the following. Either some entity (the hybrid or the human) is the speaker, or the LLM's contribution is part of what makes the act that of its speaker. Take speechwriting, ghostwriting and spokespersons, which are well-studied cases of proxy and conditional speech acts (Green and Michel). The person who endorses the output is the speaker, and the drafter's contribution creatively shapes what is said without making the drafter a speaker. A human who adopts an LLM's draft stands in the same relation. What is new is only a change in degree: how much of the content is authored by the drafting part, and how little endorsement the human gives. If the hybrid is the speaker, its individuality and precariousness are exactly the human's, so the hybrid is the human plus an instrument, and the denial of LLM agency is preserved but nothing is new. If instead the LLM's non-endorsed contributions make speech acts that no human performs, then the LLM is in effect a co-author, and that needs an agency they explicitly deny. The key premise is that endorsement by a human is what makes the hybrid's speech acts someone's.",
    "target": "The claim that midtended agency marks a qualitatively new mode of agency, and the proxy/hybrid contrast.",
    "risk": "Cases of unendorsed, uncritical use might show the human contributing no meaningful authorship, in which case the 'hybrid' really is a distinct unit. That would make the collapse partial rather than complete."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "The question 'can an LLM perform speech acts?' is under-specified because the candidate speaker can be individuated at four different grains (the weight file, the running inference instance, the deployed persona, the human–model network), and the main positions in the debate differ largely because each silently picks a different grain.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Puzio treats 'the LLM' as a node whose boundaries are an open question, and she dissolves it into a network. The speech-act literature never asks this. Gubelmann's organismic argument targets the trained artifact, whose goals are externally fixed. Attah's graded intention is assigned to a pipeline instance with belief-state tracking. Butlin's agency criterion applies to the system plus memory and reflection scaffolding, i.e. a language agent. Williams and Bayne's proto-assertion fits a persona in a conversation. Proxy views make the 'machine' a mere conduit. Once the grains are separated, many apparent disagreements go away. The weight file is a policy-type that cannot assert, just as a grammar cannot. A persona can be a fictional character whose assertions are real only inside the fiction, which brings fictionalism and attribution close together. The instance is the only grain with a single context and a single stream of 'commitments'. The key premise is that each speech-act condition (intention, sanctionability, descriptive function) is satisfied or failed at different grains, so no single grain passes them all. The denials then win only by mixing grains, for example assessing intention at the instance and sanction at the weights.",
    "target": "The shared assumption, across Gubelmann, Attah, Butlin and Viebahn, and Williams and Bayne, that there is one determinate entity, 'the LLM', whose speech-act status is in question.",
    "risk": "Defenders can reply that the instance or persona is the obvious default and that the individuation worry is a trivial relabelling. Also, humans have analogous multiple grains (organism, persona, role) without any trouble, which could make the distinction look idle."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Proxy accounts conflate two relations, authorization (a principal licenses the speech) and norm-sourcing (someone's standards fix what counts as a correct output), and in LLMs these come apart: designers and deployers authorize outputs, while the dispositions that determine each output's content and tone are shaped by annotators and raters who never authorize anything.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Green and Michel and Arora model machine speech on proxy or recorded speech, where the principal's objectives fix what is meant. For a thermostat-like or scripted device this works, because the designer's objectives settle the content. For a model whose outputs come from RLHF-shaped dispositions, the designer's objective is typically only 'be helpful and harmless', and the particular content is the aggregate residue of thousands of raters' judgments. Puzio's attention to the labour conditions of LLM production makes these workers visible as contributors to the network. The distinction shows that the proxy relation has two candidate principals. The authorizing principal is answerable but does not fix the content. The norm-sourcing crowd fixes content but has not licensed any utterance. If a proxy speech act needs a principal whose objectives are expressed in the utterance, then for much LLM output nobody satisfies both roles. Proxy accounts then split into a 'responsibility-assignment' view, which survives but is not a theory of what was said, and a 'ghostwriting' view, which is implausible. The key premise is Armstrong-style speaker meaning in terms of the principal's objectives, which requires that the objective bear on the specific content.",
    "target": "Green and Michel's and Arora's proxy accounts, and Butlin and Viebahn's use of proxy assertion as the fallback.",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists can say that proxy speech only requires a general objective, as with a spokesperson briefed loosely, so no content-level match is needed. Then the authorization/norm-sourcing split would not threaten the account."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The debate is about production-constitution (a speech act exists in virtue of facts about the speaker), whereas the relational picture suggests uptake-constitution (illocutionary force is fixed in the hearer's securing of uptake). These two give opposite classifications of fictionalism and of the 'text that humans interpret as speech acts' deflationary option.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Every denial and attributing view asks whether the machine has the intentions, norms or agency to produce the act. The fallback 'it only produces text that humans interpret as such' treats interpretation as epistemically downstream of a fact about production. Puzio's relational view and the Austin-Hornsby-Langton tradition on uptake suggest another structure. Some illocutions, such as promising, advising, apologising and warning, are completed or constituted in an interaction in which a hearer takes them up and so incurs entitlements. On a strict uptake-constitutive reading, a human interpreting a chatbot's text as advice does not merely misdescribe a non-act. It may help bring about an act of advice whose speaker slot is filled by the network, with the 'interpretation' being part of the act. This makes the standard denial 'merely interpreted' ill-formed for uptake-dependent acts, while leaving it fine for acts like assertion that may be production-constituted. Fictionalism then becomes the view that users deliberately withhold uptake. The key premise is that the speech acts differ in whether their constitutive conditions are speaker-side or hearer-side, so that no uniform answer across assertion, promise and apology is available.",
    "target": "The 'merely interpreted' deflationary baseline shared by the denials, and Mallory's and Williams and Bayne's treatment of interpretation as downstream of production.",
    "risk": "Uptake-constitutive theories are a minority view, and even their proponents require an act-performer. Without an illocutor the account may simply restate that a speech act needs a speaker, so the move collapses into the proxy view."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Butlin and Viebahn's sanctionability condition is ambiguous between being a target of sanction (being the kind of thing that can be held to account) and being a conduit of sanction (being a node through which sanction alters future outputs), and current models plausibly meet the second but not the first, so their argument establishes less than a denial.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "The norm-based denials say assertion requires openness to sanction, and models cannot be blamed, penalised or made to lose standing. But consider a user complaint, a downvote or a red-team report entering a training loop that shifts the dispositions generating later outputs. Sanction is then literally routed through the model's behaviour, as when a human speaker's reputational losses change what they later say. Puzio's network view makes this routing visible, since the sanctioning, the company, the raters and the retraining are part of one agentive network. The condition can be read in two ways. On the first, sanction must land on a bearer who suffers or is answerable. On the second, sanction must be functionally efficacious in regulating the producer's outputs. Read the first way, the condition is a moral-agency requirement that does the same work as Magnus et al.'s apology argument and is not specific to assertion. Read the second way, it is satisfied by models with online or periodic learning from feedback. Butlin's own agency criterion, sensitivity to instrumental value through learning, seems to give the second reading. So his two papers pull in opposite directions unless he adopts the stronger reading, which then has to be justified for assertion specifically. The key premise is that the practice-internal function of sanction in assertion is regulation of the speaker's reliability, not retribution.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn 2025, and Skiba's norm-based extension to all speech acts.",
    "risk": "The sanction must plausibly reach the speaker as the same entity over time. Retraining produces a successor model, not a corrected one, so this routing is not sanction of the same speaker, which would revive the first reading."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Proxy accounts of LLM speech acts are unstable: they presuppose a determinate principal whose objectives the machine's utterance carries, but for RLHF-trained models no such principal exists, so the view collapses into attributing the speech act to the trained system itself.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Green and Michel and Arora model proxy speech acts on a spokesperson or recorded message, where an identifiable principal has the relevant objective (Armstrong-style) and the device transmits it. Puzio's actor-network point is that an LLM's dispositions are shaped by annotators, preference-aggregation procedures, scraped authors, policy teams and optimisation dynamics that no one fully specified. The key premise is that proxy-hood requires the output to be answerable to some agent's communicative objective, and that this objective must be fixed prior to and independently of the particular output. For a deployed model, the only objective that reliably and specifically covers a particular novel utterance is the one encoded in the model's learned policy. If the principal is the company, the company has no objective about this sentence (a conditional proxy about all outputs is too thin to ground a particular assertion or promise). If the principal is the distributed network, no one is the speaker. So either the proxy view is true only of scripted systems, or its explanatory work is done by the policy's own objective-tracking, which is the attributing view (Attah, Butlin) under another name.",
    "target": "Proxy accounts (Green and Michel; Arora; Butlin and Viebahn's fallback), and the shared assumption that proxy is the safe middle position.",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists can say that conditional or standing intentions ('say whatever the best-trained system says') suffice, as with delegated discretion in human agency. Then the collapse claim fails and the real question becomes how much discretion a proxy relation tolerates."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The organismic/enactive denial of LLM speech acts (Gubelmann; Barandiaran and Almendros) is unstable, because its self-maintenance and individuality criteria are satisfied at the level of the model-plus-infrastructure-plus-firm assemblage, so the denial collapses into a relocation view with a corporate-hybrid speaker.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Gubelmann requires goals that are self-set by a self-maintaining system; Barandiaran and Almendros require individuality, meaning the system itself generates and regulates its own boundary. Puzio's emphasis on materiality (data centres, energy, maintenance labour, revenue loops, retraining) shows the model does not exist as a bounded unit apart from these. The key premise is that the autonomy criteria are boundary-relative, so a verdict of failure for 'the weights' says nothing about the whole. Applied to the weights alone, the criteria also fail for a liver, a neuron or a cell line outside its organism, which shows the unit of assessment cannot be fixed by physical encapsulation. Applied to the assemblage, which does precipitate its own continuation (revenue funds compute, deployment generates training signal, goals are revised in light of product performance), the criteria are met at least as well as for a firm. The denial then becomes the claim that the author of the speech act is a hybrid corporate-technical agent, which is a relocating view. The organismic argument cannot deliver the 'LLMs do not speak' conclusion without an independent, non-arbitrary reason to treat the weights as the candidate speaker.",
    "target": "Gubelmann's Kantian argument and the enactive individuality/normativity criteria.",
    "risk": "Enactivists will say firms are not autopoietic, because their self-maintenance depends on human organisms, so autonomy is derivative. This reintroduces proxy-style relocation to humans instead of defeating the collapse, but the premise that boundaries are non-arbitrary may be defended by appeal to precariousness."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "For speech acts, the contest between property-based and relational accounts of agency is not a real contest, because the leading denial (assertion needs sanctionability) is already relational, and a relational condition makes LLM speakership depend on conferrable social status and so collapses into a status-function view on which LLMs can be speakers by institutional decision.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Puzio frames the debate as individualistic property accounts versus network accounts. But Butlin and Viebahn's second condition (capable of being sanctioned by interlocutors) and Skiba's norm-based argument make speakerhood partly constituted by how others can hold one to account. The key premise is that a sanction-capacity condition is not an intrinsic property but a feature of a deontic arrangement: whether a model can be sanctioned depends on whether there are practices of reputation-loss, retraining, de-deployment or liability that attach to the model qua model rather than to its owner. These are institutional choices, already partly in place (models are deprecated for bad assertions, and users stop trusting 'it'). So the denial's verdict is contingent on arrangements we could change, and its best version predicts that LLMs become assertors when we build the right sanction practices. That is the structure of a Searlean status-function view, which the denial was meant to oppose. The 'beyond them (for now)' position turns out to concede that the question is political-conventional rather than about what the model is.",
    "target": "Normative denials (Butlin and Viebahn; Skiba) and the framing of the debate as individualist versus relational.",
    "risk": "Defenders can say sanction requires the sanctioned party to be capable of caring about sanction, an intrinsic condition, so status conferral cannot suffice. This reinstates an intrinsic property and would collapse the view toward the organismic denial instead."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Puzio's relational-material view of agency is unstable as a position on speech acts: the 'making a difference' criterion of agency she shares with Actor-Network Theory is too cheap to distinguish speakers from coffee machines, and once the speech-act-relevant subset of the network is picked out, her view collapses into either a proxy account or the thin functional account.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Puzio admits non-human agency because actants transform the network by making a difference. Speech acts require a narrower role: being the one who commits, advises or apologises, who can be addressed in the second person, and whose act is attributable. The key premise is that any view of speech acts must carry a principle that selects, among the many difference-making actants, which one is the author of this assertion. ANT's symmetry principle forbids privileging by metaphysical kind, so the selection must be done by network position or function. Two options exist. If the selecting function is 'node that carries a human principal's communicative objective', the view becomes proxy (Green and Michel). If it is 'node whose internal state controls the output in response to the situation', it becomes Attah's control conception, with a graded scale on which a thermostat has a little. Either way the relational view supplies no theory of speakerhood of its own, and what looks like a third option is a redescription of one of its neighbours, with the materiality talk playing no role in the verdict.",
    "target": "Puzio's relational-materialist account and relational accounts of distributed agency generally, as applied to speech acts.",
    "risk": "Puzio does not claim to offer a theory of speech acts, so the charge may be unfair, and a relationalist could embrace distributed authorship (a joint speech act by the network) as a genuine third position, as with group assertions."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Much of the disagreement over whether 'the LLM' performs speech acts is verbal, because 'the LLM' picks out different candidate speakers in different camps (weights, a running inference process, a persona, a deployed product, the vendor), and each camp's verdict is correct for its own referent.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Puzio stresses that an LLM is a heterogeneous assemblage of data, workers, hardware, interfaces and deployments, not a single bearer of properties. Take that seriously as a point about reference. Gubelmann's organismic argument is about a trained artifact whose goals are externally fixed, which is the weights or the training process. Butlin and Viebahn's descriptive-function test applies to a pre-trained or fine-tuned model. Williams and Bayne's proto-assertion applies to a stable conversational persona. Proxy accounts apply to the product and its deployer. Attah's thin intention applies to the Conceptualizer–Formulator pipeline inside a system. The accounts look like rivals only because each says 'LLM'. Cross-checking each verdict against each candidate shows the verdicts largely converge, with only a small residue of real disagreement. The residue is whether a persona can be a speaker without a persisting substrate. The premise the argument turns on is that no account in the literature fixes its candidate speaker independently of the conclusion it wants.",
    "target": "The shared assumption that there is one thing, 'the LLM', whose speech-act status is being disputed, as in Gubelmann, Williams and Bayne, Skiba, and Green and Michel.",
    "risk": "Defenders will say the referent is clearly the deployed chatbot-as-experienced, so the disputes are not verbal. The 'residue' might also turn out to be most of the dispute."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Whether a chatbot can apologise or promise is not settled by its agency or intentions. It is settled by whether anything persists through time that can be held to the commitment, and since that is a fact about instance individuation and memory architecture, the agency debate is aimed at the wrong target.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Magnus et al. deny chatbot apology for want of linguistic and moral agency, and Attah and Butlin dispute agency. Yet apologies and promises are commissive or reparative: their felicity conditions include a forward-looking liability borne by an identifiable party. Grant the chatbot Butlin-style agency and even full communicative intention. A fresh context window, a swapped model version, or a stateless API call still leaves no one who owes anything tomorrow. Conversely, a deployer with a persistent, updated system could satisfy the diachronic condition even if the model lacks agency. Puzio's emphasis on materiality (devices, instances, infrastructure) supplies the relevant variable: what is materially continuous with what. The argument turns on the premise that, for commissives and apologies, bearer-persistence rather than intention-possession is the binding felicity condition. It predicts that memory-equipped agents are better apologisers than more agentic but stateless ones.",
    "target": "Magnus, Buccella and D'Cruz, and the agency-centred framing shared by the denial and attribution camps.",
    "risk": "Persistence may be the very thing moral agency is meant to deliver, so this could collapse into the agency view. Humans also apologise without any enforcement mechanism."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Whether LLMs assert is not a discovery about their nature. It is a question about whether a community should institute sanction-and-uptake practices that confer speaker status, so the 'metaphysical' debate is a disguised policy question.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Butlin and Viebahn make assertion depend on being sanctionable, and Skiba extends this to speech acts generally. That is already a relational, social-status condition of the kind Puzio's network view favours. On such conditions, the status is partly constituted by the practices of the surrounding network: who answers for outputs, whether corrections feed back, whether users hold the system to account. Current failure of the sanction condition is therefore contingent and revisable by design and institution, as with corporate persons. Disputes about intentions, organisms and proto-assertion then turn out to be attempts to find in the machine an intrinsic ground for what the practice would confer. The argument turns on the premise that assertion's norm-governed status is conferred, not detected. If that is right, the live question is the ethical one of whether to confer it, and Puzio's point about distributed responsibility bears on that.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn, Skiba and Gubelmann, who treat speaker status as something a system either has or lacks.",
    "risk": "Conferral may need prior capacities, such as responsiveness to sanction, that are intrinsic. Butlin and Viebahn may reply that they already say this."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The proxy, fictionalist, quasi-assertion and denial views disagree mainly about where in the human–LLM exchange to locate the illocutionary uptake that completes a speech act, and so dispute the grain of the speech-act unit rather than the machine's mind.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "On Austin's and Hornsby's picture, an illocution is secured partly by uptake from the hearer. Barandiaran and Almendros's 'midtended' agency and Puzio's networks both suggest that outputs become acts only inside the coupling. Treat uptake as constitutive. Mallory's fictionalism puts the act in the user's make-believe, proxy views put it in the vendor's earlier intention, and Williams and Bayne's proto-assertion marks an act that is partly formed. All of these partition a single user-involving event differently. The question 'does the model have communicative intentions?' presupposes that the speaker-side contribution is separable and the unit is the utterance. If the unit is the exchange, then the model's intentions are not what is at stake, and the rival theories become bookkeeping conventions over one event. The premise is that uptake is constitutive, not merely evidential, of illocutionary force.",
    "target": "The shared assumption, in Gubelmann, Attah and Green and Michel alike, that speech-act performance is a speaker-side matter settled by speaker-side states.",
    "risk": "The constitutive-uptake thesis is contested even for humans, and it makes speech acts hostage to hearers' misreadings. It may only relabel the proxy and fictionalist views."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Attah's view that a model's communicative intentions are graded and have their purposes supplied by its design context commits him to locating those intentions in the model-plus-deployment assemblage rather than in the model, so the 'attributing' view collapses into a relational view of the kind Puzio defends.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Attah concedes that the purposes behind language-model speech are 'mediated by human designers' and 'supplied by the design context'. On his control conception, an intention is an internal state that controls the system's dynamics so that it acts in a situation that calls for action. If the system-level purpose is fixed by system prompts, fine-tuning targets, tool scaffolds and the deployer's objectives, then the state doing the controlling is not fixed by the weights. The same weights under different scaffolds have different controlling states and so different intentions. The bearer of the intention is therefore the weights-plus-context assemblage, which is Puzio's network unit, trimmed down. Attah cannot say the intention is in the model without losing the design-context clause. If he says it is in the assemblage, his view is a relocating view with a Gricean label. His own closing admission that the real difference lies in 'mental and metalinguistic agency' then looks like a symptom of this collapse. The key premise is that purposes supplied from outside the controlling mechanism are not properties of that mechanism.",
    "target": "Attah 2025 (graded functional intention), and the assumed separation between attributing and relocating views",
    "risk": "Attah can individuate the intention by the in-context controlling state inside the model, such as the activations that encode the prompt-derived goal. That keeps the bearer inside the model while the content is externally caused, as it is for any human whose goals are socially acquired."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Butlin and Viebahn's sanctionability condition on assertion, if it is read synchronically (the speaker must be sanctionable by interlocutors at the time of use), commits them to denying that human assertoric competence is itself the product of sanction. Read diachronically, it is met by RLHF and annotator labour.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Puzio's emphasis on the material conditions of production, including annotators, raters and companies, makes the training pipeline part of the system. Butlin and Viebahn say fine-tuned models may meet the descriptive-function condition but fail the sanction condition, because no one can sanction the model the way a hearer sanctions a speaker. But the pipeline is a sanction practice in which accuracy is rewarded and falsehood penalised, and the penalties change the system's dispositions to assert. Humans acquire assertoric norms by being corrected, rebuked and distrusted as children, not by being sanctioned in the moment of speaking. If sanctionability means being a possible target of repeated, behaviour-shaping, norm-keyed responses, the network satisfies it in training. If it means in-the-moment responsiveness, the condition excludes the toddler and the habitually unrepentant liar, and it misdescribes assertion. The key premise is that what matters for the practice is the sanction's uptake in the speaker's disposition, not the speaker's presence in the sanctioning loop at utterance time.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn 2025, and Skiba's extension of the norm-based argument",
    "risk": "Butlin and Viebahn can say sanction must be addressed to the speaker as the author of a particular act. Gradient updates are not addressed to anyone and do not target the act, so they are conditioning rather than sanction."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Anyone who grants a language model (or its network) the ability to promise or apologise is committed to individuating the speaker by the transcript or context window, since promissory and apologetic commitments need a persisting subject, and the weights do not persist as the subject of any particular conversation.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Puzio's network view makes the actor a changing assemblage of weights, devices, prompts and users, with no fixed individual. Promising and apologising require a persisting party who can be held to a commitment or who owes repair. The weights are shared across thousands of simultaneous instances and carry no memory between sessions. So the only candidate persisting subject is the session, meaning the context-bearing run, or the memory-augmented agent. This is a commitment that attributing views (Attah, Butlin on language agents) and relational views (Puzio) share but have not noticed. They must say that the promiser is a thing that comes into being with the transcript and ends when the context is cleared, and that the promise is discharged or voided by deletion. Pre-trained-model proponents who say 'ChatGPT apologised' are then wrong about who apologised. The key premise is that illocutions with future-directed or retrospective normative consequences require a speaker-identity condition that extends beyond the utterance.",
    "target": "Puzio 2025 and the attributing views that treat 'the model' or 'the network' as the speaker",
    "risk": "Human speakers have fragmentary and reconstructed diachronic identity, and institutional speakers persist through records. The commitment may then be harmless, just an acceptance of corporate-style persistence through records."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Puzio's distribution of agency across a network of human and non-human actors, when applied to apology, commits her to the view that chatbot apologies are institutional acts like a corporate statement of regret, and that apology therefore requires no remorse-bearing locus. That concedes the 'bullshit' verdict as a description and rejects the moral-agency premise that Magnus, Buccella and D'Cruz apply to humans.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Puzio wants ethical attention to move from the single user or model to the network of workers, firms and technologies. If the chatbot's 'I'm sorry' is an act of that network, then it resembles an organisation's apology, which is issued by no one in particular and is not felt by anyone. Either such acts are genuine apologies, in which case remorse and an individual moral agent are not necessary for apology and Magnus et al.'s requirement fails for humans and machines alike. Or they are not, in which case network agency does not suffice for the speech acts that matter morally. The relational view cannot keep both its flat symmetry and a notion of apology that needs a node that can be sorry. Corporate apologies are the test case, and the dispute over whether they are real apologies is the dispute over whether network agency is enough. The key premise is that no node in the network satisfies the felt-responsibility condition by itself.",
    "target": "Puzio 2025 (ethics reoriented toward networks), and the shared assumption that apology requires an individual locus of moral agency",
    "risk": "Puzio can accept the first horn without cost. Many theorists already hold that institutional apologies are real and that remorse is not essential to apology, so the conclusion may be unsurprising or may not be hers."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Butlin and Viebahn's sanctionability condition has two readings, sanction as imposed cost on a stakeholder and sanction as corrective shaping of dispositions, and on either reading their verdict that fine-tuned models fail the condition is unstable.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "'Capable of being sanctioned' can mean (a) capable of being made worse off in a way the speaker cares about, as with reputation loss, guilt or exclusion, or (b) capable of having future output dispositions modified in response to norm-violation. RLHF, correction within a conversation and retraining plainly satisfy (b), and models fine-tuned for correctness can have the reward indexed to truth, so on (b) the condition is cheap and some chatbots assert. On (a) the condition requires welfare or stakes, and the argument then reduces to the organismic denial (Gubelmann) or the moral-agency denial (Magnus et al.), so the 'norm-governed practice' argument does no independent work. The key premise is that the explanatory role sanctions play in constituting a norm-governed practice is served by (b) alone. Humans' felt cost is one implementation of the shaping function and is not what makes the practice normative. The two readings reclassify the debate: Butlin and Viebahn become either liberals about fine-tuned chatbots or organismic denialists with extra steps.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's second condition, and the assumption that the normative-grounds denial is independent of the organismic denial.",
    "risk": "They may reply that the sanction must be norm-indexed, so that the system is sensitive to being sanctioned as a violator of an assertoric norm and not merely to negative reward. That would be a third reading that survives the dilemma."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The descriptive-function condition is ambiguous between producer-side and consumer-side function, and on the consumer-side reading the line between pre-trained and fine-tuned models disappears or inverts.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "Butlin and Viebahn argue that a pre-trained model fails because its training objective is next-token prediction, which is not a descriptive function, whereas models fine-tuned for groundedness may pass. That locates function in what the producing system was selected to do. On Millikan-style teleosemantics, though, the content-bearing function of a representation is fixed by what consuming systems do with it, and a consumer-side function can be stabilised by users who systematically rely on the output as information, independent of the producer's training. The key premise is that assertion's function is a consumer-side relation, since it consists in licensing uptake of belief. If so, the pre-trained/fine-tuned divide tracks the wrong variable, and what matters is the stabilised pattern of reliance in a deployment. This also reclassifies the proxy view. Consumer-side function is the one thing the proxy account and the chatbot-assertion account share, and so it is unclear they are rivals.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's first condition and their asymmetric treatment of pre-trained versus fine-tuned models.",
    "risk": "Consumer-side stabilisation may be too cheap, since it would make horoscope generators and Magic 8-Balls descriptive. It also may not be what Butlin and Viebahn mean by 'function'."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The proxy account conflates two relations, endorsing a content and endorsing a generating policy, and only the second is available for LLM output, so proxy 'assertion' is a different act from assertion and is governed by a reliability norm.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "A human proxy such as a spokesperson or a translator delivers content the principal has authorised, or content whose range the principal can foresee. A deployer of an LLM authorises a policy that outputs an open-ended range of contents it has never seen and could not have vetted. The key premise is that assertion commits the speaker to the particular content p, so that she can be asked to defend it and incurs retraction obligations if p is false. Under policy-endorsement the deployer is committed to the policy's reliability and not to each output, and she may even hold that some outputs will be false. The act the deployer performs is then more like publishing a reference work or certifying an instrument than asserting. Its norm is a statistical or reliability norm, not the knowledge norm. Butlin and Viebahn's remark that AI can produce 'proxy assertions' therefore smuggles in content-endorsement, and Green–Michel and Arora inherit the same problem. The view reclassifies proxy accounts as a fourth category, neither assertion by the model nor assertion by the human.",
    "target": "Proxy accounts (Green and Michel, Arora, and the proxy fallback in Butlin and Viebahn).",
    "risk": "Defenders may say that human assertion by testimonial chains and corporate statements is already policy-endorsement, or that deployers implicitly endorse each output by dispositional commitment."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Sanctionability does not test whether a model is a speaker but fixes which entity is the candidate speaker, and that entity is a persona-lineage, neither the model nor the deployer, so the model-versus-proxy dichotomy is false.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Sanctions are applied to entities that persist across assertions: reputation accrues, retractions are tracked and trust is withdrawn. For an LLM deployment, users do not sanction a sampled forward pass, the weight file or the company, but 'Claude' or 'ChatGPT-4o' as a named, behaviourally stable character that is updated by feedback. The question 'can the model be sanctioned?' presupposes that the candidate is the weights. The key premise is that speaker identity for norm-governed practice is individuated by the sanction-and-credit practice itself, as with pseudonymous authors or institutions, and not by physical or metaphysical substrate. On this view the assistant persona is the entity to which assertoric commitments attach, and it can meet Butlin and Viebahn's conditions with no appeal to the model's own mind or to a human proxy. Fictionalism and the proxy view both then misdescribe a practice that already has a non-human, non-fictional speaker.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's framing of the candidate asserter, and the model/proxy/fiction trichotomy generally.",
    "risk": "A persona may be only a pattern in users' attributions, so the view is fictionalism by another name, or the persona collapses into the deployer's brand, which is the proxy view."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Some speech acts performed through LLM agents, such as 'Claim denied', 'Refund approved' or 'Access revoked' issued inside an institutional workflow, succeed without any intention from either the model or a human proxy, so the shared premise that every speech act needs an intending performer is false.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Denials (Gubelmann, Skiba, Butlin and Viebahn) and relocating views (proxy accounts) disagree about who the intender is, but they agree that someone must have the communicative intention or objective. Institutional declarations are a counterexample. Under electronic-agent doctrines in contract law, an automated system's 'offer' or 'acceptance' binds the operator even when no person formed any objective about this particular case. Austin's conventional performatives work by role and rule, not by speaker psychology. An LLM occupying a defined office in a network of rules, which is what Puzio's relocation of agency to actor-networks suggests, performs the declaration because the office's uptake conditions are met. The proxy account must posit a human objective directed at the specific act, and in these cases there is none. The key premise is that the force of a declaration is fixed by institutional rule and uptake, not by anyone's mental state. So Skiba's extension from assertion to all speech acts fails for declaratives, and the question 'who intended it?' has a false presupposition for this class of acts.",
    "target": "The shared presupposition of proxy accounts (Green and Michel, Arora) and no-speech-act views (Skiba, Gubelmann) that speech-act performance needs an intending performer, whether the machine or a human.",
    "risk": "Defenders will say that a conditional or standing human objective ('deny claims meeting criteria X') covers these cases, so they reduce to Green and Michel's conditional proxy acts. The thesis then needs a case where the human objective is silent or contrary to the outcome and the act is still in force."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Identical LLM computation can be the Formulator of a human's communicative intention in one deployment and the whole speaker in another, so speakerhood is not an intrinsic functional property of the system, and intrinsic-property views such as Attah's control conception, Gubelmann's autonomy and Butlin's agency criterion cannot settle the question.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Attah maps LLMs onto Levelt's blueprint (Conceptualizer, Formulator, Articulator) and argues the pipeline suggests thin communicative intentions. Take LLM-based augmentative communication systems used by people with ALS. The user supplies a few words or selects a stance, and the model expands them into fluent sentences that the user then sends. The same weights and computation that in a chatbot count as an 'intention-controlled pipeline' here plainly serve as the Formulator for the human's intention, and the speech act belongs to the user. Levelt's template describes a subsystem of a speaker, so fit to the template cannot be sufficient for being a speaker. Both denials and attributions assume that whether the LLM is a speaker depends on properties of the LLM. The counterexample shows that the same computational properties yield different answers depending on where the intention-source sits. The key premise is that the AAC user and the chatbot user cases are internally indistinguishable at the level the control conception, Gubelmann's test and Butlin's criterion operate on. Speakerhood is then a relational fact about where in the network the controlling state originates, which is a result Puzio's relational view predicts but does not argue for in this domain.",
    "target": "Attah's control conception and, more generally, the shared assumption that the dispute concerns intrinsic properties of the model (intention, autonomy, instrumental-value sensitivity).",
    "risk": "A defender can reply that the AAC case shows only that a system can be a part, while a chatbot has the whole pipeline internally. Showing that 'whole pipeline' is not an intrinsic property, since the Conceptualizer role is performed by the prompt in both cases, carries most of the argument."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Butlin and Viebahn's requirement that an asserter be capable of being sanctioned by those it interacts with is already met by chatbot personas, on exactly the terms on which pseudonymous human assertors meet it, so it cannot exclude LLM assertion.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "The sanction condition is read as requiring a persisting individual who can bear costs. Counterexample: an anonymous or pseudonymous poster, or a corporate account, asserts. The only sanctions available land on the handle (ban, loss of reputation, deletion), not on any body or person the interlocutor can reach. Handle-level sanction is nonetheless taken to sustain the assertoric norm among participants. A chatbot persona such as 'Claude' or 'Gemini' has the same structure. Users withhold trust, downvote, and report it. Developers retrain, patch or deprecate the persona, a material intervention on the weights that changes future output in the way sanction is meant to. Puzio's point that agency and consequences run through material networks of workers, companies and devices suggests that sanctions on the persona are real. The key premise is that the function of sanctionability is the maintenance of reliability under social pressure, not suffering by a subject. Even a thin version fails the counter-reply that retraining isn't felt, since a human handle ban isn't felt by the handle either.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's second condition, and Skiba's norm-based argument that inherits it.",
    "risk": "They may insist that human pseudonymous sanction ultimately bottoms out in a person who cares, so the analogy is a disguised appeal to a human behind the handle. The thesis then needs a handle case with no reachable human, such as a dead author's persisting persona."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Performing a speech act on behalf of a principal does not exclude performing it as one's own, as every discretionary human delegate shows, so proxy accounts do not relocate authorship away from the model and their rivalry with attributing views is spurious.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Proxy accounts (Green and Michel, Arora) and fictionalism are offered as alternatives to the model being the speaker, and Williams and Bayne reject proxy accounts on that rivalry. Counterexample: a hospital physician advises a patient on behalf of the hospital, an ambassador asserts for the state, and a lawyer states a position for a client. In each the delegate exercises wide discretion over content, so the delegate's advice and the institution's advice are one act, and neither is the real speaker with the other a fiction. Green and Michel's cases (recordings, fixed conditionals) have almost no discretion, which is why the proxy reading looks exclusive there. LLM outputs involve enormous discretion because deployers cannot foresee content. Puzio's network picture generalises this, since each node's act is both its own and the network's. On the human model, high discretion plus proxy status yields dual authorship. If proxy status is compatible with own authorship, then the proxy account does not by itself deny the model's speech acts, and the live question becomes whether the model's discretion is of the kind that grounds its own authorship. That reduces the proxy-versus-attribution dispute to a dispute about discretion, which Butlin's instrumental-value account and Attah's control conception already address.",
    "target": "The assumption in Williams and Bayne, Arora, Green and Michel and Butlin and Viebahn that proxy accounts and attributing accounts are rival answers to 'who is the speaker?'",
    "risk": "Defenders will say human delegates have their own independent standing as speakers, so the case presupposes what is at issue for LLMs. Even so, the thesis would show that proxy status alone does no work and the whole weight rests on the delegate's independent status."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "The sanctionability condition on assertion is falsified by humans with profound anterograde amnesia, who still assert; the only reading that spares them is within-interaction answerability, which an in-context LLM meets, so the condition cannot separate current chatbots from human asserters.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Butlin and Viebahn require that an asserter be capable of being sanctioned by the agents it interacts with. They fail fine-tuned models on this because criticism or reward does not reach the model in a way that changes its later conduct or standing. Take a Korsakoff or severe anterograde-amnesia patient. Rebukes cannot change her later conduct, and she will not remember them tomorrow, yet when she tells the nurse 'your car is in lot B' she asserts, and she can be challenged, can retract, and can be said to have been wrong. Whatever sanctionability her case preserves is local: she can be called to account, can acknowledge error, and can have her standing in the current exchange altered. If the condition is read as requiring durable behavioural or reputational consequences, it excludes her, which is absurd. If it is read as local answerability, then a chatbot that in-context takes a challenge, withdraws or corrects the claim, and changes how it speaks for the rest of the conversation meets it. The premise the argument turns on is that persistence of sanction effects across episodes is not constitutive of assertoric standing. The condition then either overgeneralises to amnesiacs or fails to exclude chatbots.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's second condition (sanctionability), and the shared assumption of denials and proxy views that persistence of consequences is what makes speakers accountable.",
    "risk": "They can say the amnesiac is sanctionable through her social role, body and legal personhood even if the particular rebuke does not stick, and that nothing parallel holds for a model. The case may then relocate sanctionability from the speaker's psychology to a wider social embedding, which chatbots lack."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The 'descriptive function' condition faces a dilemma: if function is etiological, a Swampman-style molecular duplicate of a human speaker fails to assert; if function is current-dispositional, pre-trained base models acquire descriptive function from the prompt and so already pass the condition they are said to fail.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Butlin and Viebahn hold that pre-trained, non-fine-tuned models fail the condition that outputs have descriptive functions, because next-token prediction is not selected for truth or representation of the world. This presupposes a selected-effects notion of function. Apply it to a Swampman speaker who has just coalesced, has no selection history, and says 'the bridge is out'. Everyone grants that Swampman asserts, yet on the etiological reading nothing about his outputs has a descriptive function, so the condition is not necessary for assertion. The alternative is a dispositional or systemic notion of function, on which an output has a descriptive function if the system's present organisation makes it covary with and be regulated by how things are. A base model conditioned on a context ('You are a weather service. Report today's conditions: ...') has dispositions of that kind, since its outputs are systematically sensitive to the document-type and factual context. The premise the argument turns on is that assertoric function must be fixed by something other than history, so the cases that matter turn on current organisation, where base models are not obviously excluded. The result is that the pre-trained/fine-tuned line, which does much work in the paper, is not principled.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's verdict that pre-trained models fail the descriptive-function condition, and the shared assumption that function-talk can be imported into assertion without settling its metaphysics.",
    "risk": "They could adopt a hybrid (proper function in the system or its lineage, with Swampman as a tolerated exception, or learning-history functions), on which training counts as selection and base models still fail because their training target is text-likeness. The dilemma then dissolves into a dispute over what training selects for."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Butlin and Viebahn's two conditions are not jointly sufficient, because a templated weather-report generator that can be sanctioned (delisted, banned, replaced) meets both and does not assert; so the conditions leave open the question they are meant to settle for LLMs.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Their conditions are stated as necessary, and the paper's strategy is to show current systems fail one of them. But the exercise only matters if the conditions are close to sufficient, since otherwise passing them would not show that an LLM asserts. Consider a rule-based bot that generates stubs about species, or a weather-text generator. Its outputs have a clear descriptive function (they are produced to report states of affairs and are corrected when wrong). It is sanctioned by those it interacts with: editors ban it, an app store removes it, subscribers cancel. By the paper's own criteria it meets both conditions, yet almost nobody, including the authors presumably, thinks the template engine asserts. If they accept that it asserts, assertion is cheap and the question about chatbots loses its philosophical interest. If they reject it, something further is needed (understanding, commitment, a content-sensitive stance towards what is said), and that extra element is exactly what the debate about intentions and agency concerns. The premise the argument turns on is that the sanctions in play are exerted on the system as a thing to be tuned or removed, and that tuning of this kind is not the same as holding someone to account. The paper's normative strand therefore either collapses into the agency debate it seeks to bypass or trivialises assertion.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's attempt to settle AI assertion by social-practice conditions without a commitment to intention or agency; also Skiba's extension of the norm-based argument.",
    "risk": "The authors may explicitly say the conditions are only necessary and that a third condition (e.g. assertoric intention or appropriate capacities) is left to other work. In that case this is only a point about scope, and the bot may be treated as a harmless proto-asserter."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The shared assumption that a machine utterance is either the machine's assertion or a proxy assertion by some human is false: there are orphan outputs that no human has adopted, authorised or even seen, which satisfy the conditions for being content that asserting would regulate and yet have no asserter.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Butlin and Viebahn conclude that where the AI does not assert, AI systems can still be used to produce proxy assertions on behalf of human agents. Proxy accounts (Green and Michel, Arora) likewise treat deployers as the speaker. Proxy assertion, on the standard analyses, requires that someone has adopted, authorised or delegated the output, or at least can be held to its content. Consider an open-weights agent loop that a hobbyist launched months ago, which now posts claims about drug interactions in forums, answers other agents' queries, and drafts replies that nobody reviews. The deployer never saw this content, did not specify it, and has no reasonable foreknowledge. The content is read and relied on by third parties, and the output fits the descriptive-function pattern. If the machine does not assert (by the denial view) and the deployer did not assert (no adoption, no uptake), then nobody asserted, yet hearers form testimony-like expectations and norms of reliance are triggered. The premise the argument turns on is that the proxy relation cannot be stretched to cover unreviewed, unforeseen content without making proxy assertion equivalent to mere causal responsibility. The result is an assertion gap which denial and proxy views cannot accommodate, and this pushes toward either machine assertion or a distinct category of non-assertoric but assertion-governed speech.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's fallback to proxy assertion, and proxy views generally (Green and Michel; Arora).",
    "risk": "Proxy theorists may accept thin, strict-liability adoption (whoever releases the system adopts all its outputs, as with a newspaper publisher), so the gap closes and the case is just a hard case for proxy theory, not a counterexample to the dichotomy."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Butlin and Viebahn's verdicts on whether 'the model' asserts are unstable because the candidate speaker is never fixed. Weights, a running instance, a persona, and the deployed model-plus-scaffold system each pass or fail the descriptive-function and sanction conditions differently, so the denial, proxy and attribution camps are largely answering different questions.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "'AI Assertion' tests 'pre-trained models', 'fine-tuned models' and 'AI systems' against its two conditions as if the bearer were obvious. But sanction can land on a token conversation (the user refuses to trust it again), on a persona type (a brand's assistant loses reputation), on weights (retraining), or on the vendor-system. Descriptive function attaches at yet another grain: the base model as a simulator of many authors, or one character. Fix the persona as the speaker and sanction is plausibly satisfied through reputation and retraining. Fix the weights and it is not. Fix the system and the vendor is the speaker, which is the proxy view. So the apparent disagreement between 'LLMs cannot assert', 'they assert by proxy' and 'they quasi-assert' is a disagreement about individuating the speaker, not about speech-act theory. The thesis turns on the premise that the two conditions are relational and grain-sensitive, so the verdict changes with the individuation.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's treatment of 'the model' as the bearer, and the shared assumption across the four groups that there is a single candidate author.",
    "risk": "Butlin and Viebahn may reply that they already consider the cases (base model, fine-tuned model, system) separately. The individuation point then looks like a refinement rather than a reclassification of the positions."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "In Butlin and Viebahn's own framework, 'fails the sanction condition' and 'only proxy-asserts' describe the same fact, namely that every sanction aimed at the system terminates in the deployer. The disagreement between the denial and proxy camps is therefore verbal, and fixing that does not rescue the machine as a speaker.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Butlin and Viebahn say fine-tuned models may satisfy the descriptive-function condition but fail the sanction condition, while AI outputs can still be proxy assertions for humans. Ask what makes sanctioning a proper target for the assertor. Blame, loss of credibility and redress must be able to bear on the entity whose future conduct they are meant to regulate. For current systems the only party whose conduct is regulated in a way that answers to the sanction is the vendor or deployer (policy changes, liability, retraining decisions). So the facts that make the machine non-sanctionable are the facts that make the human the asserter, and 'denies assertion' and 'relocates assertion' are one claim under two descriptions. The turning premise is that sanction-targeting is what fixes authorship in a norm-governed practice. If so, the live question is whether the sanction-bearing locus could shift to the system, not whether to call the present case 'proxy' or 'none'.",
    "target": "The division between Denials and Relocating views, especially Butlin and Viebahn's two-step structure (no machine assertion, but proxy assertion).",
    "risk": "Proxy accounts like Green and Michel's rest on objectives and speaker meaning rather than sanction, so the equivalence may hold only for Butlin and Viebahn's version. The collapse is then local, not a general reclassification."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "RLHF-style training is already an institutionalised sanction practice, so Butlin and Viebahn's second condition is met or not depending on whether assertion norms are enforced by selection pressure (feedback that shapes future output) or by Strawsonian reactive attitudes (resentment and accountability). The LLM question is a proxy for that older dispute about what makes a norm an assertion norm.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Thumbs-down, preference rankings and corrections are feedback by an interacting community that is explicitly aimed at making outputs more truthful, and the system's dispositions change in response. Under a functional, selection-based notion of sanction, 'capable of being sanctioned by the agents it interacts with' is then plausibly satisfied. Butlin and Viebahn resist this because they hear 'sanction' as requiring something like blame or loss of standing that matters to the sanctioned party. That thicker reading is a substantive thesis about assertion: its norm is enforced through reactive attitudes directed at responsible agents, not through any error-correcting feedback. Williamson-style constitutive-rule views and Brandomian scorekeeping views of assertion would diverge on exactly this point regardless of LLMs. The turning premise is that 'capable of being sanctioned' is ambiguous between corrective and reactive sanction, and the ambiguity is where the work gets done. The debate is therefore about the metaphysics of assertion, with LLMs serving as the test case.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's second necessary condition, and the assumption that the LLM debate depends mainly on facts about LLMs.",
    "risk": "Butlin and Viebahn could reply that they intend a thicker notion all along. The reframing would then only expose a hidden premise and not change the verdict. Reinforcement is also arguably not 'sanction' in any ordinary sense."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The dispute over whether pre-trained models meet the descriptive-function condition is not about language models at all. It turns on whether functions are inherited by reproduction (Millikan's derived proper functions) or must be selected for in the system itself. On the first theory the pre-trained model's outputs already have descriptive function, and Butlin and Viebahn's first condition does not discriminate between base and fine-tuned models.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Butlin and Viebahn hold that pre-trained models fail the first condition: next-token prediction selects for fit to a text distribution, not for descriptive function, and only groundedness or correctness fine-tuning might supply it. But the corpus consists largely of assertions, and the training process selects for reproducing those assertions' patterns. On Millikan's theory, items reproduced from a model inherit the model's proper function through the reproductive lineage, as a copied sentence inherits the function of the sentence it copies. The base model's outputs would then have the descriptive function of their sources, even though the system was not selected for truth. If this is rejected and only direct selection counts, then much human assertion, learned by imitation, would also fail. The first condition's verdict therefore depends on the grain and etiology theory of function, and the base/fine-tuned contrast loses its principled status. The turning premise is that Butlin and Viebahn's function notion is etiological but silent on derived versus direct selection.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's claim that non-fine-tuned pre-trained models fail the descriptive-function condition.",
    "risk": "Derived function is a poor fit for a generative system that produces novel strings, where nothing is copied. Butlin and Viebahn may also say the relevant function must belong to the output-producing system, which would block inheritance."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Butlin and Viebahn's sanctionability condition has no stable reading between 'sensitive to feedback' and 'capable of being harmed or held to account': on the first reading fine-tuned chatbots already meet it and the view collapses into the attributing camp, and on the second the descriptive-function condition does no work and the view collapses into the organismic denial.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Butlin and Viebahn say assertion requires that the asserter can be sanctioned by those it interacts with, and that fine-tuned models fail this. Ask what sanction must do to the sanctioned party. (a) Thin reading: sanction is a negative signal that changes the party's later dispositions to assert. User downvotes, corrections, in-context rebukes and RLHF updates all do this, so a model with memory or online feedback is sanctionable in exactly this sense. The condition then becomes an engineering variable, and the view agrees with Butlin's own agency criterion, which counts sensitivity to feedback as agency, and with Attah's graded attribution. (b) Thick reading: sanction must bite on something the party has a stake in, such as reputation, welfare or standing. That condition is satisfied by organisms or moral patients and not by systems with imposed goals. It is then the real work-doing condition, and the descriptive-function condition is idle. This is Gubelmann's and Magnus et al.'s position in different clothes. The premise the argument turns on is that no intermediate notion of sanction is both non-trivially unmet by current models and independent of having a stake. Without such a notion, 'norm-governed social practice' fails to yield a distinct third position.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's claim that fine-tuned models may satisfy the function condition yet fail sanctionability, as a stable middle between denial and attribution.",
    "risk": "A middle notion may exist, such as sanction that operates through addressee-directed social uptake of the speaker as a persisting individual. Persistent identity could then be the independent factor, though that begins to look like the individuality condition from the enactive literature."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The 'descriptive function' that Butlin and Viebahn require of an asserter's outputs is, for any fine-tuned LLM, conferred by developers' and raters' selection, so a model that satisfies it is already a proxy for its trainers, and their non-proxy route to AI assertion collapses into their proxy route.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "They distinguish (i) the model itself asserting, which needs outputs with a descriptive function, from (ii) humans asserting through the model by proxy. For a pre-trained model the function is next-token prediction, so it fails (i). A fine-tuned model acquires a descriptive or correctness function only because annotators, reward models and designers select outputs for accuracy. This is an etiological function with human purposes in its history, and in that respect it differs from nothing in Green and Michel's proxy picture. The structure is: the output's descriptive function is a derivative function, derivative functions are borrowed from the selector's aims, and speech acts performed through a borrowed aim are proxy acts. So the model-level condition (i) is met only in the way that (ii) is met, and the two options differ by redescription. The principle the argument turns on is that a function conferred wholly by others' selection cannot ground the system's own speech-act status without also making the selectors the speakers. To resist, a defender must say what makes derived functions count for humans (social-evolutionary selection of assertion) but not for models. That answer either re-imports sanctioning, or concedes that derived function suffices.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's two-route structure (own assertion vs. proxy assertion) and the function condition's independence from the proxy account.",
    "risk": "Humans' assertoric functions are also externally conferred by upbringing and social selection, so the argument may prove too much; the reply would be that human function is internalised and self-maintained."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Proxy assertion, offered by Butlin and Viebahn as the safe harbour for AI speech, is unstable: when the deployer neither knows nor could have vetted the content, the deployer's relation to the output is publication liability, not assertion, so the proxy view collapses into the no-speech-act view.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "On their own account assertion is a norm-governed practice whose performer is answerable for the content. When a proxy asserts for a principal, the principal commits to p. Traditionally this works because the principal authorised or vetted the proxy's content, as with a spokesperson reading a script or a lawyer filing a brief. An LLM deployer authorises a policy and not particular contents. At the token level the content is generated after authorisation, is unforeseeable, and is often unreviewed. Responsibility for such outputs is strict, product-style responsibility, as with a publisher or a vending-machine owner, and it is not speaker commitment. The premise the argument turns on is that assertoric commitment requires the committed party to have some epistemic access to the content, such as the ability to appreciate it as a claim about how things are. If the premise holds, then in the cases that matter (open-ended generation) nobody asserts: the model fails the conditions and the human is only liable. This yields a precise stable position, 'liability without speech act', that neither Butlin and Viebahn nor the proxy theorists (Green and Michel, Arora) allow. The proxy option then offers no real alternative to denial.",
    "target": "The proxy-assertion fallback in Butlin and Viebahn, and by extension Green and Michel's and Arora's accounts.",
    "risk": "Conditional and standing-commitment speech acts (such as signing a blank cheque or 'I stand behind whatever this bot says') seem to give the deployer genuine commitment without content access, which would undercut the premise."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "If descriptive function is etiological, a fine-tuned model's false outputs are malfunctions rather than false assertions, so the 'norm-governed practice' view of assertion collapses into a design-specification view and loses the thing it was meant to add.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Butlin and Viebahn locate the failure of pre-trained models in lacking descriptive functions, and suggest fine-tuning for groundedness or correctness might supply them. Suppose the function is supplied by training as selection for truth-tracking. A hallucination is then an output that fails its function, which is a malfunction in the system and not a violation of an assertoric norm by a speaker. For a human, a false assertion is a norm-violating act that is nonetheless an assertion. For the model, the same condition makes the false output either not an assertion (because it lacks the function) or an assertion that is also a defect. In the first case the model's assertoric status shrinks to its true outputs, so it can never assert falsely, which is absurd for a norm-governed practice. In the second case, the norm is just the designer's specification, and 'being held to the norm' is just being debugged. That is Gubelmann's mechanism-with-imposed-purposes picture, in which no difference between malfunction and deliberate wrongdoing can be drawn. The premise the argument turns on is that a function-based criterion of assertion must be indexed to type-level function and therefore cannot distinguish error from insincerity or violation at the level of the individual act. So the normative route to denial reduces to the organismic route, and the two do not independently support denial.",
    "target": "The claimed independence of the normative (Butlin–Viebahn, Skiba) denial from the organismic (Gubelmann) denial.",
    "risk": "Functions can be indexed to output classes or contexts, so a false output can be a failed assertion attempt (a defective assertion) without malfunction-talk, as with defective human assertions."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Because Butlin and Viebahn ground the sanctionability condition in assertion's being a norm-governed social practice, they are committed to assertoric status being partly response-dependent: if users and institutions sustainably treat chatbots as targets of reproach, correction and retraining, that practice helps make the sanctionability condition true, so whether chatbots assert is partly up to us and is not a fact to be read off the models.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Butlin and Viebahn treat the two conditions as discoverable properties of systems: pre-trained models fail the first, fine-tuned ones fail the second. But if assertion is constituted by a social practice, then 'capable of being sanctioned' is not a purely intrinsic capacity. It depends on whether participants have sanctioning practices that take hold of the system, and on whether the system's dispositions change in response. Users already scold, correct and downvote chatbots, and those signals feed back into training. The key premise is that in a constitutively social practice, membership conditions are at least partly fixed by the practice's own responses, as with who counts as a player, a promisor or a citizen. Add that sanction channels for models are engineering and policy choices, and the result is that a vendor, regulator or user community could settle the question by building the channel. Butlin and Viebahn reject, or at least do not accept, that metaphysical consequence. Their verdict that current models fail is meant as an empirical finding about the models. The social-practice premise they need makes it a contingent and revisable social fact.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's sanctionability condition, and the general assumption among denial views that speech-act capacity is an intrinsic property of the system.",
    "risk": "They can reply that sanction must be efficacious in a way that tracks the sanctioned party's own stakes. Users treating the model as sanctionable would then be a mere pretence, so the condition would not be response-dependent after all."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Butlin's own criterion of agency, together with Butlin and Viebahn's sanctionability condition, commits them to the view that assertion requires something close to desire, so assertion is more demanding than agency and more demanding than anything they say they need for the descriptive-function condition.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "In 'The agency in language agents', Butlin holds that an entity is an agent when it modifies itself in response to information about what conduces to a goal. Reflexion-style systems count unambiguously. On a thin reading, sanction is just negative feedback, which is information about what conduces to the goal, so any such agent that interacts with sanctioners is thinly sanctionable and would pass condition 2. To keep the conclusion that fine-tuned and agentic systems fail, 'sanction' must be read thickly: the sanction must matter to the system as a cost to it, which is a stake. But Butlin separately argues that stakes and goals of that kind require desire, and that desire demands practical reasoning answerable to conduciveness and an integrated, learned goal set. So the argument turns on one dilemma: either sanction is thin and some language agents already assert, or it is thick and assertion presupposes desire-like states that Butlin elsewhere says are doubtful for existing systems. That makes assertion a cognitively heavier act than agency. The authors present their two papers as modular, but they are not.",
    "target": "The joint package of Butlin (2024) and Butlin and Viebahn (2025), and more broadly the pairing of 'agent' with 'norm-governed participant'.",
    "risk": "A middle notion of sanction may exist, such as updating behaviour in response to disapproval as disapproval. That would be thicker than feedback but thinner than desire, and would block the dilemma."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "The 'descriptive function' condition, when carried over into the proxy-assertion fallback, makes a deployer's own speech-act status depend on a hidden engineering property of the tool (whether the model was fine-tuned for correctness), so Butlin and Viebahn are committed to a bizarre externalism about what humans themselves assert.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "Butlin and Viebahn say that pre-trained models produce no outputs with descriptive function, and that AI systems can nonetheless be used to produce proxy assertions for humans. The question is what makes a deployer's unreviewed chatbot outputs their assertions. The natural answer is that the deployer delegates a standing descriptive role to the system: 'whatever it says in the informative register, I stand behind.' But delegation of a descriptive function to something with none is incoherent, or at best merely nominal. If the delegation is real, it needs the tool's outputs to be reliably truth-directed, so a company deploying a fine-tuned model asserts and one deploying the same interface over a base model does not. Users, intentions, uptake and the company's liability are all identical in the two cases. The alternative is to say proxy assertion needs no descriptive function in the tool. Then the first condition is idle for exactly the cases where it matters, and the fallback becomes unconstrained. The key premise is that proxy speech acts inherit the functional conditions on the instrument. Proponents of the proxy view, including Green and Michel and Arora, would resist this.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's proxy-assertion fallback, and proxy accounts generally (Green and Michel, Arora).",
    "risk": "Proxy assertion may be grounded wholly in the deployer's commitments and uptake, with the tool as a mere channel, so the first condition never has to be inherited."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "If sanctionability by one's interlocutors is a condition on asserting, Butlin and Viebahn must either deny that anonymous, posthumous and mass-broadcast writers assert or relocate sanction to institutions, and the institutional reading dissolves their contrast between direct AI assertion and proxy assertion, because all written assertion then becomes proxy-like.",
    "move_type": "commitment",
    "sketch": "The condition says an asserter must be capable of being sanctioned by the agents it interacts with. Many human assertors cannot be sanctioned by their actual audience: the dead author, the pseudonymous poster, the wire-service writer, and the person whose words are published after they can no longer be reached. Reading the condition strictly makes these non-assertions, which is absurd. Reading it generously, as sanction through publishers, reputation systems, legal liability or platforms, is plausible. But then the thing sanctioned is a node in an institutional network, such as an imprint, a byline or a platform account, and not the individual speaker confronting an interlocutor. On that reading a chatbot's vendor, brand and model line are exactly such nodes, and the vendor is routinely sanctioned for outputs. The key premise is that institution-mediated sanction is the normal case for text. So either the condition is too strict for humans or it is already satisfied by the deployer-plus-model unit, and 'AI asserts versus AI is a proxy for a human asserter' stops being a clean contrast.",
    "target": "The sanctionability condition and its role in separating direct from proxy assertion in Butlin and Viebahn.",
    "risk": "They may stipulate that the relevant sanction is dispositional capacity in principle, which dead authors had when alive. That would handle posthumous cases but not anonymous or one-shot ones, and the dispute may collapse into counterexample-trading."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "The chatbot-apology literature runs together Austin's two kinds of infelicity, misfires (the act is not performed) and abuses (the act is performed hollowly), so the verdict 'chatbot apologies are bullshit' is incoherent with the agency-based argument offered for it.",
    "move_type": "instability",
    "sketch": "Magnus et al. argue that chatbots lack the linguistic and moral agency needed to apologise. That is a misfire claim: no apology occurs. They then classify the output as bullshit, which is an abuse category. Frankfurt's bullshit is an act of representing oneself as concerned, done with indifference to whether one is, so it presupposes that a speech act occurred and was insincere. The key premise is that 'bullshit' is act-classifying and not merely product-classifying. The position then forks. If the agency argument succeeds, the output is not bullshit, only noise that readers take for an apology. If the output is bullshit, the chatbot did something that can be abused, and the agency argument is too strong. Applied across the map, the denial views (Gubelmann, Skiba) are misfire theorists, while 'hollow speech' views are abuse theorists. Proto-assertion and quasi-testimony are best read as claims that there is no determinate fact between misfire and abuse. Seen this way, the dispute is over which infelicity category chatbot outputs fall into, and not over whether they are speech acts.",
    "target": "Magnus, Buccella and D'Cruz's bullshit verdict; the shared assumption (also in Skiba's discussion of bullshit) that 'no speech act' and 'bullshit' are compatible.",
    "risk": "The authors can say that 'bullshit' is meant in a product or soft sense (Hicks et al.'s 'soft bullshit'), which needs no act. Then the instability is only terminological."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Apology exposes a condition on speech-act performance that is independent of intention, agency and autonomy, namely diachronic answerable identity: the 'I' of 'I'm sorry' must refer to the same subject who did the wrong, and LLMs lack this even on the most generous theories of their agency.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "The debate asks whether LLMs have intentions, autonomy or sanctionability, which are all synchronic properties of an agent. Apologising, like promising, also requires that the speaker be the one to whom the earlier act is attributable and who will bear later commitments. A chatbot's 'I' ranges over a forkable, resettable, multiply instantiated process. The model weights, the session, the persona and the deployed product each have some claim to be the referent of 'I', and none is continuous with the earlier wrong in the way an apology needs. The key premise is a thought experiment. Take an agent that Butlin counts as fully agentive, with desires and instrumental sensitivity, but that is forked every turn and has no persisting liability-bearer. Its 'I'm sorry' still misfires. So there is a deficit that Gubelmann's organismic argument, Butlin's agency criterion and Attah's graded intention all miss, and one that proxy accounts handle only by moving the identity to a corporation. The positions then reclassify: the question for each speech act is which diachronic and identity conditions it needs, and agency is only one of the inputs.",
    "target": "The shared assumption that the speech-act question reduces to whether the system has intentions or agency (Gubelmann, Attah, Butlin, Barandiaran and Almendros).",
    "risk": "Persistent memory and fixed personas may supply a quasi-identity good enough for the act. The condition may also be shown to be part of moral agency, so that it is not a new distinction."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "An apology is a bundle of three separable components: an acknowledgment, a commitment to forbearance and an expression of remorse. Chatbots can plausibly perform the first two in-session, so 'can chatbots apologise?' lacks a single answer, and the remorse-based denial wrongly sets the standard for the whole act.",
    "move_type": "distinction",
    "sketch": "The denials treat apology as the paradigm of an act needing inner states, namely remorse and moral standing. Analyses of apology (Smith, Austin on expressives) distinguish an acknowledgment of the wrong (a constative-like commitment), an undertaking not to repeat it (commissive) and an expression of regret (expressive). Only the third needs a feeling. The first two can be satisfied by a system whose later in-context behaviour is answerable to what it said, and a chatbot that says 'I shouldn't have done X, I won't' and then conforms for the rest of the session satisfies them functionally. The key premise is that the commissive and acknowledgment components carry the apology's repair function, because recipients mostly want correction and not feelings. This reclassifies the views: Magnus et al. defend a remorse-centred conception, Butlin and Viebahn's normative analysis fits the commissive component, and Attah's functional intention fits the acknowledgment. Fictionalism and proxy accounts can be tested by asking which component each can reach. The result is a non-uniform verdict by component, which is different from a general pluralism about speech acts.",
    "target": "Magnus, Buccella and D'Cruz's treatment of apology as one act whose core is remorse and moral agency.",
    "risk": "Apology theorists may argue that the components are not separable, since a commitment without remorse is a mere promise. The analysis would then be a different act (an undertaking), not a partial apology."
  },
  {
    "thesis": "Apology is a norm-answering speech act, performed in response to a sanction already issued, and so it satisfies Butlin and Viebahn's sanctionability condition more readily than assertion does. On their own normative framework the ordering of difficulty for LLMs is inverted: chatbots are nearer to apologising than to asserting.",
    "move_type": "counterexample",
    "sketch": "Butlin and Viebahn say assertion requires that the speaker be capable of being sanctioned, and that models fail this. They treat sanction as a downstream target, something the speaker could face for violating a norm. Apology is structurally the reverse: it is triggered by an actual sanction (a complaint or rebuke) and expected to modify later conduct. RLHF and in-context feedback make user rebuke a causal input that systematically changes a model's later outputs, which is exactly the loop Butlin's 'sensitivity to instrumental value' describes. So the sanction channel is operative where apology is concerned, and not operative at assertion's norm of truth, because no one sanctions a pre-trained model for a false sentence. The key premise is that being sanctionable means being a system whose behaviour is reliably modulated by rebuke, and not being a subject of desert. Under that reading the normative route to denial is act-relative, and the sentence-level moral-agency argument of Magnus et al. is the only thing left standing against apology. That separates two denial strategies that have been treated as a single camp.",
    "target": "Butlin and Viebahn's sanctionability condition, and Skiba's extension of the norm-based argument to speech acts in general.",
    "risk": "Sanction in the relevant sense probably requires moral responsibility or desert, and not mere behavioural modulation. If so, a thermostat would be sanctionable and the reading trivialises the condition."
  }
]

[
  {
    "thesis": "Most chatbot 'apologies' are not failed or fraudulent moral apologies but a different act, a conversational repair move like 'oops' or 'pardon', so the moral-agency dispute is a verbal quarrel over which genre the lexeme 'sorry' belongs to.",
    "move_type": "reframing",
    "sketch": "Magnus et al. take the morally serious apology as the benchmark and ask what agency it needs. But the triggers for chatbot 'I apologize for the confusion' are almost never wrongs. They are friction: a user correction, a misparse, a refusal. In human talk this is the ordinary job of 'sorry' as a phatic or face-work token. It signals uptake of the correction, resets the exchange and keeps the interaction cooperative, and it carries no commitment to repair and no acknowledgment of wrongdoing. Such uses are standard English and not degenerate cases of apology. The premise the argument turns on is that the speech-act type is fixed by the discourse function a token serves in context, and not by the lexical item or its most morally weighty use. If so, the 'bullshit' charge and the 'it can apologise by proxy' reply both misdescribe the data. Both debate a thick act that was never in play, while the real question, whether a chatbot can perform repair moves, has a much easier and different answer. The moral-agency question only arises for the minority of cases where users take the token as a thick apology, and that is an uptake problem in the audience.",
    "target": "Magnus, Buccella and D'Cruz's choice of the morally serious apology as the paradigm, and by extension the shared assumption that 'apologising' names one act type for human and machine.",
